    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/subscribe?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK": {
      "title": "Subscribe",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Subscribe For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Subscribe Get notifications about new vacancies right in your mailbox. E-mail address * Keyword I consent to my personal data being handled as described in the policy Show privacy policy Correct the following errors before submitting:"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41423": {
      "title": "Associate Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Associate Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Associate Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/683 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, at the Sahlgrenska Academy, we conduct research and education covering a wide range of knowledge areas. The institute comprises five different departments: Clinical Neuroscience, Health and Rehabilitation, Pharmacology, Physiology and Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, invites applications for an Associate Researcher employment at the Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. At the Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry at the Sahlgrenska Academy, neurochemical research is focused on identifying and studying pathophysiology and pathologies in neurodegenerative diseases. We offer a dynamic and intellectually stimulating environment. Duties The Associate Researcher will oversee and contribute to the data management of the REAL AD study and other ongoing projects in the research group, from raw data capture through the preparation of cleaned analytical datasets. Moreover, assistance in monitoring participant adherence in real time using interactive data dashboards and proactively resolving any issues with devices/research tools and communicating updates to the principal investigators will be required. Liaising with lab staff and external partners to streamline sample tracking and maintain efficient data flow. Additionally, the research assistant will be asked to handle administrative tasks related to the activities of the REAL AD study and the research group in general. The successful candidate will also be encouraged to develop and contribute to research communication, manuscript preparation, and conference presentations. Qualifications Essential Criteria: To qualify as an Associate Researcher for this employment, the applicant should: Hold a relevant BSc or MSc degree in Medicine, Neuroscience, Biology, Psychology, Statistics or similar. Have strong skills in data science, managing multi-modal datasets. Have excellent written and spoken Swedish and English language skills. Desirable experience: Understanding of statistical modelling, biomarker data analyses and good clinical research practices. Proficiency in programming (e.g. R, Python, MATLAB, Bash). Previous experience with clinical research framework systems such as REDCap. Interest in coordination of research projects and participant communication. We value curiosity, initiative and a collaborative spirit. We are therefore seeking someone proactive and committed to supporting our research projects with the necessary tasks and contributing to supportive research environment. Personal qualities and overall suitability will therefore carry considerable weight in our selection. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 11 months, 100% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology. Starting date: as soon as possible or as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have questions regarding the employment please contact Professor Michael Schöll, phone: +46 705 727 471, e-mail: michael.scholl@neuro.gu.se Manager in charge is Professor/Head of Department Henrik Zetterberg, phone: +46 768 672 647, e-mail: henrik.zetterberg@clinchem.gu.se Application documents are not to be submitted through the contact persons. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-23 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41419": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/680 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, at the Sahlgrenska Academy, we conduct research and education covering a wide range of knowledge areas. The institute comprises five different departments: Clinical Neuroscience, Health and Rehabilitation, Pharmacology, Physiology and Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, invites applications for a Researcher employment at the Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. The Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry provides world leading translational research spanning cell and animal models to clinical studies and extensive epidemiological studies. Here, ground-breaking research in areas such as bipolar disorder, addiction medicine, aging, neurochemistry and psychiatry are conducted. The Wallenberg Centre for Molecular and Translational Medicine is a newly established research centre at the University of Gothenburg supporting molecular and translational medicine and is an effort to strengthen the life sciences in Western Sweden. In combination with the newly opened Imaging and Intervention Centre at the Sahlgrenska University Hospital, there will be excellent resources for advanced research projects in medical imaging. Duties A major task for the applicant will be to contribute to the ongoing research activities, conceptually as well as methodologically. In particular, the person is expected to take an active role in our imaging work, applying and developing advanced imaging analysis methods. Further, there are possibilities to initiate individual research projects, based on the person´s interests. The successful candidate should show a high level of responsibility and independency and be able to work in a team and be willing to supervise students. Moreover, the candidate is expected to have broad knowledge and expertise in aging research and dementia, image analysis as well as manuscript writing. Qualifications To qualify as a Researcher the applicant should hold a PhD in a relevant subject related to the project such as e.g., neuroimaging, statistics, and neuroscience. We seek for a person with experience in the field of neuroimaging in healthy aging and Alzheimer’s disease research. The candidate should be able to conduct advanced image data processing/analysis. Previous experience working with machine learning and advanced statistical methods is required. Experience with both healthy elderly and Alzheimer’s disease patients are highly valued. Social and collaborative skills are very important since the applicant will be working with other members of the research team and collaborators. Great importance will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 6 months, 100% of full-time, and is placed at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology. Starting date: as soon as possible or as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have questions regarding the employment please contact Professor Michael Schöll, phone: +46 705 727 471, e-mail: michael.scholl@neuro.gu.se Manager in charge is Professor/Head of Department Henrik Zetterberg, phone: +46 768 672 647, e-mail: henrik.zetterberg@clinchem.gu.se Application documents are not to be submitted through the contact persons. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please enclose PhD certificate with the application. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-23 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41358": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistent",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Teaching Assistent For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Teaching Assistent Reference number PAR 2026/666 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Clinical Sciences is one of six Institutes within Sahlgrenska Academy at the University of Gothenburg. We conduct research and education that puts people's health and quality of life in focus. We represent 16 different research areas and are divided into four sections.  Through a fruitful collaboration with the healthcare and Sahlgrenska University Hospital, we have developed joint projects and meeting places that benefits both health care and research We are currently seeking a teaching assistant to work in Peter Naredi’s research group at the Department of Surgery. Analysis of p38 MAP kinase inhibitors to improve cisplatin function : The Peter Naredi lab studies the action of the anti-cancer drug cisplatin with focus on the cell biology basis for its function and to identify signalling pathways that modulate its response in the nematode C.elegans. Cisplatin is a commonly used anti-cancer drug whose cytotoxic effects are regulated by intracellular signaling pathways. One of the pathways that has been identified is the p38 MAP Kinase pathway. Knockdown of the pathway leads to enhanced cisplatin sensitivity The p38/MAP kinase pathway has been identified as an important regulator of cisplatin response in both C. elegans and human tumor cells. Understanding how this pathway can be modulated may improve treatment strategies. Using a humanized p38MAP Kinase model, the lab has tested inhibitors with the aim to conduct translational research into making cisplatin more effective. Similar work is also conducted on another genetic pathway that modulates cisplatin response. Duties The teaching assistent will assist with ongoing research projects in the laboratory and contribute to experimental work. Duties will include: Performing drug treatment experiments and cytotoxicity assays. Conducting gene expression analyses using quantitative PCR (qPCR). Performing protein analyses, including Western blotting. Assisting with fluorescence microscopy experiments and image acquisition. Maintaining accurate and up-to-date laboratory records and documentation. Presenting experimental results and progress updates at research group meetings. Qualifications To be eligible for the position as a teaching assistant, you must be enrolled in a first-cycle (undergraduate) or second-cycle (master’s) programme. The cumulative duration of employment as an teaching assistent may not exceed three years. We are looking for a candidate with documented experience working with Caenorhabditis elegans (C. elegans) as a model system, including cultivation, genetic crossing schemes, and drug testing. The successful applicant should have practical experience with cytotoxicity assays and molecular biology techniques such as RNA and DNA extraction, RNA purification, qPCR, and protein analysis, including Western blotting. Experience with fluorescence microscopy for gene expression analysis is required. The position also requires the ability to work independently, in a structured and meticulous manner, and to follow established protocols and instructions. Good computer skills and excellent written and oral communication skills in English are essential. The following qualifications are considered meritorious: Experience with statistical analysis software, such as GraphPad Prism. Experience with image analysis software. Ability to read, critically evaluate, and present scientific literature. Additional experience with fluorescence microscopy and advanced molecular biology laboratory techniques. Employment The employment is limited (temporary) for 9 months, 20 % of full-time and is placed at the Institute of Clinical Sciences, the Department of Surgery. Starting date; On agreement. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Researcher Gautam Kao; phone: +46 730544845 or email: gautam.kao@gu.se. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please attach the following documents to your application: - CV - A cover letter describing why you are interested in the position and how you meet the qualifications - A description of any previous research experience (if applicable) Please also attach a certificate of enrolment/transcript from Ladok showing that you are enrolled as a student in higher education. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-22 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41340": {
      "title": "Researcher in Structural Biotechnology and Sustainable Agriculture",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher in Structural Biotechnology and Sustainable Agriculture For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher in Structural Biotechnology and Sustainable Agriculture Reference number PAR 2026/646 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Department of Conservation, professional preparatory education and research are conducted with a focus on cultural heritage issues, landscapes and cultural environments, buildings, gardens and objects. Collaboration between the department’s various areas of expertise, as well as with the surrounding national and international community, forms an important component of its activities. The subject of conservation brings together expertise within a broad field of knowledge that encompasses the natural sciences, humanities and social sciences. Research in Conservation has developed within a range of areas and themes. For more information about the Department, go to: Department of Conservation Duties Subject areas: Structural biology, biotechnology, microbial physiology, controlled-environment agriculture, sustainable food production A researcher position is available within the Department of Conservation at the University of Gothenburg to establish a new interdisciplinary research programme investigating structure-guided biological nutrient delivery for sustainable food production. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary research group working across microbial ecology, algal biotechnology, fungal biotechnology, hydroponics, aquaponics, microbial protein production. The structural biology programme will complement existing expertise in microbial cultivation, metabolomics, microbiome analysis, nutrient physiology and biotechnology. The position therefore provides a unique opportunity to apply advanced structural biology to commercially relevant challenges in sustainable food production while developing an independent research profile. We are seeking a candidate who is independent and self-motivated; details of the project can be adjusted based on a candidate’s skills and interests. The candidate will be expected to lead their project but i) develop and discuss their ideas with team members and the PI; ii) initiate and carry out their work within a collaborative environment; iii) write well-formulated reports and academic articles. The candidate will be working in a multidisciplinary team of aquatic science researchers, engineers and food scientists involving several international research groups, thus good team skills, effective communication in English and the ability to travel are required. The position will examine how microorganisms regulate micronutrient availability across microalgal cultivation, hydroponics, aquaponics, as well as microbial and fungal biomass production. The successful candidate will establish a research programme aimed at identifying the molecular mechanisms responsible for efficient micronutrient delivery for biotechnology applications. The work will integrate structural biology, microbial ecology, metabolomics, microbial biotechnology, and applied food production systems to develop next-generation nutrient delivery technologies for sustainable agriculture. The ultimate objective is to replace inefficient synthetic nutrient media formulations with biologically optimized targeted delivery systems to improve productivity across a wide range of commercial cultivation platforms. The successful candidate will connect molecular biotechnology with research in aquatic biotechnology, aquaculture, aquaponics, microalgal systems, microbial and fungal production, controlled-environment agriculture, nutrient recovery and circular food systems. The successful candidate will help establish micronutrient transport applications for nutrient delivery across multiple platforms, thus providing the structural foundation for next-generation sustainable food production technologies. The role combines scientific expertise with responsibility for planning, coordinating, and driving multidisciplinary research projects from concept to delivery. This includes developing and structuring research projects by translating scientific ideas into clear objectives, work plans, priorities, milestones, and experimental strategies, while coordinating multiple parallel activities to ensure that experimental, computational, analytical, and collaborative workstreams remain aligned across objectives. The role also includes setting research priorities and coordinating project direction, adapting plans in response to scientific results, identifying emerging opportunities and technical challenges, as well as available resources. It requires coordination and management of multidisciplinary teams. A key component of this position is the interface between academic research and industry, including the coordination of externally funded and collaborative projects requiring translation of research findings towards practical and commercially relevant applications. This includes aligning scientific objectives with partner needs, communicating across academic and industrial environments, and supporting the implementation of novel biotechnology applications from research results. Responsibilities include planning and coordinating complex research campaigns, monitoring project progress and adjusting research plans where necessary to maintain progress and delivery. The role further involves project management, including supporting other researchers in planning and executing their work, and contributing to strategic directions. The position requires high-level communication skills to convey results to collaborators, research partners, funding bodies, and industry stakeholders, as well as driving project outputs from research activities and data generation. Expected outputs include research publications, presentations, preparing communication and dissemination materials as well as patent applications. The candidate will work closely with the PI and senior researchers, but will have substantial scope to shape the scientific direction of the molecular biotechnology programme. Activities include: contributing to the scientific direction and development of the molecular biotechnology programme. planning and management within multidisciplinary funded research projects. developing research plans with regards to milestones, priorities, and experimental strategies. coordinating academic–industry collaborations and translating research into practical biotechnology applications. identifying funding opportunities and contributing to grant development and applications. communicating project progress and results to research partners, funders, and industry. driving research outputs, including publications, presentations, technology development, and new project opportunities. Qualifications The successful candidate should hold a PhD in structural biology, biochemistry, molecular biology, biophysics or a closely related discipline together with demonstrated postdoctoral experience in structure–function relationships of biologically important proteins and a proven leadership driving projects across academia and industry. The successful candidate should demonstrate expertise in several of the following areas: extensive experience in structural characterization of proteins using modern structural biology techniques; demonstrated expertise in protein–ligand interactions and molecular recognition, particularly where ligand binding induces structural or functional changes in proteins; experience investigating protein conformational dynamics using experimental or computational structural approaches; expertise in computational structural biology, including AlphaFold-based protein prediction, molecular docking, molecular dynamics and structure-based functional interpretation; experience integrating complementary structural biology techniques including cryo-electron microscopy or related methods; demonstrated ability to identify biologically relevant ligand-binding sites and relate structural observations to molecular function; experience interpreting structural biology in the context of broader biological systems rather than isolated protein structures; peer-reviewed publication record demonstrating independence and excellence in structural biology; experience working in multidisciplinary and international research projects, including strategic research development and experimental campaigns at major international research infrastructures; grant development and proposal writing experience; research planning, prioritization, and resource coordination; scientific communication, reporting, and dissemination both to a scientific audience and to the general public; cross-functional problem solving and project delivery; experience working across academia, industry, and international research infrastructures; as this project involves management of interdisciplinary research teams, strong interpersonal skills as well as academic excellence are required. The candidate should be collaborative, adaptable, and comfortable working in dynamic, international environments, with experience engaging with diverse scientific networks. Strong communication and teamwork skills are essential, particularly in fostering collaboration across disciplines and building effective connections between academic research and industry. Additional desirable qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with experience in one or more of the following: experience with metalloproteins or iron-binding proteins; strong interdisciplinary knowledge of biological and ecological sciences, including plant physiology, molecular biology, zoology, microbiology and ecology; familiarity with microbial physiology or microbial metabolites; interest in translating molecular discoveries into biotechnology applications; experience collaborating across microbiology, plant biology or industrial biotechnology; experience with microbial ecology, hydroponics, aquaponics or algal cultivation is not specifically required, but would be considered an asset to the position; peer-reviewed publication record demonstrating excellence in structural biology or molecular biotechnology. Employment Type of employment: Permanent employment, full-time Institution: Department of Conservation, University of Gothenburg, Sweden First day of employment: Nov 1, 2026 Contact information for the post For more information, please contact Dr. Alyssa Joyce alyssa.joyce@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application must include: A CV and cover letter that describes how the applicant meets the selection criteria Evidence of strong academic writing skills through supporting links to first author peer-reviewed manuscripts, any reports or project/grant proposals written If not included on the CV, name and contact details of three referees attached in a file entitled References ‘your name’.pdf If the following items are not evident on the CV, please state clearly in the cover letter (a) your willingness to travel internationally for research periods in collaborating labs (b) evidence of teamwork (c) competence with specific statistical software. Applications must be received by: 23 September 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41429": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/682 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 340 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. Salivary gland tumors are a heterogeneous group of tumors originating in the salivary glands of the head and neck region. The prognosis for patients with malignant salivary gland tumors is typically very poor, as there are no effective treatments for recurrent or metastatic disease. The treatments currently used (surgery and radiation therapy) are also often disfiguring from both a functional and aesthetic perspective. The research group has extensive experience studying salivary gland tumors and has identified several driving genomic alterations in these tumors. Duties We are looking for a researcher to help develop a new targeted treatment for salivary gland tumors. As part of the project, the researcher will plan, design, and conduct research that includes, among other things, the culture of primary/short-term-cultured tumor cells in 2D and 3D models, drug treatment/screening, proliferation/apoptosis/spheroid assays, quantitative PCR, RNA-seq, and immunoblot analysis. The duties also include project management, supervision, and written and oral reporting of research data. Qualifications A Ph.D. in a relevant field (e.g., biomedicine or molecular biology) is required. Postdoctoral research experience in the field is preferred. Previous experience with cell-based assays, in vitro drug treatment, and the culture of salivary gland tumor cells is highly preferred. The position requires a high degree of precision, independence, and initiative. Applicants should also possess strong collaborative skills and a problem-solving mindset. We will place great emphasis on personal fit. Excellent oral and written communication skills in English are required, as we work in an international environment. Employment This is a full-time, fixed-term position for 11 months, based at the Institute of Biomedicine, Sahlgrenska Center for Cancer Research. The start date is to be agreed upon. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Mattias Andersson, Associate professor/Researcher, mattias.andersson@llcr.med.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: A cover letter with a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying A CV including a list of publications and other qualifications relevant to the position Proof of completed PhD Contact details of two references Applications must be received by: 2026-09-23 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41010": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design Reference number PAR 2026/562 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The  Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and  Academy of Music and Drama.  As a combined faculty of art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work to create a more sustainable and open society. HDK-Valand is now looking for an Assistant Professor in Design, specialised in Business and Design, who will conduct research and teaching at the intersection between design studies and business studies. You will be part of the Design unit , attached to the Business and Design Lab (BDL) . The Business and Design Lab (BDL) is a research centre that since 2010 builds on a collaboration between HDK-Valand and the School of Business, Economics and Law at the University of Gothenburg. Pioneering the intersection between design studies and management and organization studies, BDL offers an inter- and transdisciplinary platform for a critical and grounded investigation of the relations between design & art, technology and the economy. Located between two faculties, the centre has developed a symmetrical dialogue between the two domains of scholarship to study both the role of design and art in organizations and the ways in which design and art work are organized and valued. With a particular interest for methodological developments based on design and artistic methods, the centre has led a large range of research projects nationally and internationally and is seeking to consolidate its next phase of development. The centre involves junior and senior researchers and is led by Elena Raviola, who holds the Torsten and Wanja Söderberg Professorship in Design Management. HDK-Valand’s research environment values a broad and inclusive approach whereby knowledge is produced within established research formats, experimental artistic practices and education. Our research contributes to discourses on environmental, societal and cultural challenges through engagement in interdisciplinary projects. Four research themes oriented around politics, the environment, economics and materiality operate as focus areas to facilitate collaboration between different subjects and actors, and to provide a broad base for both research and teaching. An overarching objective at HDK-Valand is the development of a complete academic environment grounded in these themes More about HDK-Valand here: https://www.gu.se/en/hdk-valand More about BDL here: https://www.gu.se/en/bdl Subject area Design, specialised in Business and Design Subject area description The subject area of this position is Design specialised in Business and Design as the interdisciplinary intersection of design research and management and organization studies. At this intersection between designing and organizing, research investigates design as an organizational, managerial and societal practice as well as organizations, organizing processes and other forms of economic activities as objects of design themselves. A particular area of focus is how the design of material artefacts, such as physical tools, digital technologies, and spatial architecture, is shaped by and shapes organizations and markets in different ways, including issues related to professional work, judgment, learning, leadership, accounting, innovation, strategy formulation, and other organizational phenomena, and ultimately how design contributes to the creation and transformation of organizations, markets, institutions and societal futures. It is central to the subject area of Business and Design at HDK-Valand to engage with design and artistic methodologies, such as participatory design, collaborative design, speculative design, prototyping, games, and related approaches, and to contribute to the development of innovative methodological approaches for creating new forms of engagement with researchers, practitioners, and the wider public around shared societal challenges. Research within the area may include research on the cultural sector, but may also address other sectors and phenomena depending on the candidate’s research interests. Particular priority will be given to research that, at the intersection of these fields, contributes to understand how democracy and the democratic public sphere are being reshaped through design-based and artistic methods. Duties The candidate will conduct research and teaching, with approximately 50% of the position devoted to research and 50% to teaching. Research responsibilities include to: develop and carry out interdisciplinary research projects in collaboration with other researchers nationally and internationally; publish articles and other scholarly texts in international journals and with international publishers; conduct assigned research tasks under the supervision of Prof. Elena Raviola and contribute to the national and international strategic and operational development of the Business and Design Lab, including the organization of seminar series and other meetings, the development of collaborations with local actors, and participation in larger research bid; apply for own research projects to Swedish, Nordic and European funders; participate in existing research projects by conducting research work and assisting with project management and coordination; engage with societal partners and cultural institutions in research collaborations; develop and communicate research findings in public facing activities. Teaching will take place at the undergraduate, master and doctoral levels in relevant courses and programs in design and other teaching areas of the Academy and will include to: develop existing and new courses; lead course coordination and administration; supervise student projects and theses; teach in classroom and digital formats. R esponsibilities and tasks may change over time. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates R equirements are: a familiarity with the research debates and communities both in design and in management and organization studies demonstrated in the application and/or supporting documentation experience with ethnographic approaches and artistic methods in research work , demonstrated in the application and/or supporting documentation . the ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Specific assessment criteria for this position are: the demonstrated level of engagement with and potential for intellectual contributions to the research communities in design and in management and organization studies; the demonstrated capacity of methodological innovation in ethnography and artistic methods; the demonstrated level of familiarity with science and technology studies, especially as informing design research and organization studies. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. As the engagement with innovative design and artistic methods might materialize in other outputs than publications, research expertise is also demonstrated through other research outputs, such as design prototypes, public exhibitions, curated projects and participation in internationally reputable design initiatives. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, practice-based workshops, studio-based learning and supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled, upon application, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent post as a senior lecturer in the same subject area as that of the Assistant Professor post. Promotion requires that the applicant is qualified and suitable for appointment as a senior lecturer within the subject area, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg’s Regulations on the Appointment of Teaching Staff (Ref. No. GU 2024/3252). In addition, the applicant must have been appointed as an Associate Professor (in S wedish: docent) at the Artistic Faculty. The eligibility requirements for the position of Associate Professor ( docent ) are set out in the document accessible via this link (Ref. No. V 2014/885) Anvisningar docent Furthermore, the assessment will evaluate whether you have demonstrated proficiency in Swedish at least at level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated either by passing a language test, the requirements for which are described here common-european-framework-reference-language-skills , or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg. If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during early December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Prof. Elena Raviola elena.raviola@gu.se . If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR at HDK-Valand: hr@hdk-valand.gu.se . Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Publications that cannot be attached to the application online should be sent to: HDK-Valand Box 131 405 30 and must be received by the deadline. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=40911": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Political Science, one or more",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Political Science, one or more For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Political Science, one or more Reference number PAR 2026/538 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Political Science is an international academic environment that welcomes students, researchers and staff from all over the world. Our research covers topics such as political institutions, elections, public opinion and democracy, international and European politics, environmental policy, corruption and political ideas. The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg is ranked as the leading department in Sweden and among the top 10–15 in Europe in its subject area, according to the 2024 Shanghai Ranking of Academic Subjects. We organise a large number of research seminars and workshops each year within the department’s various research areas. We offer courses at all levels in both Swedish and English. In total, around 1,400 people study with us, and we have approximately 160 staff members. For more information about the Department, visit our website The Department of Political Science, University of Gothenburg Subject area Political Science Subject area description Political Science is the study of politics. Duties The role involves research amounting to at least 50 per cent of a full-time position. The Assistant Professor is free to choose research topics, develop theoretical ideas, collect data and select research methods. The role includes actively seeking external funding for their own research and actively participating in the department’s research environments and seminar activities. Active participation in the international research community is also expected, through attendance at conferences and international publication. The duties also include teaching, course coordination, supervision and assessment in undergraduate and postgraduate courses, and possibly also in postgraduate education, corresponding to at least 30 per cent of a full-time position. Teaching on most programmes and courses at the department may be required. The duties also include course and programme development, as well as participation in the department’s educational development work. The Department of Political Science is actively involved in public debate, and its responsibilities include initiating and actively engaging in collaboration with the wider community and promoting the practical application of research findings. Management duties, teaching duties and other duties within the departmental, faculty and university collegial forums may, in the long term, form part of the role. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in the subject area of the position or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of research expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Political Science, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. In order to assess whether an applicant is sufficiently qualified for promotion, an assessment is carried out in accordance with the criteria for appointment as a senior lecturer applicable at the University of Gothenburg, in accordance with the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teaching Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103) . In such an assessment, the following requirements for promotion also apply: Scientific competence must be demonstrated through high-quality scientific publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. Publication in peer-reviewed books or book chapters is considered an asset. The applicant must also have secured external research grants through national or international competitive processes, taken on responsibilities within the academic community through, for example, review assignments, acting as a faculty examiner or serving on an examination board, and demonstrated progression in relation to their qualifications at the start of their appointment, for example by attaining the qualification of docent. Pedagogical competence must be demonstrated through at least 1,600 hours of teaching, experience of course development and various teaching methods, as well as a passed university pedagogy course equivalent to PIL101–PIL103, and supervisor training equivalent to PIL201, or a decision by the Dean regarding validation. The applicant must also have demonstrated pedagogical progression, for example through teaching at different levels, within different subject areas, or through specialisation in previous areas. Achievements in research and teaching carry the most weight in promotion decisions and are assessed equally. The applicant must also have demonstrated a capacity for collaboration and leadership by organising and participating in outreach scientific activities and activities in collaboration with the wider community, shown an ability to lead and organise scientific projects, teaching or other activities, and taken collegial responsibility for the department, for example through participation in assessment or working groups. With regard to the assessment criterion of suitability, consideration is given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to carry out the duties included in the post, and the ability to collaborate and contribute to the development of the organisation. Applicants must also meet the Swedish language requirement of at least level B2 according to the CEFR, as demonstrated by a passed language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. The interviews are scheduled to take place between 17 December 2026 and 8 January 2027. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Lena Wängnerud, Head of the Department, lena.wangnerud@pol.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Helena Jensen, HR Officer, helena.jensen@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here . Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. We ask that you submit your application in English, as it will be assessed by external experts whose working language is English. Please attach the following to your application: Résumé/CV, including complete list of publications and a list of external research grants obtained (with amounts). A description of your research qualifications containing: a) A list of your top five scholarly publications. Please indicate your role and the scope of your contribution for publications co-authored with others. Co-author certificates must be attached; they must contain information about each author's contributions (quantitatively and in substance) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but not yet published, a certificate must be attached proving that the article or book has been accepted for publication. b) A brief account of your research qualifications, your research profile, and your research plans for the coming years. (Max. 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of your top five scholarly publications. A description of your teaching qualifications containing : a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught (including courses and programmes at first-, second- and third-cycle levels b) A brief account of your teaching qualifications with information on scope and quality, including a self-reflection on teaching and learning in higher education. (Max. 2 pages.) c) Evidence (certificates of completion) of completed courses in teaching and learning in higher education. d) Certification of your teaching experience from the head of department, director of studies or equivalent at a minimum of one university/department where you have taught. Electronic copies of teaching materials such as course syllabuses, reading lists, examination tasks, lecture PowerPoint slides, and instructions for students (maximum 3 documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your educational achievements and teaching qualifications (4b). PhD Diploma Other certificates and documents that you wish to cite. Scholarly publications, articles, CVs, and the like that are not available in electronic form should be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Political Science Attention: Helena Jensen Box 711 SE-405 30 Göteborg Sweden Applications must be received by: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41313": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Linguistics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Linguistics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Linguistics Reference number PAR 2026/547 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. At the Department of Philosophy, Linguistics, and Theory of Science (FLoV) within the Faculty of Humanities, we have around 120 employees and a teaching volume that includes around 350 full-year students. We are engaged in numerous large international and interdisciplinary research projects in areas such as theoretical and practical philosophy, logic, linguistics, computational linguistics, language technology, and theory of science. Our research has many applications and collaborations with other scientific fields, including biology, computer science, economics, history of ideas, law, medicine, psychology, religion, political science, and engineering. We educate philosophers, logicians, linguists, language technologists, theorists of science, historians of philosophy, and teachers of philosophy. The department also conducts doctoral education and development work within its various academic domains. If you are interested in working in an international, dynamic and collaborative environment, FLoV offers an inspiring setting for research and education. Read more about us here. As an Assistant Professor at the University of Gothenburg, research of at least 50% and professional development are part of the position. Subject area Linguistics. Link to subject page here. Subject area description Linguistic interaction is the overarching research profile for linguistics at FLoV. The profile covers the study of language as a structured system, a social-cognitive capacity, and a situated form of action. It therefore includes both formal and/or computational modelling of the systems of conventions that constitute individual languages and studies of the social practices and psychosocial mechanisms that enable language use. Research in the subject includes psycholinguistic, sociolinguistic, and typological perspectives. In psycholinguistics, language is studied in relation to perception, language processing, language acquisition, and production. In sociolinguistics and linguistic typology, linguistic variation and diversity are investigated within and across languages, language families, and social groups. The subject also engages with broader philosophical and societal questions concerning meaning, interpretation, normativity, categorisation, and the conditions for linguistic understanding. Such questions are particularly significant in contexts where language is used and circulates through institutional, technological, and public settings, thereby shaping how knowledge, action, and shared understanding are organised. In this way, the social and societal conditions of language also become central, including how communicative practices are shaped by technological infrastructures, social conventions, and changing public contexts. Taken together, linguistics at FLoV combines theoretical, empirical, and computational approaches to language. The subject provides a foundation for investigating linguistic structure, interaction, cognition, diversity, and meaning, while also contributing to the analysis of the relationship between language, technology, communicative practices, and the societal contexts in which language is used and has an impact. Duties The position is a career-development position with an emphasis on research and scholarly development in linguistics. Job assignments consist of research, teaching, and administration. Research: As an Assistant Professor in linguistics, you are expected to conduct and publish high-quality research with international relevance within your area of expertise. The position is intended to support your development of independence as a researcher and the establishment of your own research profile within the subject area. You are also expected to develop applications for external research funding, nationally and internationally, build research networks, collaborate with researchers both at and outside the department, and contribute actively to the subject’s research environment, including participation in the subject’s research seminar. Teaching: You are expected to contribute to teaching at first- and second-cycle level in linguistics. Teaching may include central areas such as phonetics/phonology, syntax, semantics, pragmatics, psycholinguistics, and sociolinguistics, as well as teaching in programmes where linguistics is included, such as the Master in Language Technology programme and the Master’s Programme for Communication Officers in the Public Sector. Courses are taught in either Swedish or English. Applicants who are not proficient in Swedish at the start of their employment are expected to gradually develop the language skills necessary to contribute to teaching in Swedish, in accordance with the language proficiency level specified under the eligibility requirements. Teaching duties include lectures, seminars, assessment, supervision, and teaching administration. You are also expected, to an extent appropriate for a career-development position, to contribute to course and programme development within the subject area. Administration: The position may also include other tasks, such as administrative duties and development work, where you, as an assistant professor, are expected to actively participate in the department's development and work environment efforts, including workplace meetings and other internal meetings and conferences. Most of the work will be carried out on site at the University of Gothenburg and you are therefore expected to be physically present at the department. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance (link) and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Found here (only in Swedish), under Personal-Handläggningsordning . Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in linguistics or has the corresponding research competence with clear relevance to the subject area of the position. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. The ability to teach in Swedish is meritorious. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. The person appointed as assistant professor is expected to acquire proficiency in written and spoken Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to CEFR within two years of the start of employment. Qualifications for teaching in higher education in accordance with requirements at the University of Gothenburg (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103) must be validated or completed within one year of the start of the employment, more information found here. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area linguistics. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. It is particularly meritorious to be able to teach in central areas of linguistics, such as phonetics/phonology, syntax, semantics, pragmatics, psycholinguistics, or sociolinguistics, and to contribute to teaching in programmes and courses where linguistics intersects with language technology, communication, or other related areas. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. In the overall assessment, particular emphasis will be placed on the applicant’s research expertise, development potential, and ability to establish an independent research profile in linguistics with relevance to the subject’s profile in linguistic interaction. The assessment will be based on documented qualifications, ongoing work, and the description of scholarly work and pedagogical work submitted by the applicant. In this description, the applicant should explain how their research relates to the subject’s overarching profile in linguistic interaction. Weight will also be given to documented interpersonal skills, the ability to contribute to a good academic environment, the ability to plan and organise research, and the ability to communicate research findings within and beyond academia. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Philosophy, Linguistics and Theory of Science, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. For promotion to senior lecturer, the assessment is based on demonstrated progression in terms of scientific and pedagogical skills. Eligibility requirements and rules regarding promotion to Senior Lecturer are described in Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg, linked above and in the Faculty of Humanities “Instructions for Applicants in connection with Promotions to and Appointments of Academically Qualified Teaching Staff other than Professors”, see link. As an assistant professor at the University of Gothenburg, you may apply for promotion to Associate Professor (docent). For more information, please contact the department's HR officer, Jennifer Stråle (contact details below). Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg Selection process When appointing an Assistant Professor, an assessment of all applications must be obtained from at least one external expert that is appointed after the application period has expired. The amount of time given to the external expert for their assessment depends on the number of applications received. The expert assessment is reviewed by the faculty's Teacher Proposal Committee, and then the department will convene interviews and trial lectures. Once the recruitment group has drafted a proposal for employment, it is also reviewed by the committee. The Teacher Proposal Committee meets once a month, which can result in a relatively long recruitment process for this type of position. See the process step by step below: Publication period Appoint external expert Expert review Teacher proposal committee I Interviews and trial lectures Teacher proposal committee II Employment decision Interviews and trial lectures are planned to take place via Zoom between 7-11 December. More information regarding the University’s recruitment process you will find here. Contact information For questions regarding the position, subject, responsibilities, or the workplace, please contact Alexander Almér, Head of Department, alexander.almer@gu.se. For questions regarding the application, recruitment process, or employment conditions, please contact HR Officer Jennifer Stråle, jennifer.strale@gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s vacancies website, here , select the advert for this position, and follow the link marked ‘Apply’ at the bottom of the page. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should be compiled in accordance with the instructions for qualification portfolios at the Faculty of Humanities, see link. Please note that the following documents should be attached to the application: Personal letter, maximum 500 words (1 page) List of degrees and employment A description of scholarly work and pedagogical work Doctoral degree certificate Doctoral thesis The full text of a maximum of 10 scientific articles that the applicant particularly wishes to cite, and a separate list of these documents Academic Portfolio I: Scientific qualifications Academic Portfolio II: Teaching qualifications Academic Portfolio III: Management and administrative experience Academic Portfolio IV: Experience in cooperation with the surrounding community Any study materials that the applicant wishes to include Appendices (optional) Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. References for candidates in the final selection may be requested. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41350": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Health Promotion",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Health Promotion For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Health Promotion Reference number PAR 2026/526 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in Health Promotion, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas—subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in Health Promotion will be based at the Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science. Subject area Health Promotion Subject area description Health promotion is concerned with understanding how health and well-being can be promoted. Research in this field addresses a broad range of issues related to health promotion practice. It is interdisciplinary and encompasses a variety of theoretical approaches as well as both qualitative and quantitative methods. An important aim is to generate knowledge and expertise to support the development, implementation, and evaluation of health-promoting interventions. The research focuses primarily on food and physical activity. The health promotion research environment at the department also shares a strong commitment to exploring how participation and empowerment can be promoted and strengthened. Duties You will also be part of the interdisciplinary research environment in health promotion at the Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science. The work addresses societal challenges related to pupils´ dietary habits, physical activity, learning, and well-being, and aims to develop, test, and evaluate didactic models and educational programmes that can promote healthy and sustainable lifestyles. The Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science develops knowledge for a thriving world through food, nutrition, and sport. Our mission is to conduct world-class education and research in food and nutrition science and sport science. Research at the Department is characterised by a strong connection to education, interdisciplinary collaboration, and close engagement with wider society. The main focus of the Department’s research is to develop and disseminate scientific knowledge and professional expertise that can contribute to the effective promotion of sustainable habits relating to sport, physical activity, food, and meals. The Assistant Professor is expected to contribute to research and the development of teaching in close collaboration with schools and teachers, with a particular focus on integrating subjects such as Home and Consumer Studies, Physical Education and Health, and other school subjects. The aim is to develop, test, and evaluate teaching models that promote students’ learning, well-being, and sustainable lifestyles. The position offers excellent opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration within the educational science research environments at the University of Gothenburg. As an Assistant Professor, you are expected to: develop an independent research profile in health-promoting and interdisciplinary teaching in schools, with a focus on students’ dietary habits, physical activity, learning, and well-being; apply for external research funding to support research projects and doctoral students; publish scientific work in collaboration with senior researchers in food and nutrition science and/or sport science; contribute to the academic environment of the Department and the research field; plan, deliver, and evaluate teaching within relevant parts of the Department’s educational programmes. In addition to teaching within the Faculty’s teacher education programmes, you will teach in one or more of the Department’s bachelor’s programmes. Teaching in other programmes, courses, or continuing professional development activities may also be included. Teaching duties may also involve supervising students at different levels (first-cycle, second-cycle, and doctoral level) and examining degree projects. Course leadership and administrative duties are also included in the position. Teaching within the department is conducted primarily on campus and predominantly in Swedish, although teaching in English also occurs. Applicants are therefore expected to possess excellent oral and written communication skills in English and Swedish, or another Scandinavian language. Staff members are also expected to maintain a substantial on-campus presence in order to contribute to a vibrant and stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking applicants with a demonstrated ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networks For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities and suitability required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer (Universitetslektor) An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Applicants’ eligibility will be assessed after the application deadline. The documents of applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will then be sent to external experts for assessment. In addition to external expert assessment and interviews, the selection process may include trial lectures and reference checks. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December 2026. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Professor Frode Slinde Head of Department Tel: +46 (0)31 786 4220 Email: frode.slinde@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Anneli Svensson HR Officer Tel: +46 (0)31 786 2466 Email: anneli.svensson@gu.se . Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previous teaching within the subject area, showing how teaching hours have been distributed over time and across different courses, as well as qualitative documentation (such as descriptions of development work and course materials) that illustrates and provides evidence of the applicant’s pedagogical expertise. The list of publications must be structured so that peer-reviewed journal articles, peer-reviewed book chapters, and peer-reviewed books are presented under separate headings. Degree certificates, transcripts, and other certificates must be submitted together as a single PDF file. Publications or other documents that cannot be submitted electronically must be sent in duplicate to: University of Gothenburg Department of Food and Nutrition, and Sport Science Attn: Anneli Svensson Box 300 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Please ensure that the reference number PAR 2026/526 is clearly stated on the package. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41060": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Journalism with a specialization in news media",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Journalism with a specialization in news media For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Journalism with a specialization in news media Reference number PAR 2026/574 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professor with an international background at the end of 2026. The university’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. This position is based at the Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) within the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Gothenburg. It is one of the leading research environments in the Nordic countries in the field of media and communication studies, with particular strengths in areas such as political communication, news media, and journalism. Research Areas at JMG Subject area Journalism with a specialization in news media. Subject area description Journalism Studies is a social science discipline that examines the role, content, conditions, and significance of journalism in society. The discipline explores journalistic content; how news and public information are produced, distributed, interpreted, and used; and how journalism and its content are shaped by, and in turn shape, democratic, social, cultural, economic, political, and technological change. In line with the profile of journalism research at JMG, this position focuses on news media and journalism, with particular emphasis on their role and significance in society. The successful candidate is expected to conduct research on news media and news journalism, including their institutional conditions, production processes, content, audiences, and/or effects. The position also requires the application of relevant quantitative and qualitative research methods to the study of these phenomena. It could apply to any of ongoing research themes at JMG, for example focusing on the role of news media in processes of democratic erosion, political news coverage, or changes in patterns of media use. Duties The duties associated with this position include research, teaching, and engagement with the wider community. The position includes an entitlement to research time corresponding to at least 60% of the appointment. The successful candidate is expected to conduct research and actively seek external funding for their own research. They are also expected to participate actively in and contribute to the department’s research environment and seminar activities, as well as to engage with the international research community through participation in conferences and publication in international scholarly outlets. Teaching responsibilities include courses at the undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral levels. Teaching and assessment are conducted in either Swedish or English, depending on the courses involved. The duties also include pedagogical development and the administrative tasks associated with an academic position. Teaching at the Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) is campus-based, and staff members are expected to be present on campus in order to contribute to a vibrant and stimulating academic environment. JMG collaborates with a wide range of external partners, and the position includes participation in the department’s external engagement activities. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as an Assistant Professor, applicants must hold a doctoral degree in the subject area of the position or possess equivalent research expertise. Preference will be given to applicants who have obtained a doctoral degree, or achieved equivalent competence, in Journalism, Media and Communication, or in a closely related discipline with a clear specialisation in Journalism, Media and Communication. In accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance, priority will be given to applicants who have obtained their doctoral degree, or achieved equivalent competence, no more than seven years before the closing date for applications. Circumstances that constitute valid grounds for extending this period are specified in the Higher Education Ordinance. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise must be demonstrated through independent research characterized by a high degree of ability to formulate research problems, theoretical and methodological awareness, and analytical skill. Research expertise is further demonstrated through scholarly publications and ongoing participation in national and/or international research collaborations. In the assessment of research expertise, particular weight will be given to documented research accomplishments in news media and journalism (see above). Documented experience in the use of mixed methods, combining quantitative and qualitative approaches, is considered meritorious. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is further placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Consideration will also be given to how well the applicant’s research relates to and complements the research and research groups already existing within the department. Research at JMG Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Completion of further training in higher education pedagogy is considered meritorious. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of journalism, media and communication at the University of Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor has the right to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Associate Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that the individual meets the eligibility requirements for such a position and is also deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established assessment criteria for promotion to the position of Associate Senior Lecturer. To determine whether the applicant has achieved sufficient qualifications for promotion, an assessment will be conducted in accordance with the assessment criteria for employment as an Associate Senior Lecturer applicable at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). In addition, the following promotion requirements apply: Research expertise must be demonstrated through high-quality scholarly publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. Publications in peer-reviewed books or book chapters are considered meritorious. The applicant must also have actively applied for external research funding in national and international competitive calls. Success in obtaining such funding carries considerable weight in the assessment. Furthermore, the applicant must have contributed to the scholarly community through activities such as serving as a peer reviewer, acting as an external examiner (faculty opponent) at a doctoral thesis defence, or serving as a member of a doctoral examination committee. The applicant must also demonstrate progression in relation to the qualifications held at the time of appointment, for example by having attained the qualifications required for appointment as a docent. Teaching expertise must be demonstrated through at least active participation in teaching, experience of course development and different teaching methods, completion of qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education equivalent to PIL101–PIL103, and completion of supervisor training equivalent to PIL201, or a decision by the Dean recognising equivalent qualifications. In assessing an application for promotion, primary emphasis is placed on research qualifications and secondary emphasis on teaching qualifications. The applicant must also have demonstrated collaboration skills and leadership ability by, for example, participating in collaboration activities with the wider community, demonstrating the ability to lead and organise research projects or other activities, and assuming collegial responsibility within the department, for example through participation in assessment panels or working groups. With regard to the assessment criterion of suitability, consideration will be given to sound professional judgement, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department's activities. The applicant must also fulfil the Swedish language requirement at a minimum of CEFR level B2. Compliance with this requirement must be demonstrated either by passing an approved language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department confirming that a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be promoted to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/employment-benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during December 2026 and the beginning of January 2027. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Marie Grusell, Head of Department marie.grusell@jmg.gu.se or Jesper Strömbäck, Assisant Head of Department jesper.stromback@jmg.gu.se. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Sofia Hallsten, HR-officer, sofia.hallsten@jmg.gu.se Trade Union Representatives Information about trade union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. . Application To apply for this position, please submit your application through the University of Gothenburg's online recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure that the application is complete, in accordance with the instructions in this advertisement, and submitted to the University no later than the closing date. As the applications will be assessed by external experts whose working language is English, applicants are requested to submit all application documents in English. The application must include: Curriculum vitae/CV , including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding received (including amounts). Research plan. The plan must be clearly linked to the research area of the position and should be suitable for development into at least one competitive research grant application. A description of your research qualifications including: a) A list of your five most significant research publications. State your role and the extent of your contribution to publications co-authored with others. Co-author statements must be attached; these must include information on each author’s contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming its acceptance for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research merits, research profile and research plans for the next few years. (Max. 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most significant research publications. A description of your teaching qualifications including: a) a list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught (including courses and programmes at first-cycle, second-cycle and third-cycle level). b) A brief account of your teaching merits and qualifications, providing information on both scope and quality, including a reflection on your own teaching practice. (Max. 2 pages.) c) documentation of completed training in teaching and learning in higher education. d) Certification of your teaching experience from the Head of Department, Director of Studies or equivalent at at least one university/department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions for students (max. 3 supporting documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your teaching merits and qualifications. Doctoral degree certificate Other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Research publications, other written works, CVs and similar documents that are not available in electronic form should be sent to: Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) Attn: Sofia Hallsten Box 710 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Closing date for applications: 30 October 2026 The University of Gothenburg is committed to promoting equal opportunities, equality, and diversity, and values the contributions that a diverse workforce brings to the organisation. The University applies individual salary setting. In accordance with the regulations of the Swedish National Archives, the University is required to retain application documents for two years following the appointment decision. If an applicant specifically requests the return of their application documents, they will be returned after the two-year retention period has expired. Otherwise, the documents will be disposed of in accordance with the applicable regulations. To recruitment agencies and advertising sales representatives: The University of Gothenburg has contracted recruitment advertising services and therefore respectfully declines all direct contact from recruitment and staffing agencies, as well as from advertising sales representatives. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41059": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social media",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social m For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social media Reference number PAR 2026/490 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professor with an international background at the end of 2026. The university’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. This position is based at the Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) within the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Gothenburg. It is one of the leading research environments in the Nordic countries in the field of media and communication studies, with particular strengths in areas such as political communication, news media, and journalism. Subject area Media and Communication Studies with a specialization in digital and social media. Subject area description Media and Communication Studies is a broad and interdisciplinary field that examines the significance of media and communication in society. The field explores how communication is created, disseminated, interpreted, and influences individuals, organizations, and societies, as well as how it is shaped by social, cultural, political, economic, and technological conditions. Research within the field may encompass everything from everyday communication to crisis communication, political communication, strategic communication, and journalism, as well as media such as traditional mass media, social media, alternative media, and digital platforms. The specialization of this position is in digital and social media, with a focus on their societal role and significance. This entails the study of the internet, digital media, social media, and digital communication, as well as the application of relevant quantitative and qualitative methods—including data-driven and computational methods—to investigate the production, dissemination, and consumption of digital media and digital information. It could apply to any of the ongoing research themes at JMG, for example focusing on online misogyni, the role of digital media in breeding conspiracy thinking, or the role of digital media in processes of democratic erosion. Research Areas at JMG. Duties The duties associated with this position include research, teaching, and engagement with the wider community. The position includes an entitlement to research time corresponding to at least 60% of the appointment. The successful candidate is expected to conduct research and actively seek external funding for their own research. They are also expected to participate actively in and contribute to the department’s research environment and seminar activities, as well as to engage with the international research community through participation in conferences and publication in international scholarly outlets. Teaching responsibilities include courses at the undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral levels. Teaching and assessment are conducted in either Swedish or English, depending on the courses involved. The duties also include pedagogical development and the administrative tasks associated with an academic position. Teaching at the Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) is campus-based, and staff members are expected to be present on campus in order to contribute to a vibrant and stimulating academic environment. JMG collaborates with a wide range of external partners for example DEMICOM, Mid Sweden University, Hamrin-stiftelsen, Medier och Demokrati och Tore G. Wärenstams stiftelse, and the position includes participation in the department’s external engagement activities. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or has attained equivalent academic qualifications in Media and Communication Studies, or in a related field with a clear focus on Media and Communication Studies, within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an assistant professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise must be demonstrated through independent research characterized by a high degree of ability to formulate research problems, theoretical and methodological awareness, and analytical skill. Research expertise is further demonstrated through scholarly publications and ongoing participation in national and/or international research collaborations. In the assessment of scholarly merit, particular weight will be given to documented research accomplishments relating to digital and social media (see above). Documented experience in the use of mixed methods, combining quantitative and qualitative approaches, is considered meritorious. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is further placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious.  Consideration will also be given to how well the applicant’s research relates to and complements the research and research groups already existing within the department. Research at JMG. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Completion of further training in higher education pedagogy is considered meritorious. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of journalism, media and communication at the University of Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor has the right to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Associate Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that the individual meets the eligibility requirements for such a position and is also deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established assessment criteria for promotion to the position of Associate Senior Lecturer. To determine whether the applicant has achieved sufficient qualifications for promotion, an assessment will be conducted in accordance with the assessment criteria for employment as an Associate Senior Lecturer applicable at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). In addition, the following promotion requirements apply: Research expertise must be demonstrated through high-quality scholarly publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. Publications in peer-reviewed books or book chapters are considered meritorious. The applicant must also have actively applied for external research funding in national and international competitive calls. Success in obtaining such funding carries considerable weight in the assessment. Furthermore, the applicant must have contributed to the scholarly community through activities such as serving as a peer reviewer, acting as an external examiner (faculty opponent) at a doctoral thesis defence, or serving as a member of a doctoral examination committee. The applicant must also demonstrate progression in relation to the qualifications held at the time of appointment, for example by having attained the qualifications required for appointment as a docent. Teaching expertise must be demonstrated through at least active participation in teaching, experience of course development and different teaching methods, completion of qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education equivalent to PIL101–PIL103, and completion of supervisor training equivalent to PIL201, or a decision by the Dean recognising equivalent qualifications. In assessing an application for promotion, primary emphasis is placed on research qualifications and secondary emphasis on teaching qualifications. The applicant must also have demonstrated collaboration skills and leadership ability by, for example, participating in collaboration activities with the wider community, demonstrating the ability to lead and organise research projects or other activities, and assuming collegial responsibility within the department, for example through participation in assessment panels or working groups. With regard to the assessment criterion of suitability, consideration will be given to sound professional judgement, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department's activities. The applicant must also fulfil the Swedish language requirement at a minimum of CEFR level B2. Compliance with this requirement must be demonstrated either by passing an approved language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department confirming that a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be promoted to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/employment-benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during December 2026 and the beginning of January 2027. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Marie Grusell, Head of Department marie.grusell@jmg.gu.se or Jesper Strömbäck, Assistant Head of Department jesper.stromback@jmg.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Sofia Hallsten, HR-officer sofia.hallsten@jmg.gu.se Trade Union Representatives Information about trade union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application To apply for this position, please submit your application through the University of Gothenburg's online recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure that the application is complete, in accordance with the instructions in this advertisement, and submitted to the University no later than the closing date. As the applications will be assessed by external experts whose working language is English, applicants are requested to submit all application documents in English. The application must include: Curriculum vitae/CV , including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding received (including amounts). Research plan. The plan must be clearly linked to the research area of the position and should be suitable for development into at least one competitive research grant application. A description of your research qualifications including: a) A list of your five most significant research publications. State your role and the extent of your contribution to publications co-authored with others. Co-author statements must be attached; these must include information on each author’s contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming its acceptance for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research merits, research profile and research plans for the next few years. (Max. 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most significant research publications. A description of your teaching qualifications including: a) a list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught (including courses and programmes at first-cycle, second-cycle and third-cycle level). b) A brief account of your teaching merits and qualifications, providing information on both scope and quality, including a reflection on your own teaching practice. (Max. 2 pages.) c) documentation of completed training in teaching and learning in higher education. d) Certification of your teaching experience from the Head of Department, Director of Studies or equivalent at at least one university/department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions for students (max. 3 supporting documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your teaching merits and qualifications. Doctoral degree certificate Other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Research publications, other written works, CVs and similar documents that are not available in electronic form should be sent to: Department of Journalism, Media and Communication (JMG) Attn: Sofia Hallsten Box 710 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Closing date for applications: 30 October 2026 The University of Gothenburg is committed to promoting equal opportunities, equality, and diversity, and values the contributions that a diverse workforce brings to the organisation. The University applies individual salary setting. In accordance with the regulations of the Swedish National Archives, the University is required to retain application documents for two years following the appointment decision. If an applicant specifically requests the return of their application documents, they will be returned after the two-year retention period has expired. Otherwise, the documents will be disposed of in accordance with the applicable regulations. To recruitment agencies and advertising sales representatives: The University of Gothenburg has contracted recruitment advertising services and therefore respectfully declines all direct contact from recruitment and staffing agencies, as well as from advertising sales representatives. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=40412": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Geology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Geology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Geology Reference number PAR 2026/390 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Earth Sciences has approximately 75 employees working across the fields of Geology, Physical Geography and Ecosystem Sciences. Our three priority research themes are \"The Geosphere\", \"Earth Surface Processes\" and \"Climate and Climate Change\". Within Geology belonging to “The Geosphere” research theme, we have established strengths in hydrogeology, geochemistry, geophysics and structural geology. Cooperation with strong specializations in the other fields, such as climatology, alpine and polar ecology as well as GIS and remote sensing, is encouraged. Our mission is to develop and deliver research, education, and services to society that enhance our understanding of the Earth System processes and the impacts of human activity. We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive workplace and a family-friendly environment. Sweden also offers parental leave and childcare policies. Further information is available on our website: Department of Earth Sciences , University of Gothenburg Employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg We welcome applications from innovative and forward-looking geoscientists across all areas of geology. Subject area Geology Subject area description Geology at the Department of Earth Sciences encompasses studies on understanding today’s geodynamic processes of the solid Earth, as well as the evolution of Earth and other terrestrial planets over the entire history of the solar system. Our research is rooted in the drive to advance basic science, but at the same time meet societal needs. We benefit from being able to use the diverse geology of Western Sweden as a natural laboratory for our studies, as one important component of an otherwise global network of field studies. Furthermore, we are a significant collaborator for a wide range of thriving geotechnical companies in the wider Gothenburg area due to expanding activities in tunnel and building construction, increasing need for water and energy resources. The geology group at the Department of Earth Sciences offers excellent research infrastructure including state-of-the-art analytical facilities, such as a micro-geochemistry laboratory, a stable isotope laboratory, water analysis facilities, a micro-Raman laboratory as well as an ice- and microplastic laboratory. These are complemented by advanced remote sensing and visualization facilities. Current research activities include mineral resources for a green economy, ice dynamics, development of new geochemical and petrophysical tools, groundwater resources, soil and groundwater contamination, subduction zone processes and links to natural hazards, climate change in the geologic past, and geothermal energy. Duties The tenure-track Assistant Professor position is primarily research-focused, with research comprising up to 80% of the workload. The position includes a fully funded PhD position attached to it, financed by the department. The appointee is expected to establish and lead an independent research programme within their chosen field in collaboration with colleagues both at the University of Gothenburg and with national and international partners. They will pursue research at the highest international level guided by their scientific expertise and priorities and are expected to build a competitive research group supported by external funding. Key responsibilities include publishing in high-quality peer-reviewed journals and actively securing external research funding. Teaching will comprise up to 20% of the workload.  The Assistant Professor will contribute to the development of new curricula and teach both classroom-based and field-based courses. Teaching will be in the subject area of geology within the Earth Science program. They will supervise students at all academic levels including Bachelor’s and Master’s student projects and may also supervise doctoral candidates and postdoctoral researchers. The position also includes participation in administrative duties at the Department and Faculty levels, as well as shared collegial responsibility for relevant analytical infrastructure. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in Geology or a closely related field, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. The Faculty of Science and Technology uses these criteria for the assessment of scientific and pedagogical competence and general suitability. Scientific and pedagogical qualifications must be adequate in relation to the subject content and job description of the advertised position. Research expertise Research expertise should be demonstrated through internationally recognized research, primarily evidenced by a strong record of peer reviewed publications. The ability to obtain external research funding constitutes an integral part of the assessment. The applicant must have a research profile within the field of geology. Particular emphasis will be placed on demonstrated connections to one or more of the Department’s core research areas: micro geochemistry (with a focus on critical raw materials), hydrogeology (including contaminant behaviour in soils and groundwater), and structural geology (with a focus on rock and ice microstructures). Teaching expertise In addition to the research expertise, documented pedagogical qualifications are considered meritorious for the employment as Assistant Professor. Relevant qualifications include teaching experience at undergraduate and postgraduate level within the subject area of geology, the ability to develop pedagogical activities, and experience of supervision. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence For the appointment as Assistant Professor, experience in outreach and engagement with society beyond academia is considered meritorious. Such experience may be demonstrated through experience in dissemination of information experience in community outreach experience in innovation and entrepreneurship For the appointment as Assistant Professor, leadership and administrative experience are considered advantageous. Relevant qualifications may include experience in academic leadership or management at unit, department, faculty or university level participation in boards, committees or other roles relevant to the functioning of the University experience of leadership in relevant networks/associations/organizations Overall Assessment The assessment of general suitability includes an evaluation of whether the applicant, with regard to this job description, has the personal qualities required to fulfil the duties of an Assistant Professor well, to interact with other staff and to contribute to the development of the Department’s activities. To this end, a brief written statement of how the applicant’s planned research can benefit and be integrated with the Department’s research must be included in the application. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Earth Sciences, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. The assessment criteria for promotion as a teacher are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers (GU 2025/1411). Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. The assessment of qualifications and experience will take into account documented parental leave, sick leave or other similar circumstances affecting research output and academic seniority. The reasons and extent of absence should be specified in the application. The interviews are scheduled to take place on 16 November 2026. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact: Hans Linderholm, Head of Department of Earth Sciences at University of Gothenburg, Professor in Physical Geography, at +46 70-858-9504 or HansL@gvc.gu.se, or Mark Peternell, Associate Professor in Structural Geology, at +46 76-618-4553 or mark.peternell@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Therese Vestlund, Human Resources Officer, at +46 76-618-6678 or therese.vestlund@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The merit portfolio in your application consists of five parts: Personal data, degrees and evaluations Scientific portfolio Pedagogical portfolio Collaboration portfolio Leadership and administrative portfolio Together with your application which includes the merit portfolio, the following should be attached: Cover letter CV, including a complete list of publications, and a record of awarded research grants specifying funding amounts . A list of maximum ten selected scientific publications and a motivation for choosing each one. Describe your own role and input in each of the selected publications. Copies of the most relevant publications in full format (maximum ten publications). Description of how the applicant’s current and future research can be integrated with ongoing research at the department. Description of pedagogical experience, including evaluations of teaching quality. The description should also include pedagogical reflection and teaching philosophy (maximum two pages). Copy of PhD degree certificate, and other certificates/documents/transcripts that support the applicant’s qualifications. Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be mailed in two copies to: Department of Earth Sciences Attn: Eugenia Andersson University of Gothenburg P.O. Box 460 S-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden Application deadline: 30 October 2026 Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41384": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in basic medical research",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in basic medical research For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in basic medical research Reference number PAR 2026/675 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. Sahlgrenska Academy is the medical faculty at the University of Gothenburg and a leading centre for research and education in medicine, odontology and health sciences. Research, healthcare and innovation come together in a thriving life science ecosystem, supported by close collaboration with Sahlgrenska University Hospital, advanced research infrastructure, and a strong life science and biotechnology sector. Learn more about Sahlgrenska Academy . As part of the University's initiative, Sahlgrenska Academy is recruiting an Assistant Professor within the framework of the Academy's long-term efforts to strengthen life science research and research environments, spanning basic science, translational research and clinical application – as well as education. The position will be based at whichever of the Academy's six departments offers the best environment for the successful candidate's research to develop. Call for 2 assistant professor positions Subject area Basic medical research Subject area description The subject area encompasses basic medical research into the molecular, cellular and physiological mechanisms underlying health and disease. It may also include, to a greater or lesser extent, a translational component bridging basic medical research and clinical application, with the aim of developing new approaches to diagnosis, prevention and treatment. Duties The position as Assistant Professor in basic medical research primarily involves conducting independent, internationally competitive research within the subject area of the position. The research should contribute to an increased understanding of the molecular, cellular and physiological mechanisms underlying health and disease. It may encompass both basic and translational research, with the aim of creating a foundation for the development of new approaches to diagnosis, prevention and treatment. The successful candidate is expected to actively develop an independent line of research, seek external research funding and contribute to the development of a strong research environment. Duties also include collaboration with researchers within the department, Sahlgrenska Academy, the healthcare sector, and national and international networks. The position primarily involves research, but also includes teaching at first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral levels. Duties may include lectures, seminars, and the supervision of degree projects and doctoral students. The successful candidate is expected to develop their teaching expertise and gradually take on greater responsibility for the planning, delivery and development of teaching within the subject area. A successful candidate who does not have formal training in academic teaching must undertake such training within one year of taking up the position. For promotion, the candidate must have attained proficiency in spoken and written Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR) no later than six months before the end of the appointment as Assistant Professor. The University of Gothenburg offers Swedish-language courses, which the successful candidate is required to attend  as part of the appointment. All other requirements for promotion set by Sahlgrenska Academy must also be met. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Pedagogical competence must be well documented in a manner that allows the quality of the educational activities to be assessed. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Good collaborative skills, both within and beyond the candidate's own research environment, are also highly valued. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer stated by Sahlgrenska Academy. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact: Jenny Nyström, Professor and Dean at Sahlgrenska Academy jenny.nystrom@neuro.gu.se Johan Wessberg, Professor and Chairman of the Academic Appointments Board at Sahlgrenska Academy johan.wessberg@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applications must follow the layout of the recommendations for the Guidelines for application to teaching position . Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. Please note in particular that the following documents (in swedish or english) must accompany the application: Doctoral degree (If the degree certificate has been translated into English, the certificate in the original language must also be enclosed.) Research plan/Research programme A maximum of 10 selected scientific publications that the applicant want to refer to (in full text) Other certificates /proof /rating proving applicant's qualifications Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg The Sahlgrenska Academy Box 400 405 30 Göteborg SWEDEN The reference number of the application, PAR 2026/675, should be clearly stated when mailing complementary documents. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 In the event of any disagreement between the English and the Swedish versions of any announcement, the Swedish version takes precedence. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41383": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in clinical research in medicine and health sciences",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in clinical research in medicine and health sciences For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in clinical research in medicine and health sciences Reference number PAR 2026/674 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. Sahlgrenska Academy is the medical faculty at the University of Gothenburg and a leading centre for research and education in medicine, odontology and health sciences. Research, healthcare and innovation come together in a thriving life science ecosystem, supported by close collaboration with Sahlgrenska University Hospital, advanced research infrastructure, and a strong life science and biotechnology sector. Learn more about Sahlgrenska Academy . As part of the University's initiative, Sahlgrenska Academy is recruiting an Assistant Professor within the framework of the Academy's long-term efforts to strengthen life science research and research environments, spanning basic science, translational research and clinical application – as well as education. The position will be based at whichever of the Academy's six departments offers the best environment for the successful candidate's research to develop. Call for 2 assistant professor positions Subject area Clinical research in medicine and health sciences Subject area description The subject area encompasses clinical, translational and health sciences research, with a focus on prevention, diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation and follow-up across the entire continuum of care. It includes research in medicine, health and care sciences, and odontology, as well as research integrating biological, clinical, behavioural and societal perspectives to improve health and healthcare. Duties The position as Assistant Professor in clinical research in medical and health sciences primarily involves conducting independent, internationally competitive research within the subject area. The research must be clinically relevant to human health and disease and contribute to increased knowledge in prevention, diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation and care. The successful candidate is expected to actively develop an independent line of research, seek external research funding and contribute to the development of a strong scientific and interprofessional research environment. Duties also include collaboration with researchers within the department, Sahlgrenska Academy, the healthcare sector, and national and international networks. The position primarily involves research, but also includes teaching at first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral levels. Duties may include lectures, seminars, and the supervision of degree projects and doctoral students. The successful candidate is expected to develop their teaching expertise and gradually take on greater responsibility for the planning, delivery and development of teaching within the subject area. A successful candidate who does not have formal training in academic teaching must undertake such training within one year of taking up the position. For promotion, the candidate must have attained proficiency in spoken and written Swedish at a minimum level of B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR) no later than six months before the end of the appointment as Assistant Professor. The University of Gothenburg offers Swedish-language courses, which the successful candidate is required to attend as part of the appointment. All other requirements for promotion set by Sahlgrenska Academy must also be met. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on clinical expertise as well as collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise In the assessment of research expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on documented experience of clinical research in medicine or health sciences. The candidate must demonstrate the ability to establish an independent profile in clinical research relevant to the subject area of the position. Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. Particular emphasis will be placed on high-quality publications with reputable international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. Greater weight will be given to the quality, originality, and impact of publications than to the total number of publications. Considerable weight will also be given to the ability to obtain external research funding on a competitive basis. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Pedagogical competence must be well documented in a manner that allows the quality of the educational activities to be assessed. Clinical expertise In the assessment of clinical expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on clinical research relevant to the subject area of the position. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence will be assessed based on experience of scientific, clinical and academic leadership, for example through leading, coordinating and developing research or educational activities, as well as through contributions to the development of the department or faculty. Experience of scientific and clinical leadership within national and international networks is particularly meritorious. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer stated by Sahlgrenska Academy. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact: Jenny Nyström, Professor and Dean at Sahlgrenska Academy jenny.nystrom@neuro.gu.se Johan Wessberg, Professor and Chairman for the Academic Appointments Board johan.wessberg@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applications must follow the layout of the recommendations for the Guidelines for application to teaching position . Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. Please note in particular that the following documents (in swedish or english) must accompany the application: Doctoral degree (If the degree certificate has been translated into English, the certificate in the original language must also be enclosed.) Research plan/Research programme A maximum of 10 selected scientific publications that the applicant want to refer to (in full text) Other certificates /proof /rating proving applicant's qualifications Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg The Sahlgrenska Academy Box 400 405 30 Göteborg SWEDEN The reference number of the application, PAR 2026/674, should be clearly stated when mailing complementary documents. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 In the event of any disagreement between the English and the Swedish versions of any announcement, the Swedish version takes precedence. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
