    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41287": {
      "title": "Assistant professor in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant professor in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant professor in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods Reference number PAR 2026/645 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) teaching and research activities stretch from alpine ecosystems, through forests, cultivated land and streams, and into the marine environment. In these environments, we study different levels of biological organization, from genes, individuals, and populations to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics, and conservation, and integrate across these fields to understand past and present ongoing evolutionary processes and the impact of anthropogenic changes on the environment. The strategy of the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences is to foster strong internationally networked research and to promote excellence in teaching. This position is further co-funded by the Strategic Research Area: Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services in a Changing Climate (BECC; www.becc.lu.se/ ), which is a cooperation between Lund University and the University of Gothenburg. BECC brings together scientists from natural and social sciences to perform research essential in understanding the impacts of climate change on biodiversity and ecosystem services, as well as measures to mitigate and adapt to these changes. This position is particularly linked to the BECC grand challenges “Biodiversity under Global Change” and “Ecosystem Services under Global Change” ( https://www.becc.lu.se/research-imperative-questions-our-time/becc-grand-challenges ). BioEnv also hosts internationally prominent research in biodiversity and hosts the Gothenburg Global Biodiversity Centre (GGBC; https://www.gu.se/en/ggbc-global-biodiversity ), which promotes research and outreach. BioEnv and GGBC also facilitate access to several large biological collections spanning wide areas of the world and the tree of life. Subject area Ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods Subject area description The subject area encompasses ecology, evolution, diversity, and conservation of terrestrial arthropods. This includes studies of taxonomy, systematics, phylogenetics, species interactions, and evolutionary processes, as well as the ecological roles of arthropods in natural and managed ecosystems. Research and supervision tasks may be field- and/or collections-based and contribute to understanding the distribution, diversity, conservation and the role of arthropods in the functioning of terrestrial ecosystems and ecosystem services in a changing climate. Duties The main purpose of the position is to enable the successful candidate to establish an independent and internationally competitive research profile in ecology and evolution of terrestrial arthropods and to develop the scientific and pedagogical qualifications required for promotion to Senior Lecturer within 5 years. The research focus can encompass ecology, evolution, systematics or other aspects concerning terrestrial arthropods. Research can be field- and/or collection-based. Research tasks include developing an independent research program in the ecology and evolution of invertebrates. You are expected to apply for external, national and international grants and build and lead a research group, as well as supervise PhD students. Teaching tasks are conducted at undergraduate and graduate levels within the biological and environmental science programs and are conducted in both Swedish and English. You are expected to teach about ecology and evolution, with a focus on Scandinavian terrestrial arthropods. Teaching might also include ecology, evolution and conservation as well as other aspects of biology and environmental sciences. Teaching forms include traditional, in-the-field, collection-based and other forms of lectures and exercises, as well as grading reports, theses , and examinations. Teaching occurs both at the department and in collaboration with several other departments and faculties (also see the ClimBEco Graduate Research School ). In addition to lectures and exercises, course organization is also part of the job. Other tasks include supervision of first- and second-cycle degree projects in biological and environmental science. As a part of a larger team, you are expected to take an active role in the development and renewal of teaching at the department in both form and content. Contributions to the development of master’s level courses in ecology and related areas will be welcomed by the department. The successful candidate is also expected to participate in and contribute to the development of doctoral-level education. Teaching will initially comprise approximately 20% of full-time employment. The distribution between research, teaching and other duties may develop during the period of employment in accordance with the needs of the Department and the career development of the successful candidate. The position further includes research administration and project management, departmental service, societal collaboration and interaction, and participation in relevant research networks and research infrastructures. Competence development is included in the position, particularly training in teaching and learning in higher education and supervision corresponding to 20 higher education credits (PIL101, PIL102, PIL103 and PIL201, or equivalent, Courses offered in English | The PIL Unit ). For candidates who do not speak Swedish, competence development in Swedish is also included. The holder of the position is expected to develop independence as a researcher and to gain merits, both scientifically and pedagogically, to meet the eligibility requirements for a position as senior lecturer, prior to which the application for a docentship (Sve. ‘docentur’) is a milestone. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or related fileds or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Broad knowledge in ecology, evolution, taxonomy, and phylogeny, of  terrestrial arthropods is required. The following are meritorious: Knowledge of European and/or Scandinavian arthropod taxa Faunistic knowledge of multiple arthropod orders. Knowledge of species interactions and conservation biology Experience in applying an ecosystem services perspective regarding terrestrial arthropods Documented education in higher education didactics Documented ability to cooperate internally and externally Documented capacity for academic collegial cooperation and external collaborations with the society at large. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations and should be tightly linked to the subject area. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (e.g. lectures, seminars, field- and collection- based teaching, supervision, etc) at undergraduate, graduate and doctoral levels (meritorious but not required) within the subject area of the position and with emphasis on the ability to teach about Scandinavian terrestrial arthropods. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of courses and/or study programmes and instruction, consequently improving the teaching quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently (up to seven years). The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. During the employment period, no later than 6 months before it ends, the Assistant professor is expected to apply to be promoted to a permanent position as senior lecturer. It is to be noted that the application for promotion must be made within the same subject in which the applicant is employed. The requirements for promotion to senior lecturer can be found in the procedures of the Faculty of Science and Technology: https://s3.gu.se/earkiv-publikt/GU1816-1319333663-232803.pdf Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place December 14. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Åsa Arrhenius, head of department +46 31 786 2625 asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se Søren Faurby, senior lecturer +46 736 422 482 soren.faurby@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences, hr@bioenv.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application should include: Curriculum Vitae/CV, including a complete list of publications and record of received research grants (with amounts) and a link to profile in Inaturalist or similar platform. A research plan. The plan must clearly link to the research area for the position and must be able to be formulated into at least one competitive application for research funding. A description of your scientific qualifications including : a) A list of your top five scientific publications. Indicate your role and the extent of your work in relation to publications co-authored with others. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, a certificate must be attached confirming that the publication has been accepted for publication. b) A brief statement of your scientific qualifications, your research profile and your research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your top scientific publications. A description of your teaching qualifications including : a) a list of the courses and educational programs you have developed and taught (including courses and programs at undergraduate level, advanced level and postgraduate level. b ) A brief statement of your teaching credentials and qualifications with information on both scope and quality, including a pedagogical self-reflection. (Maximum 2 pages) c ) certificate of completed university pedagogical education. d ) Certificate of your teaching experience from head of department, director of studies or equivalent at at least one university/one department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, literature lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions to students (max. 3 documents). Refer to these documents in your statement of your teaching credentials and qualifications (4b). Diploma Doctoral Degree. Other certificates and documents that you wish to refer to. Scientific publications, writings, CVs and similar that are not available in electronic form should be sent to:  Maria Thell, Box 463, SE40530 Gothenburg, Sweden. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41377": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in educational work",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in educational work For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in educational work Reference number PAR 2026/669 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in educational work, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas; subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in educational work will be based at the Department of Education and Special Education. Subject area Educational work Subject area description Educational work is a subject that examines various perspectives and aspects of work within educational institutions in society. Research in educational work addresses issues relevant to educational stakeholders, professional groups and organizations. This includes the conditions under which teachers and students engage in teaching and learning, both in practice and in relation to policy and educational reforms. Duties A position as assistant professor in educational work aims to develop the employee’s pedagogical and academic competence for further career development; therefore, the role is evenly divided between research 50 % and teaching 50 %. The research will be conducted in collaboration with senior researchers at the Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools and forms part of the research programme to be conducted within the strategic research area ‘Excellence in Schools’. The Assistant Senior Lecturer is expected to be active in one or more of the department’s research environments and to contribute to the department’s academic development. The research role involves independently conducting and developing research in the field of pedagogical practice, publishing academic articles and actively seeking external research funding in line with the department’s strategic research programme, e.g. EU funding. The majority of the department’s teaching responsibilities are within the teacher training programmes, but teaching also takes place within standalone programmes and courses, as well as in commissioned education. The teaching remit may include teaching, supervision and assessment at undergraduate and advanced levels and, where relevant, at postgraduate level. Course coordination, course development, pedagogical development work and collaboration may also be included. Teaching at the department is mainly conducted on campus and in Swedish, although some teaching in English does take place. Proficiency in spoken and written English and Swedish, or another Scandinavian language, is therefore required. Staff are also expected to be regularly present on campus and to contribute actively to a stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networkst For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise A teaching qualification or experience of working as an educator Experience of activities specific to teacher education, such as school-based placement Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities [and suitability] required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Education and Special Education, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact the Head of Department, Alli Klapp, alli.klapp@ped.gu.se , tel. 031-786 2770 If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR Officer, Ivana Nikolic, ivana.nikolic@gu.se , tel. 031-786 93 24. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Please indicate which specialisation in subject-matter education you are applying for: social studies, Swedish, mathematics, or science and technology. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previous teaching within the subject area, showing how teaching hours have been distributed over time and across different courses, as well as qualitative documentation (such as descriptions of development work and course materials) that illustrates and provides evidence of the applicant’s pedagogical expertise. The list of publications must be structured so that peer-reviewed journal articles, peer-reviewed book chapters, and peer-reviewed books are presented under separate headings. Degree certificates, transcripts, and other certificates must be submitted together as a single PDF file. Publications or other written works that cannot be submitted digitally with the application must be sent in duplicate to: University of Gothenburg Department of Education and Special Education Att: Ivana Nikolic BOX 300, 405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden. Please mark the envelope with the reference number for the position: PAR 2026/669. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
