    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41206": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Public Administration",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Public Administration For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Public Administration Reference number PAR 2026/577 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position intended to give the appointee the opportunity to develop their teaching and research expertise. At the end of the appointment, an Assistant Professor may apply for promotion to Senior Lecturer. If the Assistant Professor is deemed suitable and fulfils the requirements for promotion, they shall be promoted and given a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. More information about the department is available on our website gu.se/en/public-administration . Subject area Public Administration Subject area description The School of Public Administration conducts education, research and collaboration within the subject of public administration. Public administration is a multidisciplinary field of research that studies the organisation, governance and functioning of the public sector and its relationship to society, integrating theories and methods from several social science disciplines. Research and education in public administration at the department are conducted mainly within three areas: politics, democracy and public administration; public sector accounting and financial management; and organisation, management and society. The School of Public Administration currently hosts research projects on, among other things, administration and bureaucracy, professions, the location of public services, artificial intelligence (AI), cybersecurity, energy policy, public procurement, local democracy, financial management and control, migration and work integration, and legal perspectives on public administration. More information about our researchers and ongoing research can be found on our website: Our Research The School of Public Administration has a long tradition of research on local politics and administration and of collaboration with the surrounding community. This environment is strengthened by the long-standing Partnership for Collaboration, Education and Research (PACER), which brings together the School of Public Administration, municipalities and Region Västra Götaland in research, education and collaboration. The School of Public Administration’s programmes in public administration comprise bachelor’s and master’s programmes, doctoral education and commissioned education. The School of Public Administration is also responsible for courses within the Pre-school Teacher Programme and the National School Leadership Training Programme. The School of Public Administration welcomes both applicants who wish to join some of our existing research areas and applicants who wish to develop other areas within the subject of public administration. Duties The duties of an Assistant Professor in public administration consist of research to one half and teaching to one half. The teaching component may also include administrative duties. The duties comprise: Research within the subject of public administration. The Assistant Professor is free to choose research questions, develop theoretical ideas, collect data and select research methods. The duties include actively applying for external international and national research funding and actively participating in the department’s research environments and seminar activities. The Assistant Professor is also expected to take part in the international scholarly conversation through participation in conferences and through international publication with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. Teaching, course coordination, supervision and examination in courses at first-cycle and second-cycle level in public administration. Teaching within third-cycle (doctoral) education may also become relevant. Teaching in most of the department’s programmes and courses may be considered. The duties also include course and programme development and participation in the department’s educational development work. Collaboration with the wider community. Active participation in the continued development of the department and its academic environment in Gothenburg. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as a teacher is set out in Chapter 4, Sections 3–4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg (GU 2024/1687) . Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in public administration or in another subject area relevant to the position in which the public administration perspective is central, such as sociology, social work, political science or business administration, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders. Participation in commissioned education, the performance of qualified research-related review assignments within the public sector, networking activities directed at public administrations, and a documented ability to communicate research findings to the public are all considered meritorious. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Considerable weight will be given to the applicant’s intention and ability to participate actively in the academic environment at the School of Public Administration in Gothenburg. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: School of Public Administration, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in public administration, upon application submitted no later than six months before the fixed-term employment expires, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the assessment criteria for appointment as Senior Lecturer that apply at the University of Gothenburg in accordance with the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teachers at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). The following requirements also apply in such an assessment: The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of research expertise through: Scholarly publication of good quality in leading international peer-reviewed journals. In addition, scholarly publication of good quality in peer-reviewed books or book chapters is also meritorious. Documented and recurring applications for external research funding in international and/or national competition. Obtaining such funding is highly meritorious. Taking responsibility for the research community, for example by having undertaken review assignments, acted as faculty opponent or served as a member of an examining committee. Progression in relation to the applicant’s research qualifications at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example by attaining the qualifications required for the title of docent (associate professor). The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of teaching expertise through: Teaching amounting to at least 1,600 hours. Experience of course development and of different forms of teaching. Completed and approved training in teaching and learning in higher education (courses PIL101–PIL103, or a decision by the Dean on validation) and supervisor training (PIL201, or a decision by the Dean on validation). Progression in relation to the applicant’s teaching expertise at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example through experience of teaching at different levels, in different subject areas, or through deepened expertise within previously covered areas. The applicant must have demonstrated collaborative and leadership ability through: Having organised and participated in outward-facing academic activities, for example workshops, panels or conferences. Having organised and participated in outward-facing activities in collaboration with the wider community, for example conferences with decision-makers, service users or organisations, or other activities directed at the general public. Having demonstrated the ability to lead and organise academic projects, education or other activities. Having taken collegial responsibility for the department, for example through participation in assessment groups and working groups. The applicant must demonstrate personal suitability through: Good judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties included in the position, and the ability to cooperate and contribute to the development of activities. The applicant must fulfil the following language requirement: Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated by a passed language test, the requirements of which are described here (common-european-framework-reference-language-skills), or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is manifestly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: employment benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during December 2026. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact the Head of Department, Vicki Johansson, telephone +46 31 786 15 96 or email Vicki.Johansson@spa.gu.se . If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Maria Utterhall, HR Officer, maria.utterhall@spa.gu.se , +46 31 786 29 66. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The following documents must be attached to the application: 1. A cover letter stating why you are applying for the position. In the letter, you should describe how your research and teaching fit into the subject of public administration in general, as well as how you intend to contribute to the School of Public Administration’s existing research areas and study programmes and/or to contribute new perspectives that would enrich the environment. Please also provide a detailed account of how you intend to contribute actively to the School of Public Administration’s academic environment through by being actively present at the department in Gothenburg. 2. A curriculum vitae (CV), including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding obtained (with amounts). 3. A research plan. The plan must clearly relate to the research area of the position and must be capable of being developed into at least one competitive research grant application. 4. A description of your research qualifications, containing: a) A list of your five most important scholarly publications. State your role and the extent of your contribution in the case of publications that are co-authored with others. Co-authorship certificates must be attached; these must contain information about each author’s contribution (quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, a certificate confirming that the publication has been accepted for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research qualifications, your research profile and your research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 2 pages.) 5. Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most important scholarly publications. 6. A description of your teaching qualifications, containing: a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught on (including courses and programmes at first-cycle, second-cycle and third-cycle level). b) A brief account of your teaching qualifications, with information on both extent and quality, including a pedagogical self-reflection. (Maximum 2 pages.) c) A certificate of completed training in teaching and learning in higher education. d) A certificate of your teaching experience from the head of department, director of studies or equivalent at at least one university/department. 7. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides and instructions to students (maximum 3 documents). Refer to these documents in your account of your teaching qualifications (6b). 8. Certificates of employment. 9. Doctoral degree certificate. 10. Any other documents that you as an applicant wish to invoke. Publications that cannot be attached to the application electronically should be sent in two copies to: University of Gothenburg, School of Public Administration, att: Maria Utterhall, Box 712, SE-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden, stating PAR 2026/577. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41012": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Fine Art",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Fine Art For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Fine Art Reference number PAR 2026/563 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The  Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and HSM – Academy of Performing Arts and Music. As a unified faculty for art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work of creating a more sustainable and open society. HDK-Valand is now looking for an Assistant Professor in Fine Art, who will be part of the unit Craft and Fine Art and attached to the Centre for Art and the Political Imaginary (CAPIm) a joint initiative with our partner Kungl. Konsthögskolan (KKH). The Centre for Art and the Political Imaginary is committed to interdisciplinary practice and research in the meeting between contemporary art and the future of politics. Based at two institutions of higher education in art HDK-Valand and Kungl. Konsthögskolan, the Centre’s aim is to facilitate connections between research and education through an engagement with experimental approaches. It is the first Swedish Centre of Excellence in the field of Artistic Research. HDK-Valand’s research environment values a broad and inclusive approach whereby knowledge is produced within established research formats, experimental artistic practices and education. Our research contributes to discourses on environmental, societal and cultural challenges through engagement in interdisciplinary projects. Four research themes oriented around politics, the environment, economics and materiality operate as focus areas to facilitate collaboration between different subjects and actors, and to provide a broad base for both research and teaching. An overarching objective at HDK-Valand is the development of a complete academic environment grounded in these themes More about HDK-Valand here: https://www.gu.se/en/hdk-valand More about CAPIm here: https://capim.se/en Subject area Fine Art Subject area description Fine art at HDK-Valand is characterised by the independent artistic practice of artists working individually and collectively. Fine art is typically not guided by functional or commercial imperatives that are external to the practice itself. In addition to bachelor's and master's degree programmes we have a shared commitment to freestanding courses, life-long learning, continued professional development, and research informed education in this subject area. Fine art is also characterised by a lively and broad research culture at HDK-Valand that includes the clusters: (i) Economies: Organizing, Valuating and Working, (ii) Materialities and Experimental Aesthetics, (iii) Environment: Ecological and Climate Challenges, and (iv) Politics, Practices and Publics. The subject thinks broadly and innovatively about forms of making public including but not limited to, exhibition and exhibition-making, publishing and a wide range of organizational, curatorial and mediation practices – taking account of how, where, when, for whom, and by whom art is produced, disclosed and circulated. Fine art fosters moments of exchange between education, research and the wider society. At HDK-Valand, the subject of fine art encompasses an open concept of artistic practice that includes an expanding variety of formats, genres, media, techniques and traditions such as: – Drawing; graphics; painting; automated image generation; and text-based practices. – Conceptual art; found-objects; installation; sculpture; site-specific work; land art; bio-art; and eco-art. – Moving image; animation; photography; digital media; augmented and virtual reality; robotics; and work with autonomous agents. – Performance; live-art; body-art; and sound-art. – Public art; community art; collaborative, social and relational practices; and curatorial practice. Duties Research tasks (50% of the total employment) Realise own artistic research projects within the framework of the Centre’s remit aligned with one of CAPIm’s key thematics: (i) Climate (ii) Historical (iii) Democratic and (iv) Technological Imaginaries Conduct assigned research tasks under the supervision of Prof. Mick Wilson, and in concert with colleagues at the Centre for Art and the Political Imaginary contribute to specific research trajectories associated with the Centre, including but not limited to work on: (i) Mapping the Political Imaginary; (ii) The Observatory of the Artistic Doctorate; and (iii) The role of editorial practices and small-scale presses in artistic research. This work to include tasks such as: Attending regular in-person team meetings Collaborating with colleagues at CAPIm and its broader research community to support activities (e.g., seminars with invited guest researchers; research intensives; and conferences co-organized by CAPIm.) Communicating research and research results to the public in various forms of publication and dissemination (e.g., artistic production, academic journals, academic and public presentations, seminars, workshops, symposia, online media dissemination, public screenings and exhibition) Maintaining, and sharing on a regular basis, detailed documentation of all research processes and outcomes. Participating in collaborative research funding applications based on both the assigned and self-set research Contributing to, and co-editing, joint publications produced within CAPIm Participating in competence development workshops and seminars as required Education tasks (50% of the total employment) Attending regular in-person team meetings Working together with colleagues to deliver and develop existing and new courses at Bachelor, Masters and Doctoral level, including courses associated with CAPIm, as required. Contributing to course and student administration processes and report writing, including quality assurance and enhancement processes and related tasks as required. Working together with colleagues to develop innovative educational content and formats based on current developments in research. Taking on the role of course responsible as required. Participating in competence development workshops and seminars as required. Respon s ib i l i ties and tasks may change over ti me. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. R equirements are: track record in artistic practice-based research which can be demonstrated by identifying previous artistic research activities and outputs in the application and/or supporting documentation. demonstrated knowledge and understanding of the imaginary and the political imaginary as these concepts operate within the domain of artistic production and theory which can be demonstrated by including descriptions of relevant experience in this regard (educational, research or in artistic practice) in the application and/or supporting documentation. the ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise in the fine art context is interpreted according to the norms that apply within the domain of artistic research. Activities such as public exhibition, public performances, participation in international curated projects and dissemination via internationally reputable art platforms and institutions function also as an equivalent for peer review publication in other disciplines. Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (l ectures, seminars, supervision, practical workshops, and project-based teaching ) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled, upon application, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent post as a senior lecturer in the same subject area as that of the Assistant Professor post. Promotion requires that the applicant is qualified and suitable for appointment as a senior lecturer within the subject area, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg’s Regulations on the Appointment of Teaching Staff (Ref. No. GU 2024/3252). In addition, the applicant must have been appointed as an Associate Professor (in Swedish: docent) at the Artistic Faculty. The eligibility requirements for the position of Associate Professor ( docent) are set out in the document accessible via this link (Ref. No. V 2014/885) Anvisningar docent Furthermore, the assessment will evaluate whether you have demonstrated proficiency in Swedish at least at level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated either by passing a language test, the requirements for which are described here common-european-framework-reference-language-skills , or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during early December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Prof. Mick Wilson, mick.wilson@akademinvaland.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR at HDK-Valand: hr@hdk-valand.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Publications that cannot be attached to the application online should be sent to: HDK-Valand Box 131 405 30 and must be received by the deadline. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41380": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in subject-matter education",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in subject-matter education For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in subject-matter education Reference number PAR 2026/672 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Faculty of Education is one of the Nordic region’s leading research environments in the fields of teaching, learning, leadership, and health. Through our research, we contribute to a more equitable and sustainable society. By recruiting four new assistant professors, one of whom specialises in subject-matter education, we are strengthening our work on the national initiative Excellence in school , which the University of Gothenburg and Karlstad University have been commissioned to develop. The initiative integrates three research areas—subject-matter education, knowledge assessment and conditions for learning, and school leadership—with the aim of promoting the development of knowledge among all pupils. As an assistant professor, you will be part of the new Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools , to which a total of eight new assistant professors are being recruited, four of whom will be employed at University of Gothenburg. This position in subject-matter education will be based at the Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies. Subject area Subject-matter education Subject area description Subject-matter education concerns teaching and learning with a focus on specific content, often related to school subjects, academic disciplines, or professional practice. Subject-matter education research investigates the conditions for, and the relationship between, teaching and learning in one of the following subject areas: social studies, Swedish, mathematics, or science and technology. Duties A position as assistant professor in subject-matter education in one of the specialisations listed above is intended to enable the successful candidate to develop their teaching and research expertise for further academic career progression. The position therefore comprises 50% research and 50% teaching. The research will be conducted in collaboration with senior researchers at the Centre for Teaching and Learning in Schools and will form part of the research programme to be carried out within the strategic research area Excellence in School . At the Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies, we conduct research, provide education, and engage in collaboration concerning teaching and learning through subject-matter education, practice-oriented research, and critical research. Our research is organised into three research environments: Didactical Classroom Studies; Critical Education Research; and Phenomenography, Variation Theory, and Learning Study. As an assistant professor, you will be affiliated with one of our research environments and one of our divisions for subject-matter education. Read more about the department, and our divisions and research environments here: Department of Pedagogical, Curricular and Professional Studies, University of Gothenburg The department’s teaching mostly takes place within the primary teacher education programme and the secondary teacher education programme, but also includes teaching on other programmes and courses and in continuing professional development. Teaching duties may also include school-based components of teacher education, core courses in educational studies, supervision of students at different levels (first-, second-, and third-cycle), and assessment of degree projects. Course coordination and administration are also included in the duties. Teaching at the department is conducted primarily on campus and in Swedish, although some teaching in English also takes place. Good oral and written proficiency in English and in Swedish or another Scandinavian language is therefore required. Staff are also expected to be present on campus to a significant extent, thereby contributing to a stimulating academic environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject-matter education or another relevant field of doctoral studies, or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English and in one or more Scandinavian languages is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. International experience from doctoral and/or postdoctoral environments is considered an advantage. Only applicants who meet the eligibility requirements will be assessed. In assessing applicants’ qualifications, most and equal weight will be given to research and teaching experience. Teaching expertise will be assessed as thoroughly as research expertise. The application should therefore place as much emphasis on documenting teaching qualifications as on documenting research qualifications. The assessment will also consider management and administrative expertise, collaboration with wider society, and personal suitability. For the assessment criterion research expertise , the following aspects will be considered: The quality and originality of research within the subject area of the position in relation to the international research community Research publication output Ability to secure external research funding Ability to develop, lead, and conduct research, both independently and in collaboration with others Ability to supervise and examine at doctoral level Being well-established in the research community, through, for example, academic and other expert assignments and participation in networks For the assessment criterion pedagogical expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education (training and courses) Breadth and depth of teaching experience within the subject area of the position Teaching expertise A teaching qualification or experience of working as an educator Experience of activities specific to teacher education, such as school-based placement Experience of supervising degree projects Progression and development as a teacher Educational development work, authorship of course literature, and development of teaching materials Pedagogical leadership and contributions to collegial knowledge development A rationale for one’s own teaching practice informed by teaching and learning in higher education, and the grounding of that practice in research and established experience-based knowledge. If the applicant does not, at the time of application, have qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education that meet the University of Gothenburg’s requirements (courses PIL101, PIL102 and PIL103), these qualifications must either be validated or the specified courses completed within one year of the start of employment (see Assessment and recognition of qualifications in teaching and learning in higher education | The PIL Unit ) For the assessment criterion leadership and administrative expertise , the following aspects will be considered: Experience of leadership roles or roles in decision-making and preparatory bodies, in relation to education, research, or other aspects of higher education Formal and informal leadership in knowledge-building environments Experience of positions of trust within academic activities, nationally or internationally, undertaken independently or on behalf of a higher education institution, relating to research, education, and collaboration Experience of conducting inquiries and preparing reports on specific issues For the assessment criterion collaboration with wider society , the following aspects, among others, will be considered: Communication about education, research, and development work in various forms Contributions to public education and the public communication of research Initiation and implementation of collaborative projects with external partners Relevant experience of, and insight into, the activities of key external partners Substantial contributions to commissioned education and commissioned research For the assessment criterion personal suitability , consideration will be given to sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate with others and contribute to the development of the department’s activities. In the appointment of a member of teaching staff, the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position shall be selected. It is important that the successful applicant is able to contribute to both research and teaching and has the personal qualities (and suitability) required to collaborate internally and externally in order to contribute to the academic development of the department and the subject area. An overall assessment of applicants’ qualifications will be made, taking into account that an assistant professorship is a career-development position intended for relatively recently qualified doctoral researchers and teachers. The purely quantitative extent of an applicant’s achievements will therefore not be decisive. In assessing expertise, consideration will instead be given to the length of time over which the applicant’s overall achievements have been accumulated, as well as to the quality, originality, and impact of their publications. The position will be offered to the applicant who, following an overall assessment of expertise and potential for development, is considered suitable and to have the best ability to perform the duties associated with the position, collaborate with other members of staff, engage with external partners, and contribute to the positive development of the department’s activities. Extensive experience of academic environments outside Sweden is considered a strong advantage. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An assistant professor is entitled to apply, no later than six months before the end of the fixed-term appointment, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as senior lecturer in the same subject area as the assistant professorship, provided that they meet the eligibility requirements for such a position and are also deemed suitable following assessment using the established criteria for promotion to senior lecturer. The following additional requirements for promotion apply in such an assessment: Research expertise for promotion to senior lecturer must be demonstrated in particular through the applicant’s own research. Particular weight will be given to the demonstrated quality and originality of the research, research productivity during the period of employment, and progression in the applicant’s position within the scholarly community during the period of employment. Taking into account qualifications and achievements prior to the start of the appointment, applicants are expected to have an overall research record corresponding to the requirements for appointment as an unpaid docent at the Faculty of Education. Completion of 15 higher education credits in qualifying courses in teaching and learning in higher education (or equivalent) is required for promotion, together with documented development of pedagogical expertise in both breadth and depth. This should include the ability to support student learning across a broader range of areas, in a greater variety of roles, with different groups of students, and at levels of education involving progressively greater demands. Collaboration with wider society is expected. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned for the 4, 7 and 8 December. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact the Head of Department, Annika Lilja, annika.lilja@ped.gu.se, +46 31 786 24 33. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR Officer, Mikaela Hansson, mikaela.hansson@gu.se, +46 31 786 56 08. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Please indicate which specialisation in subject-matter education you are applying for: social studies, Swedish, mathematics, or science and technology. The application should contain information and supporting documentation relevant to assessment in relation to the criteria specified under Assessment criteria. The following documents should be included with your application: A cover letter explaining your reasons for applying for the position A CV/résumé including a list of publications A description of your research profile, previous research activities, and research planned within the scope of the position A list of no more than 10 research publications to be considered in the assessment of the application. These publications must be included with the application. For any co-authored works submitted for consideration, the applicant’s contribution must be specified. An account of your pedagogical qualifications and experience, including a list of no more than 3 pedagogical texts authored by you, to be considered in the assessment. These texts must be included with the application. An account of qualifications and experience relating to leadership and administrative expertise and collaboration, including a list of no more than 3 reports or other texts authored by you in the areas of collaboration, leadership, or inquiries and report work to be considered in the assessment of the application. These reports/texts must be included with the application. Degree certificates, transcripts, other certificates, etc. A maximum of 10 research publications may be included with the application, together with a maximum of three teaching resources/pedagogical texts and a maximum of 3 reports/texts relevant to the assessment of collaboration, leadership, and administrative expertise. In addition to the information explicitly requested under the headings above, the application is expected to include, for example, a clear quantitative summary of previous teaching within the subject area, showing how teaching hours have been distributed over time and across different courses, as well as qualitative documentation (such as descriptions of development work and course materials) that illustrates and provides evidence of the applicant’s pedagogical expertise. The list of publications must be structured so that peer-reviewed journal articles, peer-reviewed book chapters, and peer-reviewed books are presented under separate headings. Degree certificates, transcripts, and other certificates must be submitted together as a single PDF file. Publications or other written works that cannot be submitted digitally with the application must be sent in duplicate to: Mikaela Hansson, Department of Pedagogical, Curricular, and Professional Studies, University of Gothenburg,  Box 300, 405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden. Please mark the envelope with the reference number for the position: PAR 2026/672. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41298": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age Reference number PAR 2026/517 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The position of Assistant Professor in Archaeology, with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age, is based at the Department of Historical Studies, which is part of the Faculty of Humanities. The Department conducts research in the fields of History, Archaeology, and Classical Archaeology and Ancient History. Teaching in these disciplines is offered at all levels, from undergraduate courses to doctoral education. In addition, undergraduate teaching is provided within the main field of Cultural Heritage Studies. The Department’s current educational mission comprises more than 730 full-time students. Research is carried out both within the individual disciplines and through larger interdisciplinary projects, where national and international collaboration forms an important component. Today, the Department employs nearly 100 researchers, teachers, doctoral students, and support staff. The Department is centrally located in Gothenburg. Subject area Archaeology with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age. Subject area description This position specializes in the Nordic Iron Age, that is, the period from approximately 500 BCE to 1050 CE. This era constitutes a strategic link within the Department’s research profile, connecting two strong areas of expertise: on the one hand, Stone and Bronze Age archaeology, and on the other, Medieval History. More information about the Department and the subject of Archaeology is available on our website: https://www.gu.se/historiska-studier . Duties The duties include research, teaching, and external engagement, as well as related administrative tasks. The position is primarily to be carried out on site at the University of Gothenburg, and the successful candidate is therefore expected to be physically present at the Department. The primary responsibility is to conduct and develop research in Archaeology with a specialization in the Nordic Iron Age. The successful candidate is expected to develop an independent research profile, actively seek external research funding, and contribute to the development of the Department’s research environment through national and international collaborations. In addition, the successful candidate is expected to engage in course development within the subject area. The duties also include teaching and supervision in Archaeology at the undergraduate and master's levels and, depending on qualifications and the needs of the Department, at the doctoral level. Teaching may encompass both chronological and thematic courses in Archaeology and may be conducted in Swedish and English. In addition, the position includes participation in the Department’s shared responsibilities and engagement with the wider community, for example through collaboration with the cultural heritage sector, museums, and other relevant societal stakeholders. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an associate senior lecturer. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates Ability to teach in Swedish (or another Scandinavian language) and English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Great importance will also be placed on personal suitability and strong collaborative skills. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Chronologically, the primary focus should be on the first millennium CE. Applicants are expected to demonstrate a critical and independent engagement with the current state of research. We also value expertise that facilitates collaboration with our strong research environments in Bronze Age and Medieval studies, particularly the latter. Extensive experience from academic institutions outside Sweden will be regarded as a strong asset. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Experience in leading and organizing archaeological field courses is also considered a merit, as is teaching experience relevant to the main field of Cultural Heritage Studies. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Historical Studies, Gothenburg, Sweden. Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. For more information, please visit the Faculty of Humanities' webpage: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Head of Department Henrik Janson at henrik.janson@history.gu.se . If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR officer Johanna Hagbranth at johanna.hagbranth@gu.se . Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The application must include: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a complete list of publications and an account of external research funding obtained (including amounts awarded). Research plan. The plan should clearly relate to the research area of the position and should be developed to a level that would support at least one competitive application for external research funding. A description of your research qualifications, including: a) A list of your five most significant scholarly publications. For co-authored publications, please specify your role and the extent of your contribution. For all co-authored publications, co-author statements must be provided. These statements should describe each author's contribution, both quantitatively and substantively, and must be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation verifying its acceptance must be enclosed. b) A concise account of your research achievements, research profile, and research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 3 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most significant scholarly publications. A description of your teaching qualifications, including: a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught, including courses and programmes at undergraduate, master's, and doctoral levels. The documentation should indicate the scope of your contributions. b) A concise account of your teaching merits and qualifications, addressing both their extent and quality, including a reflection on your teaching practice. (Maximum 3 pages.) c) Certificates documenting completed training in higher education pedagogy. d) Certificates verifying your teaching experience, issued by a Head of Department, Director of Studies, or equivalent representative at at least one university or department. e) Any pedagogical writings and publications (maximum 3 items). Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and student instructions (maximum 3 items). Please refer to these documents in your account of teaching merits and qualifications. Doctoral degree certificate (PhD diploma). Any additional certificates or documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Scholarly publications, written works, CVs, and similar materials that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: Department of Historical Studies Attn: HR Box 200 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41058": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in English with a Specialisation in Literary Studies",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in English with a Specialisation in Literary Studies For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in English with a Specialisation in Literary Studies Reference number PAR 2026/579 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Languages and Literatures is the largest department within the Faculty of Humanities, comprising 16 different languages, approximately 90 employees, around 1,090 full-time equivalent students, and an annual turnover of just over SEK 110 million. The department operates in modern facilities centrally located near Götaplatsen Subject area English with a specialisation in Literary Studies Subject area description English encompasses both language studies and literary studies and has degree-awarding powers at all levels, including doctoral education. In addition to stand-alone courses, English is included in several degree programmes at the department, such as the Bachelor’s Programme in English with a focus on working life, the Master’s Programme in Languages and Intercultural Communication, the Teacher Education Programme, and the Nordic Master’s Programme for Language Teachers. The subject offers both campus-based and online courses. Research spans both language studies and literary studies and includes applied research projects focusing, for example, on language learning and digital humanities. In addition, research in the discipline is conducted through interdisciplinary research environments. Duties An Assistant Professor will be given the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and teacher and to strengthen both their research and teaching qualifications in order to meet the eligibility requirements for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The duties include research amounting to at least 50% in accordance with an established research plan in English Literary Studies, and teaching and administration amounting to no more than 50%. The successful candidate is expected to follow developments within their own subject area, actively apply for external research funding, and contribute to the development of the department’s teaching and research environment. Applicants should therefore describe in their application how their own research can promote and contribute to the department’s research areas. The position also includes teaching, supervision and examination in English with a specialisation in Literary Studies at first- and second-cycle levels, both on campus and online, and both on stand-alone courses (for example, literary theory and method, literary and cultural history, and academic communication) and within the Teacher Education Programme (for example, school-based education/VFU and literature teaching methodology). Course development and administrative duties are also included, such as course coordination and work with the University’s learning platform. Evening teaching may occur. Teaching on interdisciplinary courses and programmes at the department may also be included, and the successful candidate is expected to be prepared to contribute to teaching in Swedish within two years. The main part of the work is to be carried out at the department at the University of Gothenburg, and the person appointed is expected to be present and to participate actively in the department’s development and working environment, including staff meetings and other internal meetings and conferences. Work in close dialogue with colleagues is an integral part of the role, and the person appointed is expected to contribute constructively and collegially both to the discipline and to the department as a whole. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in the subject area of the position or has corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved equivalent competence at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds include sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of research expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision and examination) at first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, particular emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its teaching, and consequently improved quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who, based on an overall assessment of expertise and development potential, is deemed suitable and to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. A research background in postcolonial and/or decolonial studies is also highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: the Department of Languages and Literatures, the Faculty of Humanities, Gothenburg Starting date: By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their research and teaching qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. An Assistant Professor employed under Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance shall, upon application, be promoted to Senior Lecturer if they are eligible for such an appointment and, following assessment, are deemed suitable for such an appointment in accordance with the assessment criteria established by the University in connection with the recruitment. Promotion also requires completion of supervisor training and higher education teaching and learning training as determined by the Vice-Chancellor, as well as fulfilment of the Swedish-language requirement determined by the Vice-Chancellor. The assessment criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer must be stated in the vacancy announcement for the Assistant Professorship. An application for promotion entails permanent employment as Senior Lecturer. For more information, see “Guidelines for applications for appointment as, or promotion to, Senior Lecturer at the Faculty of Humanities, 2026”, available at: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position . Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work-life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. In addition to external expert review, interviews, a trial lecture and reference checks will form part of the assessment. The trial lecture will be delivered in English and the subsequent interview will be conducted in English. Particular emphasis will be placed on the applicant’s ability to deliver a trial lecture in English within the advertised subject area and to participate in an interview in English, including an oral discussion of their research and teaching experience. Interviews and trial lectures will be conducted via Zoom and are planned for week 50. Trial lecture topic: The Societal Relevance of Literary Studies: How Can Your Research Profile Advance Knowledge in English Literary Studies and Contribute to Our Understanding of Pressing Societal Issues? Based on a field related to your own research, discuss and exemplify how English literary studies can contribute to the understanding of current societal challenges. The lecture should demonstrate how theoretical, methodological and/or historical perspectives within the subject can generate new knowledge, while simultaneously relating your own research focus to broader research questions within English literary studies. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that the application is complete when the application period closes. An incomplete application will be assessed on the basis of the documents submitted. The following documents must be attached to the application: Cover letter Research plan Curriculum vitae (CV), including a record of degrees and previous employment Description of research and teaching activities, including research and teaching reflections Qualifications portfolio I: Research expertise Qualifications portfolio II: Teaching expertise Qualifications portfolio III: Collaboration with the wider community Qualifications portfolio IV: Leadership and administrative expertise Publications submitted for consideration (maximum five), together with a separate list of the publications Doctoral degree certificate Doctoral thesis References: names and telephone numbers of at least two referees For further information about the application, see “Guidelines for applications for appointment as Assistant Professor at the Faculty of Humanities, 2026” (PDF), available at: https://www.gu.se/en/humanities/about-us/application-for-position Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Head of Department Petra Platen (prefektSPL@sprak.gu.se) or Director of Studies Anna-Lena Fredriksson ( anna- lena.fredriksson@sprak.gu.se). If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact the HR Officer at personalhandlaggare@sprak.gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41342": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Economics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Economics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Economics Reference number PAR 2026/642 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. With more than 3 700 full-time students, 470 employees, 160 international partner universities and four departments, the School of Business, Economics and Law has unique breadth. The School of Business, Economics and Law is “triple-crown” accredited - EQUIS, AACSB and AMBA. The Department of Economics is part of the School of Business, Economics and Law at the University of Gothenburg and brings together around 100 researchers and teachers from several countries. The department offers teaching in economics, finance, and statistics to around 800 students across courses and programmes. Our research spans a broad range of fields, including labour, health, applied microeconomics, environmental, development, behavioural, and financial economics. More information about the department is available at www.economics.gu.se . Department of Economics is seeking to appoint one or two Assistant Professors in Economics. Subject area Economics Subject area description Economics is a social science that studies how individuals, firms, and societies make decisions, how markets and institutions function, and how resources and opportunities are allocated. It combines theoretical reasoning and empirical methods to understand economic behaviour and outcomes, and to evaluate the effects of policies and institutions. The position is open to candidates working in any field of economics, with opportunities to contribute to both the department’s research and teaching. Duties The position comprises a maximum of 50 percent teaching and a minimum of 50 percent research. The successful candidate is expected to pursue high-quality research with the potential to make a significant contribution to international research and to publish in leading international academic journals. Active participation in research seminars and academic networks is expected. Teaching constitutes a central part of the position. The successful candidate will primarily teach core undergraduate courses in economics, such as macroeconomics, statistics, international trade, and financial economics. Teaching may also include courses at advanced and doctoral levels and in other areas of economics than those listed above. Swedish language courses are offered by the university for applicants who do not speak Swedish. Duties also include course development, course coordination, and administrative tasks related to teaching. The successful candidate is expected to actively contribute to the development and quality assurance of the department’s education, as well as to pedagogical development within the subject area. The successful candidate is also expected to contribute to the development of the department’s teaching and research environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg and the guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441). Qualifications The following qualifications are required for this position: The ability to teach undergraduate courses in fields like macroeconomics, international trade, or financial economics. The ability to independently initiate, conduct, and publish high-quality research. The ability to contribute positively on-site to the Department’s research, teaching, and working environment, as well as overall suitability for the position (see below). In addition to these requirements, the following qualifications are considered particularly meritorious: Previous experience of successfully teaching undergraduate courses in macroeconomics, international trade, or financial economics. Previous experience of successfully teaching courses in Swedish. Previous international academic experience and publications in well-regarded peer-reviewed journals in economics. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise. Significant importance will also be attached to teaching expertise and to the ability and willingness to take an active role in the department’s teaching at both undergraduate and advanced levels. Collaborative and administrative competence will also be taken into account. The assessment will consider the qualifications of the candidates as a whole and in relation to the requirements and needs of the position. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and active participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on the quality, originality, and international relevance of the applicant’s research and publication record. The assessment takes into account demonstrated familiarity with the subject area and previous research, research independence, creativity and originality in the chosen research direction, as well as the depth of analysis and scope of scientific output, taking into consideration the length of time since the doctoral degree was awarded and the research trajectory during this period. In addition to the scholarly works cited in the application, the following factors, among others, shall be given weight in the overall assessment of the applicant’s academic merits and research expertise: Documented active participation in the scholarly community, for example through the presentation of research findings at national and international conferences, seminars, and similar forums. Demonstrated ability to interact and collaborate constructively with other researchers. Demonstrated ability to develop and sustain an independent research agenda. Evidence of research potential and a strong research trajectory, including the ability to publish in leading international and field journals and other high-quality scholarly outlets. For further details, see the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441). Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. Previous experience of teaching core undergraduate or advanced level courses such as macroeconomics, international trade, or financial economics is considered meritorious. Also experience of teaching in Swedish is a merit. In the assessment of pedagogical competence, importance will also be attached to documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. For further details, see the Guidelines for the Employment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441) Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, active participation in seminars, mentoring, engagement in the development of teaching, and contributions to the overall development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment The position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position aimed at researchers and teachers who have completed their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who, based on an overall assessment of their qualifications and development potential, is assessed to have the strongest prospects for carrying out the duties of the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of the department’s activities. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:School of Business, Economics and Law at Department of Economics Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer Promotion to Senior Lecturer is regulated by the Higher Education Ordinance, the University of Gothenburg’s Appointment Regulations (GU 2024/3252), and the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Academic Staff at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg (GU 2025/3441). For an Assistant Professor (biträdande universitetslektor) to be promoted to Senior Lecturer (universitetslektor), an application for promotion must be submitted to the department no later than six months before the end of the five-year appointment period. In accordance with the guidelines “Appointment as Assistant Professor and Promotion from Assistant Professor to Senior Lecturer” (GU 2025/3441), at the Department of Economics, promotion to and permanent employment as a Senior Lecturer is based on demonstrated excellence in both research and teaching. During the appointment period, the candidate is expected to demonstrate clear and sustained progression in these two areas. External reviewers assess whether these criteria are met. Research progression is assessed based on a research record developed during the appointment period, which is expected to correspond to at least two publications in top field journals or high-quality general-interest journals in economics or finance. In addition, the assessment considers the candidate’s research independence, contribution to co-authored work, and overall research trajectory.  Active engagement in the research community, both within the department and externally, is expected, including seminar participation, mentoring, and related academic activities. Progression in teaching is for instance demonstrated through teaching excellence, course development, active on-site participation in teaching committees, and strong course evaluations. The overall assessment will be based on the candidate’s research and teaching merits, as well as their potential to contribute to the long-term development of the department. Promotion also requires completion of a university teaching and learning qualification programme approved by the Vice-Chancellor, as well as fulfillment of the Swedish language requirement established by the Vice-Chancellor ( Appointment Regulations for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg , GU 2024/3252) Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact:Head of Department Ola Olsson. ola.olsson@economics.gu.se , Tel: +0046 (0) 766 181341 If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact: HR Officer Neha Rohtagi, neha.rohtagi@economics@gu.se or (from 1/10) Axel Bondemark, axel.bondemark@economics.gu.se ,Tel: +0046 (0) 0766-18 27 01 Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. he following documents must be attached to the application: Curriculum Vitae (CV) , including a complete list of publications and an account of any external research funding received (including amounts). Research Plan. The plan must clearly relate to the research area of the position and should be capable of forming the basis for at least one competitive research grant application. A description of your research qualifications , including: a) A list of your five most important scientific publications. For publications co-authored with others, specify your role and the extent of your contribution. Statements from co-authors must be attached; these should include information on each author's contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming acceptance for publication must be attached. b) A brief account of your research achievements, research profile, and research plans for the coming years. (Maximum 2 pages.) Complete electronic copies of up to ten of your most important scientific publications. A description of your teaching qualifications , including: a) A list of the courses and degree programmes that you have developed and taught on (including courses and programmes at the undergraduate, master's, and doctoral levels). b) A brief account of your teaching achievements and qualifications, including information on both the scope and quality of your teaching, as well as teaching self-reflection. (Maximum 2 pages.) c) Documentation of completed higher education pedagogy training. d) Certification of your teaching experience from a Head of Department, Director of Studies, or equivalent in at least one university or department. Electronic copies of teaching materials , such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and student instructions (maximum three supporting documents). Refer to these documents in your statement of teaching achievements and qualifications (Section 5b). Doctoral degree certificate (PhD diploma). Reference letters from 2–3 people from your previous positions. Any other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Scientific publications that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Economics and Statistics Attn: Neha Rohtagi/Axel Bondemark Box 640 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Please indicate reference number PAR 2026/642 on all submitted materials. The publications must be received no later than 2026-10-30 Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=40963": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Social Work with a focus on crime prevention",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Social Work with a focus on crime prevention For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Social Work with a focus on crime prevention Reference number PAR 2026/549 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professor with an international background at the end of 2026. The university’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. The Department of Social Work is part of the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Gothenburg. It is one of the largest and most established academic environments for research and education in social work in the Nordic region. The Department has more than 130 employees and provides education at first-, second- and third-cycle levels. Its educational offerings include the Social Work Programme, master’s programmes in Social Work and human rights, and other further education. Research is conducted across several thematic areas in an active and expanding academic environment characterised by a strong commitment to society. The department also has extensive international partnerships and collaborates with research networks and universities worldwide. More information is available on the Department of Social Work website: gu.se/en/socwork We are currently seeking to appoint an Assistant Professor in Social Work who will contribute to strengthening our research and education environment. Subject area Social Work Subject area description Social work examines the living conditions and experiences of people in situations of social vulnerability. The subject also explores the causes and consequences of social problems and how these can be understood at individual, group and societal levels. Written communication and the use of language in different forms and contexts are central to professional social work practice. Social work is a main field of study at first- and second-cycle levels and a third-cycle subject. Duties The duties of an Assistant Professor include conducting research equivalent to at least 50 per cent of full-time. This includes identifying research questions, developing theoretical ideas, data collecting and choosing research methods. The position also includes actively seeking external funding for their own research and actively participating in the department's research environments and seminar activities. The duties also consist of education in the form of preparation for, implementation of, and follow-up work on various forms of teaching at undergraduate level, advanced level and possibly in doctoral education. Course and programme development, course responsibility, course administration and other pedagogical work are also included in the position. As an Assistant Professor, you will be part of the strategic research programme Building Resilience in Crime Prevention (BRiC): Advancing Professional Knowledge of Child Vulnerability, Drug Markets and Emerging Technologies. BRiC was launched in spring 2026 and is currently in its start-up phase. The programme is funded by the Swedish Research Council and is a collaboration between Lund University, the University of Gothenburg, Malmö University and Linnaeus University. BRiC brings together more than 60 researchers and aims to strengthen scientific and professional knowledge in crime prevention through research conducted in close collaboration with practitioners in social services and the criminal justice system. As an Assistant Professor, you will have the opportunity to help shape this emerging research environment and further develop your research and collaboration networks in the field of crime prevention. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in Social Work or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Proficiency in Swedish is not required for this position. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg . We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. The position requires: Documented teaching expertise in higher education, Documented, current and relevant experience of practice-based and/or professionally oriented research in social work, In the research plan, applicants are expected to formulate an innovative research project of high scientific quality within crime-preventive social work in a Swedish context. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within Social Work. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and administrative competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Particular emphasis will be placed on documented research merits with clear relevance to social work. Particular emphasis will also be placed on the quality of the submitted research plan. The research plan must clearly demonstrate how the project is connected to and contributes to social work, with a focus on practice-based and/or professionally oriented crime-preventive social work in a Swedish context. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Social Work, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden. Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. To assess whether an applicant is sufficiently qualified for promotion, an evaluation is carried out in accordance with the assessment criteria for employment as Associate Professor (Senior Lecturer) applied at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teaching Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103) . In such an evaluation, the following additional requirements for promotion apply: The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of scientific competence through: High-quality scientific publications in leading international peer-reviewed journals. In addition, high-quality scientific publications in peer-reviewed books or book chapters are also considered meritorious. Success in obtaining external research funding in national and/or international competition Contributions to the scientific community, for example by undertaking peer-review assignments, serving as an external examiner, faculty opponent, or as a member of an examination committee. Demonstrated progression in relation to the applicant’s scientific merits at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example by achieving Associate Professor qualifications. The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of pedagogical competence through: Teaching experience to at least 1,600 hours. Experience in course development and in various teaching methods. Completion of approved training in higher education pedagogy (courses PIL101–PIL103 or validation approved by the Dean), as well as supervisor training (PIL201 or validation approved by the Dean). Demonstrated progression in relation to the applicant’s pedagogical competence at the beginning of the employment as Assistant Professor, for example through teaching at different levels, within different subject areas, or through further specialization in previously covered areas. The applicant must have demonstrated collaboration and leadership skills through: Organising and participating in outreach-oriented scientific activities, such as workshops, panels, or conferences. Organising and participating in outreach activities in collaboration with wider society, for example conferences involving policymakers, service users, or organisations, or other forms of public engagement. Demonstrated ability to lead and organise research projects, educational activities, or other initiatives. Taking collegial responsibility for the department, for example through participation in review panels and working groups. The applicant must demonstrate personal suitability through: Sound professional judgement, the ability to perform the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate and contribute to the development of the organisation. Language requirements: Proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR). This must be demonstrated by a passing result on an approved language test or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that such a test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. The position is based in Gothenburg and requires the successful applicant to reside in and be registered in Sweden. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews and trial lectures will be held from mid-December to the beginning of January. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Therese Wissö, therese.wisso@socwork.gu.se and Tobias Davidsson, tobias.davidsson@socwork.gu.se. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Ebba Ståhl, HR Officer, ebba.stahl@socwork.gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. All stated merits must be supported by certificates. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The following documents should be attached to the application: Curriculum vitae/CV , including a full list of publications and a statement of external funding received (including amounts). Research plan , not exceeding 3 pages. The plan must clearly relate to crime-preventive social work in a Swedish context, and be capable of being developed into at least one competitive research funding application. A description of your scientific qualifications. a) A list of your five most significant scientific publications. For co-authored publications, the applicant’s contribution must be clearly specified. Please indicate your role and the extent of your contribution. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but not yet published, a certificate of accepted publication must be attached. b) A brief statement of your scientific merits and research profile (maximum 2 pages). Full electronic copies of up to five scientific publications. A statement of your teaching qualifications containing: a) A list of the courses and study programmes you have developed and taught at undergraduate, advanced (master's), and doctoral levels. b) A description of your pedagogical competences and qualifications, including information on their scope and quality. c) A description of your teaching material production (textbooks and other teaching material). d) A pedagogical self-reflection on your teaching philosophy and teaching practice (maximum 3 pages). e) Certificates documenting your training in higher education pedagogy, if applicable. f) Certificates of your teaching experience from a Head of Department/Director of Studies or equivalent at at least one university or department. Complete copies of a maximum of three works from your teaching materials production , if applicable. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and instructions for students (maximum 3 items). Please refer to these documents in your statement of teaching merits and qualifications under section 5.b. Doctoral degree certificate . Other certificates and documents that you wish to submit in support of your application . Publications that cannot be submitted digitally must be sent in two identical copies to: University of Gothenburg, Department of Social Work, Att: Ebba Ståhl, Box 720, 405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden. Please state the reference number: PAR 2026/549. Application deadline : 30 October 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41331": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance Reference number PAR 2026/658 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Swedish Research Council will announce an attractive funding opportunity for Assistant Professors with an international background at the end of 2026. The University’s ambition is to nominate strong candidates from this recruitment initiative to the Swedish Research Council. With approximately 3,700 full-time students, 470 employees, 160 international partner universities and four departments, the School of Business, Economics and Law offers exceptional breadth and is Triple Crown accredited through EQUIS, AACSB and AMBA. The Department of Business Administration is the School’s largest department and conducts extensive research and education at first-cycle, second-cycle and doctoral levels. The position is based in the Industrial and Financial Management & Logistics (IFEL) section, which conducts research and education in areas including corporate finance and collaborates closely with research environments such as the Centre for Finance (CFF), Northern Lead and Lighthouse. Subject area Business Administration, specialising in Corporate Finance. Subject area description Corporate Finance examines firms’ financial decision-making, with a focus on investment decisions, financing strategies, and mechanisms for ensuring a market-based return for both shareholders and lenders. Central to the field is the development of theoretically and empirically grounded frameworks that facilitate profitable investments and efficient financing while taking risk and the cost of capital into account. Particular emphasis is placed on analyzing the factors that influence firms’ choices of capital structure and investment strategies, especially in environments characterized by asymmetric information and transaction costs. A clear grounding in financial theory is essential for understanding, explaining, and analyzing these decisions and their consequences. The field has a strong methodological orientation, employing quantitative tools such as discounted cash flow models, real and financial option models, as well as statistical and econometric methods, to analyze how external market conditions affect firms’ financial decisions. Duties The position comprises a maximum of 50% teaching and a minimum of 50% research. Research is expected to focus on Corporate Finance, align with the subject area description above, and be of a quality suitable for publication in high-quality international scientific journals. Research is also expected to be actively disseminated through research seminars and academic networks. The Assistant Professor will teach at first-cycle and second-cycle (Master’s) levels and is also expected to contribute to doctoral education and contract education, as well as course development and administrative duties. Teaching will primarily concern courses in Corporate Finance, including Corporate Valuation, Advanced Corporate Finance, Financial Management, or similar courses, as well as supervision of Bachelor’s and Master’s theses in finance and within Accounting and Financial Management. The position also requires active efforts to apply for external research funding during the employment period and to contribute to the development of the department’s educational and research environment. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg GU 2024/3252. To be eligible for appointment, the candidate must have been awarded a doctoral degree in Business Administration specialising in Corporate Finance, or have corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to candidates who were awarded their doctoral degree or achieved equivalent competence no more than five years before the application deadline. Candidates who achieved such competence earlier may also be considered if there are special grounds, such as sick leave, parental leave, or similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg GU 2024/3252. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research in Corporate Finance. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. Particular emphasis will be placed on high-quality theoretical and/or empirical research in Corporate Finance and publication in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability and potential to conduct further high-quality research during the appointment, and the ability to obtain external research funding in competition, are meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of planning and delivering teaching in various forms, including lectures, seminars, and supervision, at first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area. Particular emphasis is placed on the quality of teaching, potential for continued pedagogical development, and documented contributions to the development of courses, study programmes, or teaching methods. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community may be demonstrated through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, and through communicating research findings to the public. Administrative competence includes experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, and contributions to the development of the department or faculty. Personal qualities, including the ability to collaborate effectively within the department and with external partners, will also be considered when assessing suitability. Overall Assessment An Assistant Professorship is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who, based on an overall assessment of expertise and development potential, is considered suitable and to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties of the position, collaborating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of the department. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Business Administration, School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg Starting date: By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop independence as a researcher and strengthen scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established promotion criteria (GU 2025/3441). An application for promotion must be submitted to the department no later than six months before the end of the appointment period. At the time of application for promotion, the applicant must demonstrate research expertise and clear progression in research qualifications and achievements during the appointment. The assessment considers familiarity with the subject area and prior research, creativity and originality, analytical depth and breadth, scientific quality, independence, and forward-looking development. Active participation in the scientific community, academic service, constructive research collaboration, and societal impact of research may also be taken into account. For teaching qualifications, the applicant must have completed higher education teaching and learning training in accordance with University requirements, or have equivalent documented knowledge, and must demonstrate substantial high-quality teaching experience at first-cycle and/or second-cycle level during the appointment. The assessment includes documented teaching qualifications, pedagogical reflection and development, experience from different educational environments and teaching formats, and, where relevant, course and programme leadership and production of teaching materials. For internationally recruited Assistant Professors, promotion to Senior Lecturer requires successful completion of Swedish language studies at a minimum of level B2 according to the CEFR, in accordance with the Vice-Chancellor’s decision (Ref. GU 2022/1887). Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work-life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the CEFR, you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned after completion of the external expert assessment. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Taylan Mavruk, Professor and Head of the IFEL Section, taylan.mavruk@handels.gu.se, +46 (0)31 786 5958, or Jonas Flodén, Associate Professor and Deputy Head of the IFEL Section, jonas.floden@handels.gu.se, +46 (0)31 786 5131. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR Officer Natalie Axell at natalie.axell@gu.se. Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available on the University website. Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The application must include: Curriculum Vitae, including a complete publication list, copies of degree certificates and relevant certificates, and an account of external research funding obtained. A letter of intent/research plan (maximum three pages) describing how you intend to carry out the duties and develop your research in Corporate Finance during the appointment. An account of research qualifications, previous research activities, research profile and contributions (maximum two pages). PhD thesis and up to four additional scholarly works. An account of teaching qualifications, including higher education teacher training, previous teaching assignments and a pedagogical reflection (maximum two pages). Relevant course evaluations or equivalent documentation may be attached. A brief account of achievements in management, collaboration, and administration. At least two references, including name, title, current higher education institution/organisation, and contact details. Other certificates and documents that you wish to invoke. All merits must be documented and certificates attested. Attachments should be named so that their content is clearly identifiable. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41227": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Mathematics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Mathematics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Mathematics Reference number PAR 2026/400 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Mathematical Sciences at the University of Gothenburg and Chalmers University of Technology has about 200 employees and is one of the largest departments of mathematical sciences in the Nordic countries. The department consists of three divisions: Algebra and Geometry, Analysis and Probability, Applied Mathematics and Statistics. We contribute in a significant way to society by our extensive research and education. The working environment at the department is supportive, stimulating, and international, with a continuous flow of international guests. Equality and diversity are substantial foundations in all our activities. More information about us is available on the website: http://www.chalmers.se/en/departments/math/ This is a \"tenure-track\" position which comes with a start-up package that includes funding for a doctoral student, as well as funding for travel and inviting guest researchers. Subject area Mathematics Subject area description We are looking for an Assistant Professor in mathematics to contribute to the department's research activities at the division of Algebra and Geometry. Division of Algebra and Geometry | Chalmers The new employee is expected to independently develop and conduct research at a high international level. The position also includes teaching at 20%, this corresponds to one course including examination, a total of about 10 weeks, per year. We expect the new employee to contribute to strengthening and complementing the research in mathematics conducted at the division of Algebra and Geometry. The department has dedicated teachers with extensive and broad experience in all aspects of undergraduate and postgraduate education in mathematics. We are appreciated by students and continuously seek opportunities for pedagogical development. Our ambition is that our teaching in mathematics will be conducted at the highest international level. Duties The duties include developing and conducting research in mathematics, supervising students (at bachelor's, master's and doctoral levels), actively seeking external grants, participating in the department's activities through administrative assignments, teaching mathematics at both undergraduate and graduate levels. Communication and collaboration skills are important as many of your tasks will be carried out in collaboration with others. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. A PhD or equivalent foreign degree in a subject area relevant to the position is a requirement. Primary consideration should be given to applicants who have been awarded a PhD or achieved equivalent expertise no more than seven years before the application deadline for the position as Assistant Professor. Applicants who were awarded a PhD or achieved equivalent expertise earlier may also be considered if there are special grounds. Such grounds include leave due to illness, parental leave, or other similar circumstances. The reasons for the absence, its dates, and its extent must be clearly specified in the application and supported by appropriate documentation. The official language at the University of Gothenburg is Swedish and teaching is normally in Swedish at bachelor's level and in English at higher levels. Therefore, good language skills and the ability to teach in English are required. Assessment criteria Primary consideration will be given to applicants’ scientific qualifications, although pedagogical skills will also be taken into account. In the assessment of scientific proficiency, particular attention will be paid to research documented through high-quality publications. Quality is considered more important than quantity. Successful research after the PhD and international scientific collaboration are considered merits. In the assessment of pedagogical merits, particular emphasis will be placed on the ability to plan and teach courses in mathematics. The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. International experience, as well as experience from academic environments other than the one in which the doctoral degree was obtained, is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Department of Mathematical Sciences, Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The position comes with a start-up package which includes funding for a PhD student as well as for own travel and to invite guests, thereby allowing the candidate to start building their own research group. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. The assessment criteria for promotion as a teacher are set out in the Higher Education Ordinance, the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg, and the Faculty of Science and Technology’s governing document on assessment criteria for recruitment and promotion of teachers. (GU 2025/1411) Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place on December 17. Contact information Bernt Wennberg, Head of Department, tel +46 (0)31-772 5326, email wennberg@chalmers.se Daniel Persson, Head of Division, tel +46 (0)31-772 3174, email danper@chalmers.se Lars Martin Sektnan, Head of Unit, tel +46 (0)31-772 3719, email sektnan@chalmers.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the ‘Apply’ button. In order to apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to register an account in our online recruitment system. Together with your application, the following should be attached: Personal letter. CV, including list of publications. Certificate of doctoral degree and other documents that you wish to be considered. Copies of your most relevant publications in full format (maximum six publications) together with motivation for your choice and an account of your contribution to the publications. A 4-year research plan with a description of current and planned research as well as other planned activities relevant to this position (maximum four pages). Description of pedagogical expertise, including information about the extent and level of teaching done and evaluations of its quality. It should also include a short pedagogical reflection (maximum four pages). Description of your experience of leadership, administration and cooperation with the surrounding community. Two references whom we can contact. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure that the application is complete in accordance with the instructions in the job advertisement, and that it is submitted before the deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be sent to: University of Gothenburg Department of Mathematical Sciences Att: Pernilla Junedahl Chalmers tvärgata 3 S-412 96 Gothenburg Sweden Stating PAR 2026/400. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41183": {
      "title": "Educational Assistant",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Educational Assistant For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Educational Assistant Reference number PAR 2026/618 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Educational Assistant Duties The Department of Earth Sciences is seeking Teaching Assistants for intermittent employment (hourly employment as needed) from November 2026 through April 2027. The work involves assisting with laboratory sections of courses in geography, geology and earth sciences. The tasks include, among other things, preparing, in consultation with the course coordinator, laboratory sessions including materials, which can take place both at the department and in the field. In the implementation of laboratory work, it can include presenting the activity and supervising students. Tasks may include grading students' labs and other undergraduate assignments. Some participation in research as support to various researchers may also occur. As a teaching assistant, there may be tasks involving contact with prospective students (undergraduate or master’s level). For example, you may be assigned have a possible student “shadow you for a class”, participate as a student ambassador when a school class comes to visit or other forms of collaboration, as well as contribute with photos/films to our digital channels where you give an insight into what studying geosciences can look like. The tasks may also include reorganization of the premises, relocation and inventory. Qualifications We are looking for students within our own Department of Earth Sciences. Applicants must have completed the course GV1410 or GE0400. Additional requirements may apply depending on the work tasks. A completed bachelor's programme in geography or geosciences is an advantage.  Alternatively, current master’s Earth Science program students with an equivalent to GV1410 or GE0400 are also welcome to apply. For teaching tasks, the following is desired: good knowledge of Swedish at level C1 and English and good computer skills. You can easily familiarise yourself with specific problems and come up with an alternative solution that you can communicate to students. Previous experience in supervision is a merit. Employment The position is an intermittent position, which means varying hours as needed during the period November 2026 through April 2027. Placement in the Department of Earth Sciences. Your official job title at the university will be assistant. Selection process Employment will take place continuously during the application period. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Eugenia Andersson, HR Administrator, HR@gvc.gu.se Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You apply for the position via the University of Gothenburg´s recruitment portal by clicking on the “Apply” button. You as an applicant are responsible for ensuring that the application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is received by the University no later than the application deadline. Applications must be received by: October 4, 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41172": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution Reference number PAR 2026/537 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Sociology and Work Science is home to one of Sweden's leading research groups in criminology. In conflict resolution, researchers and practioners work together in a well-integrated environment focused on empirical studies of crime and conflict. The Department has approximately 110 employees and conducts internationally regocnized in areas such as social inequality, work, organizations, welfare, and education in close collaboration with the wider community. For more information, please visit the department’s website . Subject area Assistant Professor in Sociology with a Specialization in Criminology and Conflict Resolution Subject area description The field encompasses sociological research on crime, social control, conflict, conflict management, conflict resolution, and related processes that contribute to the understanding of contemporary societies. The position is intended to strengthen the department's research and teaching within these areas and to contribute to the development of internationally competitive research environments. Duties The Assistant Professor is expected to conduct research of the highest international standard within criminology, conflict resolution, or closely related areas of sociological research. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop an independent research profile with the potential to attain international recognition and leadership in the field. The successful candidate is expected to: develop and lead an independent research agenda publish in leading international academic journals and other scholarly outlets actively pursue external research funding establish and develop national and international research collaborations contribute to the department's research environment teach at first-cycle (undergraduate), second-cycle (Master's) and doctoral levels contribute to research-based development and academic advancement of the department's educational in conflict resolution Particular emphasis will be placed on the candidate’s ability to strengthen the scientific foundation of the department's activities in conflict resolution by integrating current research with education and knowledge development in the field. An appointment as an Assistant Professor includes research duties amounting to at least 50% of the total working time. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Gothenburg, Sweden Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. To assess whether a candidate is sufficiently qualified for promotion, an evaluation will be conducted in accordance with the assessment criteria for appointment as Associate Professor (Senior Lecturer) at the University of Gothenburg, as set out in the Guidelines for the Appointment and Promotion of Teaching Staff at the Faculty of Social Sciences (GU 2025/1103). The following additional requirements shall also apply when assessing eligibility for promotion: The applicant must have demonstrated a high level of research excellence through: publication of high-quality research in leading international peer-reviewed journals. In addition, publication of high-quality research in peer-reviewed books or book chapters will be considered a merit. success in obtaining external research funding in national and/or international competition. contributions to the scholarly community, for example through service as a peer reviewer, external examiner of doctoral theses, or member of an examining committee for doctoral dissertations. demonstrated progression in research qualifications during the period of employment as Assistant Professor, for example through achieving the qualifications required for appointment as Docent (Associate Professor). The applicant must have demonstrated teaching excellence through: teaching experience equivalent to at least 1,600 hours. experience in course development and in a variety of teaching and learning formats. completion of accredited training in teaching and learning in higher education (PIL101-PIL103 or equivalent qualifications validated by decision of the Dean), as well as doctoral supervision training (PIL201 or equivalent qualifications validated by decision of the Dean). demonstrated progression in teaching competence during the period of employment as Assistant Professor, for example through experience of teaching at different educational levels, across different subject areas, or through further development and specialization within previous areas of teaching. The applicant must have demonstrated engagement, collaboration, and leadership through: organising and participating in outward-facing scholarly activities, such as workshops, panels, or conferences. organising and participating in outreach and engagement activities in collaboration with stakeholders outside academia, such as conferences involving policymakers, practitioners, or civil society organisations, or other activities directed towards the wider public. demonstrating the ability to lead and coordinate research projects, educational activities, or other forms of academic work. contributing to the collegial governance and development of the department, for example through active participation in the department's seminar series, evaluation committees, working groups, or similar bodies. The applicant must demonstrate personal suitability through: sound judgement in professional matters, the ability to carry out the duties associated with the position, and the ability to collaborate effectively and contribute to the development of the department and its activities. The applicant must meet the following language requirement: proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of CEFR level B2. This must be demonstrated either through a passed language test in accordance with the requirements specified here (Common European Framework of Reference for Languages: Language Skills) or through certification from the Head of Department stating that such a language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be promoted to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits| University of Gothenburg. If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during week 51-52 (December 16 to December 23, 2026). Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact head of department, Kristian Daneback, kristian.daneback@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact human resource officer Anna Karin Wiberg, ph +46 31 786 3156, anna-karin.wiberg@gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Application You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg's recruitment portal by clicking the \"Apply\" button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring their application is complete in accordance with the advertisment and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The following documents must be attached to the application: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a complete list of publications and an account of externally funded research grants obtained (including amounts awarded). Research Plan. The plan must clearly relate to the research area of the position and be formulated so that it could form the basis of at least one competitive external research funding application (maximum 3 pages). A statement of your scientific qualifications, including: a) A list of your five most significant scientific publications. For publications co-authored with others, specify your role and the extent of your contribution. Co-author statements must be enclosed and should describe each author's contribution (both quantitative and substantive) and be signed by all co-authors. If an article or book has been accepted for publication but has not yet been published, documentation confirming acceptance for publication must be provided. b) A concise account of your scientific merits, research profile, and research plans for the coming years (maximum 2 pages). Complete electronic copies of up to five of your most significant scientific publications. A statement of your pedagogical qualifications, including: a) A list of the courses and educational programmes you have developed and taught, including courses and programmes at the undergraduate, master's/advanced, and doctoral levels. b) A concise account of your teaching merits and pedagogical qualifications, addressing both the scope and quality of your teaching experience, including a reflective statement on your teaching practice (maximum 2 pages). c) Certificates documenting completed higher education teaching and learning training. d) Documentation of your teaching experience issued by a Head of Department, Director of Studies, or equivalent representative at at least one university or academic department. Electronic copies of teaching materials, such as course syllabi, reading lists, examination assignments, lecture slides, and student instructions (maximum three supporting documents). These materials should be referenced in your statement of teaching merits and pedagogical qualifications (Section 5b). Doctoral degree diploma. Any additional certificates or documents that you wish to submit in support of your application. Publications that cannot be submitted electronically must be sent in two identical hard copies to: University of Gothenburg Department of Sociology and Work Science Attn: Anna-Karin Wiberg Box 720 SE-405 30 Gothenburg Sweden Please state reference number PAR 2026/537. The application must be received no later than 30 October 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41403": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Chemistry, with a specialization in chemical glycobiology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Chemistry, with a specialization in chemical glycobiology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Chemistry, with a specialization in chemical glycobiology Reference number PAR 2026/591 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology at the University of Gothenburg conducts high-quality research and education in chemical sciences and molecular life sciences. Our research and education focus on a fundamental understanding of chemical and biological processes, ranging from the molecular and cellular level to human health and the environment. The Department provides an international and interdisciplinary research environment, bringing together expertise in chemistry, molecular biology and related areas. Research spans a broad range of fields , including organic and medicinal chemistry, analytical and physical chemistry, biochemistry, molecular and cell biology, structural biology and biomolecular interactions. The Department is also responsible for education at Bachelor's, Master's and doctoral levels and contributes to several educational programmes within the Faculty of Science and Technology. The Department has approximately 200 employees and is located in Natrium at Campus Medicinareberget, providing a modern research and teaching environment with access to advanced research infrastructure and close proximity to other research environments within the Faculty of Science and Technology, and the Sahlgrenska Academy. We are now looking for an Assistant Professor with tenure track in Chemistry, specializing in Chemical Glycobiology. Following a positive evaluation, the department is responsible for offering long-term employment in a faculty position. The recruitment will strengthen the interface between chemistry and molecular life sciences at the department and contribute to the further development of research and education in molecular and chemical approaches to biological systems. The position comes with a generous package with full financial support for the Assistant Professor’s own salary, plus two to three additional recruitments (PhD student or postdoc) and running costs. Subject area Chemistry with a specializing in chemical glycobiology Subject area description Chemical glycobiology concerns the development and application of chemical approaches to understand the structures, dynamics and biological functions of glycans and glycoconjugates. The field integrates synthetic and analytical chemistry with chemical biology and molecular and cell biology to investigate how glycosylation influences molecular recognition, signalling, membrane organisation and biological function in health and disease. Relevant approaches include carbohydrate and bioconjugation chemistry, metabolic and bioorthogonal labelling, chemical probe design, glycan editing, and mass-spectrometry-based glycomics and glycoproteomics. By enabling the identification, characterisation and manipulation of glycans and glycoconjugates in complex biological systems, chemical glycobiology provides a molecular link between chemistry, glycobiology and membrane biology. At the Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology, the subject will complement and connect existing strengths in medicinal and analytical chemistry, biochemistry, glycobiology, proteomics and membrane biology. Available department infrastructure includes advanced microscopes, facilities for culturing mammalian cells and microbes, high throughput/high content phenotypic cell-based screening, equipment for purification of small molecules and macromolecules, NMR, lasers, electron microscopy, and imaging mass spectrometry. An important opportunity is to integrate experimental glycoscience with computational and AI-based approaches for predicting and interpreting molecular structure, interactions and function, in interaction with existing research groups in the department. The position is associated with the government-funded strategic research area CEMERAI (Centre for Modelling Life at the Membrane with AI), coordinated by Stockholm University, bringing together world-leading expertise in experimental and computational approaches to understand cellular membranes and their molecular components. Within this environment, chemical glycobiology provides experimental approaches and quantitative molecular information needed to understand the role of glycans and glycosylation in membrane organisation and function. A central aim is to develop and implement chemical tools to enable mechanistic studies of glycans and glycosylation in biological systems. Duties The main purpose of the position is to enable the successful candidate to establish an independent and internationally competitive research profile in chemical glycobiology and to develop the scientific and pedagogical qualifications required for promotion to Senior Lecturer within five years. The position primarily involves research, including: Establishing and developing an independent research profile in chemical glycobiology that complements and strengthens existing research at the department. Developing chemical and analytical approaches for studying glycans and glycoconjugates in biological systems. Scientific publication and active participation in national and international research collaborations. Applying for and obtaining external research funding. Supervision of students at undergraduate, master's and doctoral levels. Participation in interdisciplinary collaborations within CEMERAI and other relevant research environments at the University of Gothenburg and beyond. The position also includes teaching, course development and examination within the Department's educational programmes comprising chemistry, molecular biology and pharmaceutical programmes. The successful candidate is expected to contribute to undergraduate and advanced-level teaching in chemistry according to the needs of the department. This may include teaching in biochemistry, organic, and medicinal chemistry and related areas, as well as supervision of bachelor's and master's degree projects. Contributions to development of master’s level courses in chemical biology and related areas will be welcomed by the department. The successful candidate is also expected to participate in and contribute to the development of doctoral-level education. Teaching will initially comprise approximately 20 % of full-time employment. The distribution between research, teaching and other duties may develop during the period of employment in accordance with the needs of the department and the career development of the successful candidate. The position further includes research administration and project management, departmental service, societal collaboration and interaction, and participation in relevant research networks and research infrastructures. Competence development is included in the position, particularly training in teaching and learning in higher education and supervision corresponding to 20 higher education credits (PIL101, PIL102, PIL103 and PIL201, or equivalent). For candidates who do not speak Swedish, competence development in Swedish is also included. The University of Gothenburg will provide Swedish language courses, which are mandatory within the framework of the employment. The holder of the position is expected to develop independence as a researcher and to gain merits, both scientifically and pedagogically, to meet the eligibility requirements for a position as senior lecturer, prior to which the application for a docentship (‘docentur’ in Swedish) is a milestone. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in subject or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates The ability to teach in English is a requirement. More specifically, the position requires: A doctoral degree in chemistry or a closely related subject, or equivalent scientific competence, relevant to the subject area of chemical glycobiology. Documented scientific competence relevant to chemical glycobiology or a closely related research area. Postdoctoral experience, preferably at least two years, at a university, college, research institute or equivalent. Very good ability to communicate in English, both orally and in writing. The applicant must demonstrate the personal qualities required to carry out the duties of the position, collaborate with colleagues and contribute positively to the research, educational and working environment of the Department. For candidates who do not speak Swedish, development of written and oral proficiency in Swedish (proficiency level B2 according to CEFR, Common European Framework of Reference for Languages) is required at least six months before the time of promotion to Senior lecturer in order to enable participation in teaching and other departmental activities conducted in Swedish. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are looking for candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality research within the subject area of the position. Assessment will be based primarily on research expertise, secondly on teaching expertise, and thirdly on collaborative and administrative competence. The faculty for natural science and technology applies the below criteria in assessment of scientific and pedagogical skills, and general suitability. Scientific and pedagogical competence shall be sufficient relative to subject content and work tasks. Research expertise Research expertise is demonstrated through scholarly publications and current participation in national and international research collaborations. In the assessment of scientific expertise, particular emphasis is placed on high-quality publications with prestigious international publishers and in respected international peer-reviewed journals. The quality, originality, and impact of publications are given greater emphasis than the total number of publications. The ability to obtain external research funding in competition is highly meritorious. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of having planned and implemented teaching in various forms (lectures, seminars, supervision) at the first-cycle, second-cycle and/or doctoral level within the subject area of the position. When assessing teaching expertise, special emphasis is placed on documented teaching work that has contributed to the development of a course or study programme and its instruction and consequently improved the quality Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location:Department of Chemistry and Molecular biology Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled to be considered for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship, provided that the individual is qualified for such an appointment and is deemed suitable following an assessment based on the established criteria for promotion to Senior Lecturer. Criteria for promotion from assistant professor to senior lecturer During the employment period, no later than six months before it ends, the Assistant professor is expected to apply to be promoted to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. It is to be noted that the application for promotion must be made within the same subject in which the applicant is employed. The requirements for promotion to Senior Lecturer can be found in the procedures of the Faculty of Science and Technology (V 2018/117) and is summarized as follows: The applicant should be eligible for a position as Senior Lecturer in accordance with the Rules of Appointment of the University and Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance The applicant should be deemed suitable for such a position according to the assessment criteria established by the Faculty of Science and Technology, which means that applicant should: Hold a docentship (‘docentur’ in Swedish) in the subject area Have undertaken pedagogic and supervision training in teaching and learning in higher education equivalent to 20 credits (PIL 101, PIL 102, PIL 103, and PIL 201 or equivalent) Have been graded at least 4 (excellent) on a 5-point scale, regarding both research and teaching skills, as of assessed by external experts. In addition to the requirements above, the suitability of the candidate for promotion will be assessed based on: Competence in Swedish in speech and writing that enables participation in teaching at basic level. Ability to supervise/assist students/doctoral students at different levels The personal suitability and ability to interact with other staff and positively contribute to the work environment at the department Ability to actively and independently apply for and successfully obtain external funding. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/employment-benefits If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. For candidates who do not speak Swedish, a development of proficiency in oral and written Swedish to level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR) is expected, at least six months before promotion to Senior Lecturer, in order to enable participation in education and other departmental tasks that are performed in Swedish. The University of Gothenburg offers courses in Swedish that are compulsory within the framework of the employment. Personnel recruited internationally will be offered a relocation package and an advisory meeting about taxes / Global Mobility. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews will take place Tuesday , December 15, 2026 . Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact assistant Head of Department, professor Per Sunnerhagen, phone: +46705294180 e-mail: per.sunnerhagen@cmb.gu.se. If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact hr@cmb.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. The selection of candidates is made based on the qualifications registered in the application. Together with your application, the following should be attached: Cover letter CV, including a complete list of publications and a record of research grants received (with amounts). Description of scientific expertise and a list of ten selected scientific publications and why you chose to refer to each of them in your application. A research plan with a description of current and planned research as well as other planned activities relevant to this position (maximum five pages). Description of educational expertise, including information about the extent and level of your teaching experience and evaluations of the quality of your teaching. The description should also include a reflection on your educational approach and teaching philosophy. Description of experience of administrative duties such as collaborative projects and/or leadership or management positions. Copies of your most relevant publications in full format (maximum ten publications). Doctoral certificate and other certificates and documents to be considered. Any documents that are not available in electronic format should be clearly marked with the position’s reference number PAR 2026/591 and sent in copies to: University of Gothenburg Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology, HR Box 462 405 30 Göteborg Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41414": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation Reference number PAR 2026/679 The University of Gothenburg offers a research environment where internationally recognised excellence meets an open and collaborative culture. Here, early-career researchers are given the conditions to grow, build networks, and contribute to research with global relevance. This position is part of the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment initiative Gothenburg Global Scholars. Learn more about the initiative and the University here: Gothenburg Global Scholars. The Artistic Faculty consists of the departments HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design and  Academy of Music and Drama. As a combined faculty of art and learning, we are an independent, visionary and critical force in the necessary work to create a more sustainable and open society. The Academy of Music and Drama offers education in music and the performing arts and conducts research in artistic and educational practices. The Academy provides an environment where artistic practice, education and research intersect, and where collaboration takes place across genres, disciplines and professional fields. We are now seeking an Assistant Professor in Musical Performance and Interpretation, specialising in improvisation and/or composition. You will join the Music Unit, where improvisational, compositional and technology-based practices come together in education and artistic practice. The Academy has a well-established environment for research and doctoral education in Music Performance and Creation, with projects in improvisation, composition, interpretation, sound and media, as well as collaborations across artistic fields and academic disciplines. The Academy is also home to the Lindblad Studio, an experimental laboratory for sound, media, music technology and computer-assisted composition. More about the Academy of Music and Drama: https://www.gu.se/en/music-drama Subject area Musical Performance and Interpretation Subject area description Musical Performance and Interpretation encompasses artistic research in which musical practice is an integral and indispensable part of knowledge creation. Research in the subject area explores questions relating to the creation of music and sound, musical practice, artistic processes, performance, listening, materiality, technology, notation, embodiment, interpretation, musical interaction, and the relationship between music and its social and cultural contexts. For this position, the focus is on improvisational and/or compositional practices, understood in a broad sense. This may include improvisation rooted in, for example, Nordic jazz traditions, free improvisation, electronic music, or other experimental or tradition-based forms of musical expression. It may also include composition with an emphasis on areas such as electroacoustic music, sound art, music and sound production, live electronics, generative or interactive systems, digital instruments, and other artistic methods for creating music and sound. We welcome applicants with a range of profiles and areas of emphasis within the field. Applicants may be primarily rooted in improvisation, composition, or at the intersection of these areas. Duties The Assistant Professor will conduct and further develop independent artistic research in Musical Performance and Interpretation, primarily rooted in improvisation, composition, or the intersection of these areas. The duties include developing and carrying out artistic research projects; documenting, critically reflecting on, and communicating research processes and outcomes in relevant artistic and academic formats; and actively seeking external research funding. The candidate is expected to initiate and participate in national and international collaborations and to contribute actively to the Academy’s research environment. The duties also include teaching and supervision at first-cycle and second-cycle levels and, where applicable, at doctoral level. Teaching will be within the subject area of Musical Performance and Interpretation, with the main emphasis on the candidate’s area of expertise. The candidate is expected to contribute to the development of programme content and teaching formats, and to foster a close and critically reflective relationship between artistic practice, research, and teaching. The duties also include collaboration with the professional music and cultural sectors, participation in the Academy’s collegial work, and administrative duties. At least 50% of the position is allocated to research. Responsibilities and tasks may change over time. Eligibility Eligibility for appointment as Assistant Professor is governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. Qualification requirements for employment as an Assistant Professor are fulfilled by someone who has been awarded a doctorate in Musical Performance and Interpretation or has the corresponding research competence. Primary consideration should be given to someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent competence within seven years of the deadline for application for employment as an Assistant Professor. However, someone who has been awarded a doctorate or achieved the equivalent expertise at an earlier date may also be considered if there are special grounds. Special grounds are sick leave, parental leave or other similar circumstances. The reason, dates, and extent of the absence must be clearly specified in the application and supported by documentation/certificates. The applicant must also demonstrate documented experience of artistic practice at a high level in improvisation and/or composition, evidenced by publicly presented works, productions, performances, or comparable artistic commissions. The ability to teach in English is a requirement. Assessment criteria The assessment criteria for appointment as Assistant Professor are governed by the Higher Education Ordinance and the Appointments Procedure for Teachers at the University of Gothenburg. We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated the ability to independently initiate, develop, and conduct high-quality artistic research within the subject area of the position. Both artistic research expertise and teaching expertise will be assessed. In the overall ranking of candidates, the greatest weight will be given to artistic research expertise, followed by teaching expertise. The ability to collaborate, contribute to collegial work, and carry out administrative duties will also be taken into account. Artistic research expertise Expertise in artistic research is assessed according to the standards and publication practices that apply within the field of artistic research. Public performances, artistic presentations, participation in internationally curated projects, and dissemination through internationally recognised artistic platforms may therefore count as forms of publication and knowledge dissemination comparable to peer-reviewed publication in other disciplines. Artistic research expertise is demonstrated through publications and ongoing participation in national and international research collaborations. In assessing artistic research expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on: high-quality publications grounded in the applicant’s own strong artistic practice; the ability to formulate competitive research projects and secure external research funding on a competitive basis; documented artistic practice at a high level in improvisation and/or composition, with particular consideration given to its scope, quality, independence, and relevance to the subject area of the position. Applicants are not required to have comparable qualifications in both improvisation and composition. The assessment will be based on the quality of the applicant’s individual profile and its relevance to the field and to the Academy’s activities. Teaching expertise Teaching expertise is demonstrated through documented experience of planning, delivering and developing teaching, as well as supervising students, in Musical Performance and Interpretation or other areas relevant to the position. In assessing teaching expertise, particular emphasis will be placed on: the ability to integrate and communicate artistic practice, research and critical reflection in teaching; the ability to support students’ independent artistic development; the ability to work in both individual and group-based teaching formats. Emphasis will also be placed on documented contributions to course and programme development, evidence of pedagogical reflection, and the ability to create inclusive and intellectually challenging learning environments for students from diverse artistic backgrounds. Collaboration with the community at large and Administrative Competence Expertise in collaboration with the wider community is demonstrated, for example, through the ability to develop existing collaborative relationships or to establish new collaborations between academia and relevant societal stakeholders, as well as through the ability to communicate research findings to the public. Administrative competence refers to experience of academic leadership, such as coordinating research or educational activities, as well as contributing to the development of the department or faculty. Overall Assessment A position as Assistant Professor is a career-development position targeting researchers and teachers who defended their doctoral thesis relatively recently. The position will be offered to the candidate who is assessed to have the best prospects for carrying out the duties required in the position, collaborating and cooperating with other staff, and contributing positively to the development of activities based on an overall assessment of their expertise and development potential. Extensive experience in academic environments outside Sweden is highly meritorious. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment for five years Extent: Full-time (100%) Location: Gothenburg Starting date : By agreement An appointment as Assistant Professor is a fixed-term career-development position. The purpose of the position is to provide the successful candidate with the opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher and to strengthen their scientific and pedagogical qualifications in order to meet the requirements for eligibility for appointment as Senior Lecturer. The appointment may be extended, but for no more than a total of two years, if additional time is required to fulfil the purpose of the appointment due to sick leave, parental leave, or other special circumstances. Promotion to Senior Lecturer An Assistant Professor is entitled, upon application, to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer in the same subject area as the Assistant Professorship. Promotion requires that the applicant is qualified and deemed suitable for appointment as a senior lecturer within the subject are, in accordance with the Higher Education Ordinance and the University of Gothenburg’s Regulations on the Appointment of Teaching Staff (Ref. No. GU 2024/3252). In addition, the applicant must have been appointed as an Associate Professor (in Swedish: docent) at the Artistic Faculty. The eligibility requirements for the position of Associate Professor (docent) are set out in the document accessible via this link (Ref. No. V 2014/885): Anvisningar docent Furthermore, the assessment will evaluate whether you have demonstrated proficiency in Swedish at least at level B2 according to the CEFR. This is demonstrated either by passing a language test, the requirements for which are described here: common-european-framework-reference-language-skills , or by a certificate from the Head of Department stating that the language test is clearly unnecessary. Moving to Gothenburg Gothenburg is the second largest city in Sweden, beautifully situated on the west coast. The city is well known for its friendly atmosphere and high quality of life. Choosing a career in a new country is a major step. Gothenburg offers a welcoming environment and strong support for international talent relocating to the region. Internationally recruited staff are offered a contracted relocation package and tax advisory services (Global Mobility support). Move to Gothenburg - a guide for international talents Welcome Services - support for international staff relocating to Gothenburg Other information The position of Assistant Professor is a career-development appointment and constitutes a step in an academic career path designed to enable researchers to develop their independence and establish their own research profile, with the right to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Senior Lecturer. At the University of Gothenburg, Senior Lecturers are also eligible to be assessed for promotion to a permanent position as Full Professor. To support a healthy work–life balance, we offer academic freedom, flexible working arrangements, and generous annual leave. Employees are also entitled to a range of benefits, including a wellness allowance, supplementary parental benefits, and an occupational pension scheme. For more information about employment benefits at the University of Gothenburg, please visit: Employment benefits | University of Gothenburg If you do not have formal training in academic teaching, you will be given the opportunity to undertake such training as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. If you do not have proficiency in Swedish at a minimum of level B2 according to the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages (CEFR), you will be offered language courses as part of your employment. Participation in these courses is mandatory. Selection process Eligibility screening of applicants will take place after the application deadline. The application documents of eligible candidates will subsequently be submitted to external reviewers for assessment. As part of the selection process, in addition to expert assessment and interviews, trial lectures and reference checks may also be conducted. Interviews are planned to take place during end of week 49. Contact information If you have any questions regarding the position, please contact Erik Jonasson, Head of the music unit, +46766184167, erik.jonasson@hsm.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact HR at tha Academy of music and drama, hr@hsm.gu.se Unions Information about union representatives at the University of Gothenburg is available here. Applications You apply for the position through the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. Applicants are responsible for ensuring that their application is complete in accordance with the advertisement and that it is submitted no later than the application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Audio, video, and web-based media materials that cannot be uploaded to the application platform could be submitted via stable direct links in a Word or PDF document under the relevant eligibility criterion. The document must clearly state what the material contains and explain its relevance to the application. The links must remain accessible throughout the entire recruitment process, and the material must be available for download in order to be considered as supporting documentation submitted with the application. Publications that cannot be attached to the application digitally should be sent to: Academy of Music and Drama HR Box 210 SE-405 30 Göteborg Sweden and must be received no later than the application deadline. Application deadline: 30 October 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41334": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science -  PhD position in multimodal neuroimaging",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science -  PhD position in multimodal neuroimaging For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science -  PhD position in multimodal neuroimaging Reference number PAR 2026/657 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical science Project title: Multimodal neuroimaging to decipher neurodegenerative diseases The Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry is located at the Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's disease (AD) begin years to decades before symptoms emerge. With the approval of the first anti-amyloid therapies, which are most effective when initiated early, there is an urgent need for biomarkers that can detect, stage and monitor disease in this presymptomatic window. Yet the combined value of available neuroimaging biomarkers for early neurodegeneration remains insufficiently established, and emerging modalities capturing processes upstream of atrophy require validation. This PhD project uses multimodal neuroimaging to decipher early neurodegenerative disease across three complementary modalities and world-leading cohorts. Structural MRI measures of hippocampal volume, cortical thickness, white matter hyperintensities and perfusion will be studied in relation to cerebrospinal fluid and plasma biomarkers and cognition in the population-based REAL AD Validation cohort. Longitudinal tau PET with [18F]RO948 in the ALFA+ cohort of cognitively unimpaired individuals will characterise the temporal relationships between amyloid, tau spreading, neurodegeneration and cognitive change across biomarker-defined disease stages. Finally, synaptic density will be quantified with [18F]SynVesT-1 SV2A PET in the SYNAPSE study, evaluating whether synaptic loss – a strong correlate of cognitive decline – can serve as a marker of neurodegeneration preceding structural atrophy, and whether it is sensitive to cognitive training-induced plasticity. Across modalities, diagnostic and prognostic models will be developed to determine the added value of each technique, alone and combined with fluid and cognitive measures, for early detection, disease staging and prediction of progression. The project is embedded in an international supervisory network spanning Gothenburg, Stockholm, Barcelona and Madison, and will deliver validated imaging tools to support earlier diagnosis, improved trial design and treatment monitoring in AD. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology  Department of Psychiatry and Neurochemistry. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Neuroscience and Physiology require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Michael Schöll, Institute of Neuroscience and Physiology, e-mail: michael.scholl@neuro.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-09-21 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41335": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Health Care Sciences",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Health Care Sciences -  Person-centred integrated health and social care For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Health Care Sciences -  Person-centred integrated health and social care Reference number PAR 2026/656 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Health Care Sciences Project title: From fragmentation to integration: Evaluating the CO-DIAC working method in residential aged care The Department of Learning and Leadership for Health Care Professionals is located at the Institute of Health and Care Sciences at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Health Care Sciences comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: The project focuses on person-centred and integrated health and social care in residential care facilities for older people. Current care is often fragmented, making it difficult to coordinate services and meet older people’s needs. In line with Sweden’s transition towards Close Care (Nära vård), practical methods are needed to support proactive, health-promoting and person-centred care across professional and organisational boundaries. The older person is an active partner whose experiences, resources, needs and goals guide care and everyday life alongside professional knowledge and clinical expertise. The overall aim of the project is to evaluate the feasibility, effectiveness, relevance and practical usability of a working method for person-centred and integrated care in residential care facilities. Four interrelated studies will involve older people, relatives and staff. Using qualitative and quantitative methods, the project will evaluate the working method’s effects, relevance and use in practice. The project will generate knowledge about how and under what conditions available resources can be used effectively to promote well-being and meaningful improvements for older people living in residential care, relatives, and staff. The doctoral student will be part of the University of Gothenburg Centre for Person-Centred Care ( GPCC ), the SHIFT CARE research school and the Centre for a dementia-friendly society with a person-centred mindset ( DEMSAM ). These research environments offer a strong network of researchers and doctoral students and opportunities to develop the knowledge and competencies required for a doctoral degree. The project is interdisciplinary and will be conducted in close collaboration with municipal and regional primary care and researchers within CO-Designing Integrated Aged Care ( CO-DIAC ). The work involves both independent work and close collaboration within the research group and includes, among other activities, a randomised controlled trial, surveys, interviews, focus groups, and qualitative and quantitative data analysis. Data collection and analysis are conducted in Swedish and involve communication with Swedish-speaking participants. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Health Care Sciences - Department of Learning and Leadership for Health Care Professionals . Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Health Care Sciences require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Andreas Fors, Institute of Health Care Sciences, e-mail: andreas.fors@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-09-21 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41326": {
      "title": "Doctoral position in practical philosophy, with a focus on economic ethics and/or environmental ethics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral position in practical philosophy, with a focus on economic ethics and/or environmental ethi For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral position in practical philosophy, with a focus on economic ethics and/or environmental ethics Reference number PAR 2026/638 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Department of Philosophy, Linguistics, and Theory of Science (FLoV) within the Faculty of Humanities, we have around 120 employees and a teaching volume that includes around 350 full-year students. We are engaged in numerous large international and interdisciplinary research projects in areas such as theoretical and practical philosophy, logic, linguistics, computational linguistics, language technology, and theory of science. Our research has many applications and collaborations with other scientific fields. We educate philosophers, logicians, linguists, language technologists, theorists of science, historians of philosophy, and teachers of philosophy. The department also conducts doctoral education and development work within its various academic domains. If you are interested in working in an international, dynamic and collaborative environment, FLoV offers an inspiring setting for research and education. Read more about us here. Our department offers postgraduate education in six subjects. Our research environment is international, with several recognised research projects and our staff representing over twenty countries. Read more here. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Project description We are now looking for a doctoral student to join the department’s Financial Ethics Research Group . The research group consists of philosophers and economists dedicated to ethical and political issues raised by money and finance (in the broadest sense). The group’s previous projects have been on topics such as sustainable business, ethical investment, banking for the poor, digital currencies, climate metrics, and the value of biodiversity. More specifically, the doctoral student will be connected to the research program Sustainable Finance Lab , which is a collaboration between several Swedish universities that is funded by Vinnova, Sweden’s innovation agency. The program explores various aspects of how to make markets and money into a positive force for sustainable development. The doctoral student is expected to do research in economic ethics and/or environmental ethics. Economic ethics is the application of moral or political philosophy to critical issues in business organizations or the economic system. Environmental ethics is the application of moral or political philosophy to critical issues in the relationship between humans and non-human environmental systems. The research area should be understood broadly so as to include subtopics like business ethics, the political philosophy of markets, ethics of commodification, ethics of climate change or biodiversity, collective action problems, and the philosophy of (environmental) economics. Any applications related to these subfields are welcome. The applicant must describe a more specific research question within this area that they intend to explore. Some examples of potential research questions are: What moral duties do commercial firms or banks have to mitigate climate change? Do consumers have a duty to buy sustainable goods, or invest in sustainable firms? How should societal institutions (such as central banks or the monetary system) be set up to promote biodiversity? Is the state justified in coercing market participants to mitigate climate change? What is the relationship between sustainability and human rights? Are current conceptualizations of sustainability in terms of environmental, social and governance (ESG) criteria adequate? Duties Doctoral studies comprise four years of full-time study. The candidate’s main responsibilities will be to write a doctoral dissertation in the area outlined above, and to complete obligatory course work. The doctoral student may also undertake a limited amount of teaching, administration or research not directly connected to her or his dissertation topic (but not more than 20%). Such duties extend the position accordingly. The candidate is expected to carry out the work in Gothenburg and to participate on a regular basis in the department’s activities, especially the seminars in practical philosophy. And expected to take an active part in the activities of the Financial Ethics Research Group, including meetings, seminars and conferences. The working language within the group is English. There will be funds available for travel and shorter visits to other universities. Eligibility We welcome applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. The specific entry requirements for practical philosophy are: 30 higher education credits in practical philosophy as a second-cycle subject, at least 15 of which must be from a degree project, or equivalent knowledge, and the level of English and/or Swedish skills needed to benefit from compulsory courses and to be able to actively participate in seminars and similar activities. For further information about the general and specific requirements please refer to the general study syllabus you will find through the following webpage. Assessment criteria Applicants will be assessed based on their estimated ability to complete the study program with a successful result. We will base this assessment primarily on two criteria: The candidate’s research proficiency as it is demonstrated in submitted theses, publications and other documents. Submitted theses and publications will be assessed on aspects such as: suitable knowledge of relevant literature, suitable formulation of research questions, academic rigor in arguments and reasoning, as well as independence and originality of thought. How well the project plan fits with the research focus and ambitions of the Financial Ethics Research Group and the Sustainable Finance Lab. The candidate is encouraged to explain how the proposed research connects to ongoing projects and competencies, and/or how it can broaden them with new perspectives. The project plan will be assessed on aspects such as: suitable knowledge of relevant literature, fruitfulness of the research question, feasibility as a doctoral project, as well as potential to contribute to the research front. The selection process will also include an interview. Selection, interviews, and assessments are conducted by a recruitment group at the department, after which the Head of Department makes the final decision. More information regarding the University’s recruitment process you will find here. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral candidate can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment may apply for a maximum of one year and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. The agreement is only available in Swedish, please contact us if you would like to know more. Please note that doctoral studies at FLoV require physical presence to conduct the studies. Help can be provided to obtain a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden. The department has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Type of employment: Fixed-term employment, HF 5 kap 7§ Extent: 100 % Location: Department of Philosophy, Linguistics and Theory of Science, Gothenburg First day of employment: January 2027, or by agreement Contact information For questions about our third-cycle studies please contact Associate head of third cycle studies, Alva Stråge, alva.strage@gu.se +46 (0)31-786 5963 For questions about the Financial Ethics Research Group or the Sustainable Finance Lab, please contact project leader, Professor Joakim Sandberg, joakim.sandberg@gu.se , +46 (0)31-786 4571 For questions regarding the application, recruitment process, or employment conditions, please contact HR-officer Jennifer Stråle, jennifer.strale@gu.se +46 (0)31-786 4570 Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here . Application You apply to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. For the application to be considered complete it must include: An introductory letter of 500 words maximum (approx. 1 page). The letter should consist of three paragraphs in the following order: a) a short presentation of yourself, b) a short presentation of your merits, and c) a short account of why the department / research group / research program is of interest to you. A CV, including a list of publications (if applicable). Bachelor’s thesis, master’s thesis and (if applicable) up to three other research publications. A project proposal of no more than 2,500 words, in which you describe the specific research question you wish to explore, justify its relevance in relation to both theory and practice, and indicate some main lines of argument you wish to defend. (This is merely a proposal and you will be able to adjust your project at later stages, in consultation with your supervisor.) Certificates of degrees. Other documents or attachments that you wish to appeal to (optional). It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please submit your application in English, as it will be reviewed by external reviewers whose working language is English. Applications must be received by: October 13, 2026 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41264": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Innovation, Entrepreneurship and Management of Intellectual Assets",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Innovation, Entrepreneurship and Management of Intellectual Assets For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Innovation, Entrepreneurship and Management of Intellectual Assets Reference number PAR 2026/50 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Economy and Society at the School of Business, Economics and Law, University of Gothenburg is announcing 1-2 doctoral student positions in Innovation, Entrepreneurship and Management of Intellectual Assets at the Unit for Innovation and Entrepreneurship. The unit offers single-subject courses at the Bachelor´s level, two Master´s programs (MSc in Innovation and Industrial Management; MSc in Knowledge-based Entrepreneurship) and doctoral education. For more information: Unit for Innovation and Entrepreneurship | University of Gothenburg . These doctoral students will be an important part of the international research center Gothenburg U-GOT KIES , which is hosted at the unit, as well as ongoing international research projects. The doctoral student project will broadly focus on knowledge creation and knowledge transfer - between small and large firms and between firms and universities, within specific technologies and industries. We work with many firms, universities and organizations, regionally and globally, to conduct and present our research. The associated research group that the doctoral students will join consists of national and international scholars. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages: https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies . Duties As a doctoral student you will use social science methodologies to investigate how and why innovation and entrepreneurship affect business and society. Each project will focus on a carefully selected topic in which these phenomena manifest themselves. The topic and individual study plan will be developed together with the supervisor(s). Below is a selection of potential topics that may be relevant to the advertised doctoral students projects: The shifting needs of the Swedish food industry and associated value chains with respect to sustainable transitions and crisis preparedness – particularly focusing on the Swedish maritime industries – and how innovation and entrepreneurship can contribute to meet and strengthen these needs. The role of innovation and entrepreneurship, together with the governance and policy structures associated with them, in the development of new sustainable solutions for the Swedish and European energy sector and its value chains, with a particular focus on renewable energy sources such as solar, wind, and tidal power. Artificial intelligence, innovation and entrepreneurship: Examining how AI transforms innovation processes, the identification of entrepreneurial opportunities, business model development and knowledge creation across industries, as well as analysing the governance mechanisms and policy frameworks required for the responsible and sustainable adoption and diffusion of AI-based innovations. How organized collaboration and knowledge sharing among entrepreneurs (“collective entrepreneurial action”) affect environmental, economic, and social sustainability in local and rural contexts, with partial focus on the Swedish craft brewing industry. Repurposing as an innovation strategy within the biotech and pharmaceutical industries and beyond: Analyzing how firms and research organizations reuse and recombine existing knowledge, technologies, or products to accelerate innovation, reduce uncertainty, and improve R&D productivity. Collaboration as an enabler for innovation and knowledge generation: Examining how collaboration networks evolve, which factors promote effective collaboration, and how the internationalization of R&D shapes scientific advancements and innovation in complex, knowledge-intense industries. The doctoral education (leading to a doctoral degree) in Innovation, Entrepreneurship and Management of Intellectual Assets consists of conducting a scientific research project and completing at least 90 credits of doctoral courses. Moreover, doctoral education may also include applying research results in collaboration with companies, universities and the public sector, and participating in international research conferences. Doctoral education requires a doctoral thesis consisting of either compiled papers or a monograph, corresponding to 150 credits, and defended in public. More information about the doctoral education at the department can be found at: Doctoral Studies | University of Gothenburg . Doctoral studies comprise four years of full-time study and lead to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20% of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, which results in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels but may also include research and administration. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totaling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are at least 15 credits of coursework and 15 credits for a degree project at second-cycle level within the subject or a closely related field. To be qualified, the applicant must demonstrate a high level of proficiency in both spoken and written English corresponding to English 6/English B at Swedish upper secondary school level, or an equivalent level demonstrated through an internationally recognised test. For more information about general entrance requirements, see www.universityadmissions.se (English) and www.antagning.se . Applicants who do not speak Swedish already will be expected to learn Swedish during the doctoral education. Applicants can apply before fulfilling all these requirements but the decision for admission can only be made after all requirements are fulfilled. Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate doctoral studies. During the assessment, we will specifically evaluate and place a high value upon documented experience of: specific knowledge and skills within the research subject area and closely related fields experience of scientific theory and relevant research methods scientific analysis and presentation, as demonstrated through a Master’s thesis, degree project, scientific publications, etc. Great importance will be placed on personal suitability. Good collaborative skills are important for the position. You should be able to work both independently and as part of a team. You should have the ability to learn both independently and through interaction with leading scholars through doctoral courses and conferences, both nationally and internationally. Proficiency in Swedish is considered a merit, corresponding to the Swedish upper secondary school courses Swedish 3 (Svenska 3) or Swedish as a Second Language 3 (Svenska som andraspråk 3). Experience of conducting interviews, coding interview responses in relation to theory, and Master’s-level courses in qualitative methods is considered a merit. Experience of quantitative methods is also considered a merit, including experience of using statistical software such as R or Stata, and conducting econometric analyses. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. The position is located at the Division of Innovation and Entrepreneurship, Department of Economy and Society. Starting date is 2027-02-01 or as agreed, but no later than 2027-04-01. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at Department of Economy and Society require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, Department of Economy and Society has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. For applicants with a master’s degree from a foreign country If your degree is from a university/HEI outside of Sweden or has not been examined by a Swedish authority, it is important that you include diploma and transcript of records to facilitate evaluation. Please also provide contact details (telephone number or email address) for the awarding institution so that the degree can be verified. For information on how to document the Swedish language proficiency considered a merit, see: Tisus – Test in Swedish for university studies . For information on how to meet the specific English language requirement, see: https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ . Selection process The selection process may include interviews, written tests and checks of applicants’ references. Contact information If you have questions about the employment, you are welcome to contact: Ethan Gifford, Head of Unit, Docent, ethan.gifford@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please attach the following in the application: Personal letter of max 2 pages with an overview of your previous experience, education, and future goals CV Degree certificate and transcripts/grades for completed courses Research proposal of max 2 pages outlining how you would carry out one of the projects listed above, including title, theory, method and expected outcomes A thesis at second-cycle/Master’s level (master’s thesis or equivalent) High school grades Documentation of English language requirements and Swedish language qualifications/merits Two letters of reference, including their contact details Other documents relevant for the application Applications must be received by: 2026-10-08 (October 8 th ) Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41304": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/653 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 315 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. We are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic researcher with a background in islet physiology and single-cell genomics to join the lab of Dr. Joan Camuñas. The lab of Dr. Joan Camuñas stands at the interface of genomics, biophysics and precision medicine. Our mission is to develop experimental and computational methods to identify molecular mechanisms leading to dysfunctional cellular states in human disease ( www.camunaslab.org ). The candidate will contribute to the improve Slice-seq, a new method to study human pancreatic tissue slices using high-resolution calcium imaging in combination with spatial transcriptomics. The researcher will develop methods to improve the viability of pancreatic slices cultures, develop approaches for their cryopreservation, and distribution. The researcher will also run single-cell genomic and spatial transcriptomic assays on pancreatic tissue. The project will require significant interdisciplinary work in the areas of physiology, molecular biology and islet biology. The research group is embedded in the Wallenberg Center for Molecular and Translational Medicine (WCMTM) and SciLifeLab in the University of Gothenburg. and is tightly connected to a broad network of international collaborators. Duties Develop and perform high-resolution spatial transcriptomics in multiple slices. Perform high-resolution calcium imaging in acute pancreatic tissue slices. Perform single-cell protocols using high-throughput liquid handling instrumentation. Develop methods to improve acute tissue slice culture, cryopreservation and distribution. Analyze the generated data, design and perform validations through tissue imaging and other functional measurements. Lead the project, present results in group meetings and conferences, and write research papers based on scientific discoveries. Help supervise and mentor junior team members. Qualifications The ideal candidates should have the following qualifications: PhD in a relevant research area (e.g. Physiology, Molecular Biology). Highly motivated, proactive and diligent. Ability to work independently and in a team. Experience working with acute pancreatic slices. Experience in experimental methods in spatial transcriptomics and single-cell genomics (Parse is a plus). Experience in pancreatic islet biology Knowledge in using a variety of methods including cell culture, molecular biology, and biochemistry is advantageous. Good skills in both written and spoken English are required. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, 10 months, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Dr. Joan Camuñas Soler, joan.camunas@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-15 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41274": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher Reference number PAR 2026/641 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 340 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. We can offer an exciting opportunity to join the Mucin Biology groups at the Department of Medical Chemistry and Cell Biology, Institute of Biomedicine, Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. These are internationally renowned research groups forming a strong collaborative network focused on understanding the role of mucins and mucus in protecting epithelial surfaces and how their dysfunction contributes to disease. Duties The detailed molecular structure of mucins and related proteins in mucus is not yet fully understood. This is especially true regarding how different proteins and their domains interact with each other and thereby organize the mucus. With increased knowledge of the structure of mucus proteins, we will now be able to predict and begin to understand how different properties of mucus arise, how it can change, and how defects in this structure can be linked to disease. We will express and purify mucins, various mucin domains, and mucus-associated proteins for structural determination using cryo-electron microscopy combined with data processing and modeling, as well as perform other biochemical methods including thermophoresis. The work will be carried out independently, involving both practical structural biology research, compilation of results, and publication. Qualifications A doctoral degree in a subject area relevant to the studies is required. This project requires you to have extensive experience and knowledge in structural determination using cryo-electron microscopy and in advanced analysis of the generated data. Knowledge and experience in expressing mucins and their domains, purifying this type of proteins, and performing a wide range of biochemical analyses are expected. Experience in mucin biology research is considered a strong merit. You must have substantial experience conducting independent research at an internationally leading level within the area of structural biology of glycoproteins, such as mucins, and related proteins. You should have an extensive publication record in a relevant research field. The research environment is international, with English as the main language of communication; therefore, excellent spoken and written English skills are required. Employment The position is a temporary appointment and full-time for 1 year with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Professon Gunnar C. Hansson. E-mail: gunnar.hansson@medkem.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-15 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41262": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Bioinformatics and Cancer Genetics",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Bioinformatics and Cancer Genetics For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Bioinformatics and Cancer Genetics Reference number PAR 2026/634 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Postdoctor in Bioinformatics and Cancer Genetics The Institute of Biomedicine is involved in both research and education. In both of these areas, we focus on fundamental knowledge of the living cell – what it consists of, how it works, how its function is directed by the genetic material, and how it interacts with various kinds of micro-organisms. Using this knowledge, we try to elucidate the causes of diseases, and find new ways to diagnose and treat them. The Institute is composed of the following four departments: The Department of Infectious Diseases The Department of Microbiology and Immunology The Department of Medical Biochemistry and Cell Biology The Department of Laboratory Medicine At present, the institute has about 360 employees and approximately 450 million SEK in total assets. Subject area Bioinformatics and Cancer Genetics Subject area description Somatic mutation accumulation and clonal evolution are central processes in cancer development. Through large-scale whole-genome sequencing and other high-throughput approaches, these processes can be mapped in detail, providing insights into tumor initiation, progression, and treatment response. This position focuses on the application and development of bioinformatics methods for the analysis of genomic cancer data, including large public international datasets and locally generated patient-derived sequencing data. The project may involve, for example, studies of somatic mutational processes, clonal dynamics, tumor evolutionary history, or integrative analyses of genomic and clinical data. Analyses of non-cancer-related sequencing data may also be considered. The work will be carried out in a research environment with strong expertise in cancer genomics, computational biology, and translational research. Work published by the group in recent time includes: Ögren et al., Light-based footprinting of a eukaryotic genome. Science Advances 2026; Elliott et al., Mechanistic basis of atypical TERT promoter mutations. Nature Communications . 2024; Elliott et al. Base-resolution UV footprinting by sequencing reveals distinctive damage signatures for DNA-binding proteins. Nature Communications. 2023; Boström et al. Somatic mutation distribution across tumour cohorts provides a signal for positive selection in cancer. Nature Communications. 2022; Elias et al. Independent somatic evolution underlies clustered neuroendocrine tumors in the human small intestine. Nature Communications. 2021; Van den Eynden et al. Lack of detectable neoantigen depletion signals in the untreated cancer genome. Nature Genetics . 2019. Duties The postdoctoral researcher will apply and develop computational methods for the analysis of whole-genome sequencing and other NGS-based data from patients, including mutation calling and detection of mosaic mutations. The work may also include statistical analyses and data visualization to identify and interpret somatic mutational patterns and other genomic alterations, as well as integration of genomic data with clinical and phenotypic variables when relevant. The researcher is expected to contribute to manuscript writing and publication in international scientific journals and may present research findings at national and international conferences. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Good programming skills and linux/unix experience is a requirement. Knowledge of Matlab or R is also highly desired. Previous experience with analysis of next-generation sequencing data is recommended, particularly calling of somatic mutations. Experience from relevant biological fields is recommended, and the applicant is expected to have published several articles in peer-reviewed journals. Excellent communicative skills are required, including fluency in oral and written English. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is full time and temporary, two years with the possibility of one year's extension, with placement at the Institute of Biomedicine. First day of employment as agreed. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact Erik Larsson Lekholm, Professor, phone: +46 31 786 6942, e-mail: erik.lekholm@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application should contain: - A cover letter giving a brief description of previous research experience, and a motivation to why you are applying - A CV including a list of publications - Contact details of two references - Proof of completed PhD Applications must be received by: 2026-10-10 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41284": {
      "title": "Student workers wanted for the Cranet Survey",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Student workers wanted for the Cranet Survey For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Student workers wanted for the Cranet Survey Reference number PAR 2026/644 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Would you like to gain hands-on experience of working with an international HRM research project? From September to December, the Center for Global HRM will conduct the Swedish part of the Cranet Survey, an international survey of HR policies and practices conducted in around 40 countries. To support the data collection, we are looking for two student workers for temporary positions during this period. Each position corresponds to approximately one month of full-time work, distributed over four months (around 25% from September to December). Duties Position 1: Data management The main focus of this position is to support the technical preparation, administration and management of the survey and its data. Tasks will include: Preparing and administering the survey in Qualtrics Contributing to the development of automated feedback reports for survey respondents Creating and maintaining a database for reminders and possible future surveys Preparing and transferring survey data to SPSS Other tasks related to survey administration and data management The position requires good data management skills. Previous experience of working with Qualtrics is highly desirable, and familiarity with SPSS is an advantage. Position 2: Communication and survey recruitment The main focus of this position is to help us reach potential respondents and encourage participation in the survey. Tasks will include: Identifying relevant potential respondents Contacting and communicating with HR professionals Following up contacts and encouraging survey participation Contributing to communication and visibility for the survey, including through LinkedIn Other tasks related to respondent recruitment and survey communication The position requires good communication and interpersonal skills, as well as the ability to communicate effectively in both Swedish and English. What do we offer? As a student worker, you will have the opportunity to work closely with the research team behind the Swedish Cranet Survey, led by Professor Stefan Tengblad at the Center for Global HRM. You will gain insight into how a large-scale international research project is organised and conducted and may also have opportunities for contact with Cranet research teams in other countries. The project also provides an excellent opportunity for students interested in writing a Master's thesis using Cranet data. Student workers will have the possibility to explore thesis projects based on the survey data, including collaboration with a thesis partner. The Swedish results from the survey will be reported during 2027. This is expected to generate further opportunities for student work related to the preparation of reports and other communication of results in both Swedish and English. Qualifications Please send  your CV + short expression of interest in the position and why you think you can add value to the project to stefan.tengblad@gu.se by August 25. In your application, please indicate whether you are primarily interested in Data Management or Communication and Survey Recruitment. Please also register your application in the recruitment system by following the link below. Interviews may take place on an ongoing basis throughout the application period. Employment Each position corresponds to approximately one month of full-time work, distributed over four months (around 25% from September to December). Start in September and lasts to the 31th of December. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact stefan.tengblad@gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact human resources officer Natalie Axell at natalie.axell@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-03 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41279": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral researcher position in global change biology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral researcher position in global change biology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral researcher position in global change biology Reference number PAR 2026/643 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Postdoctoral researcher position in global change biology At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) we have teaching and research activities that stretch from the alpine ecosystem, through forests, cultivated land and streams, all the way into the marine environment. In these environments we study different levels of biological organisation from genes, individuals and populations, to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics and combinations of these fields in order to understand the impact of natural and anthropogenic changes of the environment. The department is placed at two different localities: in Natrium at Medicinaregatan 7B in Gothenburg and at the Kristineberg marine research station operated by the Marine Infrastructure at the University of Gothenburg. The current employment is based at Natrium, with the possibility to conduct research projects at the marine station. We offer The University of Gothenburg is a state authority, which means special benefits, more holidays and a great pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . Subject area Global change biology Project description Climate change and the increased frequency, intensity, and duration of heatwaves pose growing threats to the persistence of animal biodiversity. A central challenge in global change biology is to understand why species and populations differ in their vulnerability, so that research can inform conservation, management, and policy. However, we still cannot reliably predict how climate change will affect animal populations across their full life cycle. Accumulating evidence suggests that early life stages can be more sensitive to extreme heat than adults, yet developmental stages remain critically understudied. Current methods used to quantify climate vulnerability are also often not directly comparable between embryos and later life stages, which has further limited our ability to identify critical windows of sensitivity to climate change. New methodological advances now allow us to standardise how we compare vulnerability across life stages, opening up exciting opportunities to understand how climate change affects all stages of life. The central question of this postdoc project is: How and why does thermal sensitivity vary across the life cycle, and what are the consequences for animal populations under climate change? This fully funded 2-year postdoc project is part of a research program funded by the Swedish Research Council (Vetenskapsrådet) on the vulnerability of fish life stages to climate change. The successful applicant is expected to combine empirical research with evidence synthesis and comparative analyses. While some projects will primarily focus on fish, comparative studies can be broader in scope, and there will be opportunities to study other taxa (e.g., invertebrates). The project plan is flexible in its scope, and the postdoc is encouraged to develop original research questions. The applicant is also expected to take part in collaborations at both local and global scales, and take leadership roles through the Thermal Ecology Alliance (e.g., managing globally distributed experiments). Therefore, the successful postdoc will have excellent opportunities to develop a large collaborative network and address broad questions that no individual lab can tackle alone. The postdoc will be primarily supervised by Patrice Pottier, and co-supervised by Prof. Fredrik Jutfelt at the University of Gothenburg. The successful applicant will have meaningful opportunities to shape the intellectual and collaborative culture of the group, while collaborating more broadly within the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences and beyond. The successful applicant will benefit from at least one supervisory meeting per week, frequent and structured support during the early stages of all projects, mentoring and career development guidance, and gradual transfer of responsibilities. Expectations, working ethics, and supervision needs will be discussed openly at the start of the position, and revisited through mutual feedback and open communication. The successful applicant will have opportunities to develop skills in experimental design, laboratory and field research on animals, statistical modelling and programming, evidence synthesis, climate modelling, open and reproducible research, scientific writing, teaching, communication, as well as leadership, project, and people management. The successful applicant will also have opportunities to co-supervise MSc and/or PhD students. Training will be tailored to the career objectives of the applicant. Duties The principal duty of the postdoctoral research is to undertake high-quality research, communicate their findings, and develop/strengthen skills that will benefit their scientific career. The successful applicant will: Develop research questions and study designs; Conduct laboratory experiments and, where relevant, field sampling or environmental monitoring; Collect, manage, analyse, and interpret data using transparent and reproducible workflows; Conduct evidence synthesis, meta-analysis, comparative analyses, or environmental modelling; Comply with relevant animal-welfare, ethical, data-management, and health-and-safety requirements; Communicate results through scientific publications, seminars, conferences, and other appropriate formats; Participate constructively in departmental and collegial activities Contribute to leading and managing large collaborations through the Thermal Ecology Alliance Periods of fieldwork at Kristineberg marine station or research visits elsewhere in Sweden or abroad may form part of the project. The need, timing, and accessibility of such activities will be discussed and planned with the successful applicant. This is a 100% postdoctoral research position (postdoctor) targeted for recent PhD graduates, with potential opportunities to contribute to teaching and departmental duties up to 20% of full-time employment. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Required qualifications for the employment: To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree in ecology, evolutionary biology, quantitative biology, or related fields Excellent communication and interpersonal skills in written and spoken English A clear motivation for research in global change biology and an ability to identify scientific questions relevant to the broad aims of the project Experience, relative to opportunity, in planning, conducting, interpreting, and publishing high-quality research High levels of curiosity, independence, and excellent organisational skills High levels of critical thinking, problem solving, and a general drive to learn new concepts and methods Advanced skills in statistical analysis and coding in R or similar programming environment A willingness to take leadership roles and manage global collaborations through the Thermal Ecology Alliance An ability to receive and give constructive feedback, and work collaboratively and respectfully with people who may have different disciplinary, cultural, or personal backgrounds A strong commitment to scientific integrity, fairness, and open science principles Desirable qualifications for the employment: Fundamental knowledge in thermal ecology and evolutionary biology Leadership skills and experience managing large collaborations Experience with evidence synthesis, meta-analysis, or other comparative methods Experience in experimental research with animals or other biological systems Experience in field ecology, animal sampling, or environmental monitoring Experience in data management, open science, and reproducible analytical workflows Experience publishing research in scientific journals Personal qualifications: We are looking for an engaged, enthusiastic, and curious person who is passionate about understanding, forecasting, and mitigating the impacts of climate change on biodiversity. We particularly welcome researchers with strong communication and interpersonal skills who enjoy leading and managing collaborative teams. We are also looking for someone with great organisational capabilities who is willing to develop their independence and leadership skills. In addition, we would love to welcome someone who enjoys developing new ideas, thinks critically about the state of the evidence, and is willing to explore concepts outside of their immediate field of expertise. All qualifications and achievements will be assessed relative to opportunity. Assessment will focus on the ideas, ambitions, and potential demonstrated by the applicant rather than publication counts, institutional prestige, or the linearity of their career path. The application should be written in English and must include: A cover letter (max 2 pages) describing why the applicant is interested in this position and how they meet the selection criteria A list of qualifications (CV; max 4 pages) A certificate of PhD exam A complete list of publications, including submitted and accepted manuscripts The contact details of 1-2 referees (candidates will be notified before contact with the referees is made) Shortlisted candidates will be invited to a structured interview, which will be conducted in English and may be held digitally. Candidates will receive information about the interview format in advance. Employment The employment is a temporary employment for 2 years, 100 % of full time, placed at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science. Starting date preferably before March 2027 (with flexibility to accommodate notice periods and residence permits), or upon agreement. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact: Patrice Pottier Phone: +46702976011 Email: patrice.pottier@bioenv.gu.se Webpage: patricepottierlab.com Åsa Arrhenius, Head of Department tel. +46 31 786 26 25 Email: asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science, hr@bioenv.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Publications that cannot be attached to the application in digital format should be mailed to: Department of Biological and Environmental Science Att: Patrice Pottier University of Gothenburg Box 461 or 463 S-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden Label the envelope “Postdoctoral position in global change biology, PAR 2026/643. The publications must be received no later than the application deadline. Applications must be received by: 21 September 2026 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41290": {
      "title": "Researcher position in advanced BLS microscopy",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Researcher position in advanced BLS microscopy For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Researcher position in advanced BLS microscopy Reference number PAR 2026/617 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Physics at the University of Gothenburg is located in the center of Gothenburg, with approximately 100 employees. The communication routes are good both nationally and internationally. The research focuses within the fields of Atom- and Molecular Physics, Condensed Matter Physics and Spintronics, and Complex Systems and Biophysics and is performed in an international environment with extensive national and international collaborations. The education programs at the Department include Bachelor- and Master programs in Physics, physics teacher-training programs, as well as outreach courses in Physics aimed for the general public. Gothenburg Physics Centre is a close collaboration with three other departments within the Faculty of Science and Chalmers University of Technology, which creates an innovative environment for all researchers and students at the department. For further information about the Department of Physics, please visit our website at gu.se/en/physics . More information about Gothenburg Physics Centre can be found at chalmers.se/gpc . About the research group One of the department's most successful research areas is spintronics, with a focus on magnetodynamic phenomena, spin/orbital torque, nanofabrication, and the fundamental physics of spintronic oscillators. We study these devices using magnetic, electrical, and optical methods (particularly micro-focused BLS), complemented by GPU-accelerated micromagnetic simulations for modeling. Johan Åkerman's research group is running a major project funded by VR distinguished Professor grant, dedicated to developing new spintronic devices. To further support the BLS facility, the department is seeking a researcher to contribute to driving this part of the project forward. Job assignments The primary responsibility of this position is the development and application of Brillouin light scattering (BLS) microscopy for advanced material characterization. The role demands strong expertise in optical systems and precision measurement techniques. Experience with CW and high-power femtosecond (fs) lasers, including the design and construction of free-space optical setups and optical alignment, is required. Experience with pump-probe spectroscopy will be considered an advantage. Key tasks include developing and maintaining BLS microscopy systems, including femtosecond frequency comb–driven setups. Performing BLS measurements and analysis on spintronic nanodevices and a variety of material systems. This role plays a central part in driving forward research on cutting-edge optical microscopy techniques for material and device characterization. You will work together with PhD students and postdocs, contributing to both experimental design and analysis to drive research forward. Additional responsibilities include publishing findings in scientific articles, contributing to team publications, and presenting results at scientific conferences. Eligibility Eligible to be employed as a researcher is a person who has a Swedish doctoral degree or has a foreign degree that is deemed to correspond to a doctoral degree. Qualifications We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated strong scientific skills in the following areas: optical systems, optical spectroscopy, and optical microscopy techniques. Experience with Brillouin Light Scattering (BLS) or Raman microscopy, femtosecond frequency combs, and the excitation and detection of acoustic and spin waves is considered a plus. Scientific merit will primarily be assessed based on peer-reviewed publications in scientific journals and presentations at international conferences. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment 6 months Extent: 100% Location: Department of Physics, Gothenburg First day of employment: 5 October 2026 Appointment procedure Please apply online. The application shall include: - Cover letter with an explanation of why you apply for the position - CV including scientific publications - Copy of exam certificate - Two referees (name, telephone no, relation) Contact details for the position For further information regarding the position and employment procedure, please contact: Johan Åkerman, Professor, johan.akerman@physics.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg: www.gu.se/english/job-opportunities/union-representatives How to apply In order to apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to register an account in our online recruitment system. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure that the application is complete in accordance with the instructions in the job advertisement, and that it is submitted before the deadline. The selection of candidates is made on the basis of the qualifications registered in the application. The application deadline is : 2026-09-10 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41267": {
      "title": "PhD position in global change biology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | PhD position in global change biology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. PhD position in global change biology Reference number PAR 2026/640 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. PhD position in global change biology At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) we have teaching and research activities that stretch from the alpine ecosystem, through forests, cultivated land and streams, all the way into the marine environment. In these environments we study different levels of biological organisation from genes, individuals, and populations, to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics, and combinations of these fields to understand the impact of natural and anthropogenic changes of the environment. The department is placed at two different localities: in Natrium at Medicinaregatan 7B in Gothenburg and at the Kristineberg marine research station operated by the Marine Infrastructure at the University of Gothenburg. The current employment is based at Natrium, with the possibility to conduct research projects at the marine station. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies We offer The University of Gothenburg is a state authority, which means special benefits, more holidays and a great pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . Project description Climate change and the increased frequency, intensity, and duration of heatwaves pose growing threats to the persistence of animal biodiversity. A central challenge in global change biology is to understand why species and populations differ in their vulnerability, so that research can inform conservation, management, and policy. However, we still cannot reliably predict how climate change will affect animal populations across their full life cycle. Accumulating evidence suggests that early life stages can be more sensitive to extreme heat than adults, yet developmental stages remain critically understudied. Current methods used to quantify climate vulnerability are also often not directly comparable between embryos and later life stages, which has further limited our ability to identify critical windows of sensitivity to climate change. New methodological advances now allow us to standardise how we compare vulnerability across life stages, opening up exciting opportunities to understand how climate change affects all stages of life. The central question of this PhD project is: How and why does thermal sensitivity vary across the life cycle, and what are the consequences for animal populations under climate change? This fully funded PhD project is part of a research program funded by the Swedish Research Council (Vetenskapsrådet) on the vulnerability of fish life stages to climate change. The PhD project is expected to combine empirical research with evidence synthesis and comparative analyses. While some projects will primarily focus on fish, comparative studies can be broader in scope, and there will be opportunities to study other taxa (e.g., freshwater and marine invertebrates). The project plan is flexible in its scope, and the PhD student will be encouraged to develop original questions and take increasing intellectual ownership of their research agenda. The applicant is also expected to take part in collaborations at both local and global scales, and contribute to the Thermal Ecology Alliance. Therefore, the PhD student will have excellent opportunities to develop a large collaborative network and address broad questions in global change biology. The projects will be primarily supervised by Patrice Pottier, and co-supervised by Prof. Fredrik Jutfelt at the University of Gothenburg. The PhD student will have meaningful opportunities to shape the intellectual and collaborative culture of the group, while collaborating more broadly within the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences and beyond. The student will benefit from at least one supervisory meeting per week, frequent and structured support during the early stages of all projects, mentoring and career development guidance, and gradual transfer of responsibility as the PhD student develops independence. Expectations, working ethics, and supervision needs will be discussed openly at the start of the position, and revisited through mutual feedback and open communication. The PhD student will have opportunities to develop skills in experimental design, laboratory and field research on animals, statistical modelling and programming, evidence synthesis, climate modelling, open and reproducible research, scientific writing, teaching, communication, as well as leadership, project, and people management. Training will be tailored to the career objectives of the student. Duties The principal duty is to undertake doctoral education and complete a doctoral thesis under supervision. This includes developing theoretical understanding, methodological expertise, analytical skills, scientific thinking, and increasing research independence. The PhD student will: Develop research questions and study designs; Conduct laboratory experiments and, where relevant, field sampling or environmental monitoring; Collect, manage, analyse, and interpret data using transparent and reproducible workflows; Conduct evidence synthesis, meta-analysis, comparative analyses, or environmental modelling; Comply with relevant animal-welfare, ethical, data-management, and health-and-safety requirements; Communicate results through the doctoral thesis, scientific publications, seminars, conferences, and other appropriate formats; Participate constructively in departmental and collegial activities Contribute to leading and managing collaborations Periods of fieldwork at Kristineberg marine station or research visits elsewhere in Sweden or abroad may form part of the project. The need, timing, and accessibility of such activities will be discussed and planned with the PhD student. As part of the employment, the PhD student may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout the study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Eligibility To meet the specific entry requirements for third-cycle studies, applicants must: have a second-cycle (advanced-level) degree in a relevant* subject area in the natural sciences, or have completed studies for at least 60 higher education credits at a second-cycle level in relevant subject areas in the natural sciences, or have completed a corresponding program of relevance to the planned third-cycle program, in Sweden or in another country, or have equivalent qualifications. *Relevant subjects for the planned third-cycle education include ecology, evolutionary biology, animal physiology, zoology, marine or freshwater biology, environmental science, quantitative biology, or another subject judged relevant to the project. Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. It is a requirement that the applicants have: A clear motivation for research in global change biology and an ability to identify scientific questions relevant to the broad aims of the project Experience, relative to opportunity, in planning, conducting, and interpreting a substantial piece of work, such as a Master’s thesis or a scientific publication Excellent communication skills in written and spoken English, at a level sufficient for doctoral education and working in an international environment High levels of curiosity, independence, and excellent organisational skills Evidence of critical thinking, problem solving, and an ability to learn new concepts or methods A willingness to work with quantitative evidence and develop skills in statistical analysis and reproducible research A willingness to contribute to teaching, mentoring, and communication to diverse audiences A willingness to take leadership roles and manage collaborations An ability to receive and give constructive feedback, and work collaboratively and respectfully with people who may have different disciplinary, cultural, or personal backgrounds A commitment to scientific integrity, fairness, and open science principles Other desirable qualifications are: General knowledge in ecology, evolutionary biology and zoology Experience in experimental research with animals or other biological systems Skills in statistical analysis in R or another programming environment Experience in field ecology, animal sampling, or environmental monitoring Experience with evidence synthesis, meta-analysis, or other comparative methods Experience in data management, open science, and reproducible analytical workflows Experience publishing research in scientific journals All qualifications and achievements will be assessed relative to opportunity. Assessment will focus on the ideas, ambitions, and potential demonstrated by the applicant rather than publication counts, institutional prestige, or the linearity of their career path. The application should be written in English and must include: A short cover letter (max 2 pages) describing why the applicant is interested in this position and how they meet the selection criteria A list of qualifications (CV; max 4 pages) Examination certificates and a transcript of courses with grades A copy of the Master thesis or equivalent independent work Employment certificates and other documents deemed important by the applicant The contact details of 1-2 referees (candidates will be notified before contact with the referees is made) Shortlisted candidates will be invited to a structured interview, which will be conducted in English and may be held digitally. Candidates will receive information about the interview format in advance. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Placed at the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences, extent 100%, first day of employment is flexible, but preferably before the end of 2026 (with flexibility to accommodate notice periods and residence permits), or by agreement. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For further information please contact: Patrice Pottier Phone: +46702976011 Email: patrice.pottier@bioenv.gu.se Webpage: patricepottierlab.com Åsa Arrhenius, Head of Department tel. +46 31 786 26 25 Email: asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences, hr@bioenv.gu.se Union Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Applications must be received by: 21 September 2026 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41246": {
      "title": "Postdoc in Type theory and programming languages",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoc in Type theory and programming languages For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoc in Type theory and programming languages Reference number PAR 2026/633 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The department of Computer Science and Engineering is strongly international, with approximately 300 employees from over 50 countries. The department is a fully integrated department with the University of Gothenburg and Chalmers University of Technology as principals. The postdoc position is placed in the Logic and Types unit in the Division for Computing science at the department, with the University of Gothenburg as the employer. Subject area: Type theory and programming languages Subject area description The project is about compilation of cubical type theory: the construction of a compiler that translates from cubical type theory to (hopefully reasonably efficient) executable code. This includes proving that the compiler is correct. Ideally the compiler should also support erasure: removal of computationally irrelevant pieces of code. Job assignments The position is a full-time position for two years in the Logic and types research group at the Department of Computer Science and Engineering. The successful applicant will be expected to conduct high-quality research and to publish in leading international venues. The applicant should be able to work both independently and as part of a team on research related broadly to the goals of the project, and communicate the results verbally and in writing. The position may also include teaching responsibilities for up to 20% of the workload, such as supervising Bachelor's and Master's thesis projects, as well as the possible co-supervision of PhD students. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree in a relevant area according to the specific position stated in the advertisement, for example computer science or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment Competitive candidates are expected to have a strong background, with an excellent publication record, experience of working with cubical type theory, and ideally also experience of programming language implementation/compilation. However, we also welcome candidates working in nearby areas. Experience of supervising doctoral students, master's students and teaching in third cycle courses will also be considered as a merit. The applicant should be able to work both independently and as part of a team. The abilities to cooperate and to take own initiatives are important qualities together with accuracy and interest in research. When evaluating the applicants, particular emphasis will be put on research skills in the relevant research field. Whereas both Swedish and English are used in undergraduate courses, English is the working language for research at the Department of Computer Science and Engineering. Thus, both verbal and written fluency in English is mandatory for candidates. Employment Type of employment: Fixed-term employment, 24 months, central agreement Extent: 100 % of full time Location: Department of Computer Science and Engineering /Division of Computing science, Logic and Types unit First day of employment: 2026-11-05 or after mutual agreement. For further information: Regarding the project, please contact Nils Anders Danielsson (e-mail: nad@cse.gu.se ). Regarding the position, please contact Richard Torkar (e-mail: richard.torkar@gu.se ). Regarding the appointment procedure, please contact Jenny Lind (e-mail: jenny.lind@cse.gu.se ). Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position How to apply Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application must be written in English. The Department aims to actively improve our gender balance and we work broadly with equality projects. Equality and diversity are substantial foundations in all activities at the University and Department. We therefore welcome a diversity of applicants with different backgrounds and experiences. The selection of candidates is made based on the qualifications registered in the application. You need to provide: CV: (Please name the document as: CV, Family name, Ref. number) including: CV, include complete list of publications; Previous teaching and pedagogical experiences; Two references that we can contact. Personal letter: (Please name the document as: Personal letter, Family name, Ref. number) , 1-3 pages where you: Introduce yourself; Describe your previous research fields and main research results; Describe your future goals and future research focus. Other documents: Attested copies of completed education, grades and other certificates. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-29 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41232": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Microbiology",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Microbiology For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Microbiology Reference number PAR 2026/631 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Molecular and Clinical Medicine, at the Institute of Medicine, conducts translational research primarily on metabolism, diabetes, obesity and cardiovascular disease in order to improve health and healthcare in the community. The overall goal is to create an excellent translational research environment of a very high international standard. We have more bacteria than our own cells in the body and these bacteria interact with each other in ecosystems, the microbiota, which can affect how we feel. By combining clinical samples and gnotobiotic animal models, we have shown that the gut microbiota affects our physiology and metabolism. We are now looking for a postdoctoral researcher who can analyze the gut microbiota and isolate specific microbial modulators for metabolic diseases by quantifying and characterizing bacterial species, strains, and genomes with a focus on diet-microbe and microbe-microbe interactions within the gut microbiota. Read more about the research group here: https://backhedlab.org/ Subject area Microbiology Subject area description The gut microbiota contains approximately 1,000 times more genes than the human genome and constitutes a complex ecosystem that influences host physiology through the production of bioactive metabolites. Despite major advances in microbiome research, it remains largely unknown how individual bacterial species and strain-level genetic variation contribute to microbiome function and the development of metabolic diseases. This project aims to identify and characterize bacterial species and strains within Lachnospiraceae and Oscillospiraceae, and particularly the genus Faecalibacterium , one of the most abundant and functionally important bacterial groups in the healthy human gut microbiota. Duties The successful candidate will investigate the functional diversity of Faecalibacterium and its interactions with other gut bacteria using an integrated experimental and bioinformatic approach. The project combines comparative genomic and bioinformatic analyses to determine how strain-level genetic variation shapes bacterial metabolism and functional capacity with physiological characterization of bacterial growth, substrate utilization, and production of bioactive metabolites under defined anaerobic culture conditions. Building on our observation that Faecalibacterium abundance is associated with increased stability of the gut microbial community, potentially through interactions with members of the Lachnospiraceae and Oscillospiraceae families, the candidate will also isolate and characterize novel bacterial strains from faecal samples obtained from healthy donors. These isolates will be used in experimental models to investigate microbial interactions, cross-feeding mechanisms, and their effects on host metabolism. The project integrates clinical cohort data, large-scale genomic analyses, anaerobic cultivation, metabolomics, and experimental model systems to identify microbial mechanisms and bioactive metabolites that contribute to metabolic health and may provide the foundation for future microbiome-based treatments for cardiometabolic diseases. Eligibility To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree in a relevant subject, or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Documented experience in the preparation, optimisation and development of culture media for strictly anaerobic human gut bacteria, including YCFA fatty acid medium, as well as cultivation and isolation of strictly anaerobic gut bacteria under anaerobic conditions, particularly using Coy chambers or equivalent anaerobic cultivation systems, is required. Documented experience in genome-based phylogenetic identification and functional characterisation of bacterial species and strains is also required, including analyses using tools such as Protologger, phylogenetic analyses and phylogenetic tree reconstruction, average nucleotide identity (ANI), digital DNA–DNA hybridisation (dDDH), and functional genome annotation using software such as Prokka and COGs/EggNOG. Experience in programming and statistical data analysis using R, together with excellent written and spoken English, is also required. Experience in the isolation, cultivation and physiological characterisation of human gut commensals belonging to the Lachnospiraceae and Oscillospiraceae families, particularly butyrate-producing and/or fibre-degrading bacteria, is considered highly meritorious. Experience with faecal cultures derived from human samples, including selective and enrichment-based isolation strategies under strictly anaerobic conditions, is also considered highly meritorious. Additional merit will be given to applicants with experience in assessing bacterial metabolism of dietary components, including dietary fibres, complex carbohydrates and other diet-derived substrates. Experience in the physiological and functional characterisation of strictly anaerobic gut bacteria, including the use of API kits or other phenotypic methods for bacterial characterisation, particularly of Faecalibacterium and related human gut anaerobes, is also considered meritorious. International research experience, for example through doctoral or postdoctoral training abroad, is also considered meritorious. Great importance will be attached to the applicant's ability to collaborate and interact effectively with researchers and clinicians in a multidisciplinary research environment. Great emphasis will also be placed on personal suitability, including the ability to take initiative, work independently, demonstrate flexibility, and apply strong analytical and problem-solving skills. Employment Employment type: Fixed-term, 36months. Extent: 100% Location: Institution of medicin, Gothenburg Start date: 2026-11-01 or by agreement Contact information Fredrik Bäckhed Professor +46(0)702182355 Fredrik.Backhed@wlab.gu.se Malin Levin Head of department malin.levin@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Information for International Applicants Visit the webpage Join us - jobs and career (skapa länk till sidan: https://www.gu.se/en/medicine/about-us/join-us-jobs-and-career ) for more information on the Institute of Medicine and what opportunities we have for you. Discover our world-class research and research infrastructure, as well as what the benefits are for you and your family. Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Please attach PhD certificate with the application, if the PhD certificate has not yet been issued, it needs to be attached latest at the time when the employment decision is to be made. Applicants should attach a cover letter including a brief description of previous experience in anaerobic cultivation, isolation and characterization of human gut bacteria, as well as bioinformatic/genome-based analysis. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-07 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41216": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Experimental evaluation of the combined effects of changes associated with ocean alkalinity enhancement (OAE) implementation",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Experimental evaluation of the combined effects of changes associated with ocean alkal For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Experimental evaluation of the combined effects of changes associated with ocean alkalinity enhancement (OAE) implementation Reference number PAR 2026/628 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) we have teaching and research activities that stretch from the alpine ecosystem, through forests, cultivated land and streams, all the way into the marine environment. In these environments we study different levels of biological organisation from genes, individuals and populations, to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics and combinations of these fields in order to understand the impact of natural and anthropogenic changes of the environment. The department is placed at two different localities: in Natrium at Medicinaregatan 7B in Gothenburg and at the Kristineberg marine research station operated by the Marine Infrastructure at the University of Gothenburg. The current position is placed at the Kristineberg Marine Research Station, Fiskebäckskil. We offer The University of Gothenburg is a state authority, which means special benefits, more holidays and a great pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . Subject area Experimental evaluation of the combined effects of changes associated with ocean alkalinity enhancement (OAE) implementation Subject area description Ocean Alkalinity Enhancement (OAE) is a marine Carbon Dioxide Removal (mCDR) technique that may have a significant role in facilitating large scale CO 2 removal to substantially reduce global warming and ocean acidification. However considerable uncertainties and knowledge gaps remain regarding its efficacy, technological and economic viability, its environmental and ecological impacts and its governance. This 2 years postdoctoral position is part of the EU Horizon project OAeSIS ( https://oaesis.eu/ ), a diverse, interdisciplinary team of leading experts in natural science, law, governance and socio-economic assessments. One key aspect is quantifying the impacts of OAE on key species, communities and biodiversity, including interactions with other marine ecosystem stressors - assessing the feasibility and sustainability of OAE as a tool to counteract climate change and ocean acidification The position specifically focuses on resolving the combined effects of changes associated with different OAE implementation methods through experiments using a range of marine organisms. This will allow to produce performance curves for key drivers associated with OAE deployments, multiple stressors experiments, and modelization of performance landscapes for different OAE methods using experimental data and literature review. This will be done in close collaboration with key partners within the consortium, particularly with the University of Copenhagen and the International Atomic Energy Agency. The position will contribute to enabling informed decision and policy-making regarding the potential application of this leading mCDR approach as a climate mitigation tool. Duties Task #1 - To resolve the non-linear biological effects and potential interactions for all main drivers (TA, pH, trace elements) associated with OAE deployment combined with modelling. Evaluation of state-of-the-art using meta-analysis; Fill-up the gaps by resolving performance curves for each driver using key species and life-history stages in laboratory experiments; Development of a mechanistic model for biological impact of OAE deployment at the species level; Use the model to project impacts under different scenarios. Task #2 - Experimentally evaluate the model developed in Task #1 using a multiple stressor experimental approach, identify potential novel interactions and use the collected data to improve the mechanistic model Perform complex multiple stressors experiments Improve the mechanistic model developed in Task #1 accounting for potential interactions. The work will thus include: Field sampling of key species (e.g. echinoderms, bivalves); Perform single and multiple drivers experiments manipulating the carbonate chemistry and trace elements simulating different methods for OAE deployment; Meta-analysis of the existing literature; Statistical analysis to resolve performance curves and landscapes; Publication of the results and communication with other partners and stakeholders in the OAeSIS project. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria Required qualifications for the employment: The main criterion for selection will be the research potential of the applicant. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree in a field relevant for the research project Excellent communication skills, written and spoken, in English are necessary since we work in an international environment. Specialization in marine biology/eco-physiology, as well as experience in experimental design, marine animal culturing and meta-analysis is required Desirable qualifications for the employment: Experience in manipulation and measurement of seawater carbonate chemistry (e.g. ocean alkalinity enhancement, ocean acidification) Ability to design and perform complex laboratory experiments on a range of marine animals, including early life-stages Understanding of multiple stressors and ability to develop strategies to resolve their combined effects (e.g. ecotoxicology, global change biology) Good statistical skills (e.g. non-linear modeling), including experience in meta-analyses The most qualified candidates, in relation to the assessment criteria in the advertisement, are invited for an interview. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is a temporary employment during 2 years, 100 % of full time, placed at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science. Starting date as soon as possible or upon agreement. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact: Sam Dupont, Senior Lecturer and Associate Professor Phone: +46 766 22 9531 Email: sam.dupont@bioenv.gu.se Webpage: https://www.gu.se/en/about/find-staff/samdupont Åsa Arrhenius, Head of Department tel. +46 31 786 26 25 Email: asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science, hr@bioenv.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Publications that cannot be attached to the application in digital format should be mailed in single copy to: Department of Biological and Environmental Science Att: Sam Dupont University of Gothenburg Box 566 S-451 78 Fiskebäckskil, Sweden Label the envelope \" Postdoctor in Experimental evaluation of the combined effects of changes associated with ocean alkalinity enhancement (OAE) implementation, PAR 2026/628\". The publications must be received no later than the application deadline. The application should preferably be written in English and must include: A cover letter with the applicant’s justification for the application, i.e., that describes how the applicant meets the selection criteria A list of qualifications (CV) Certificate of PhD exam Complete list of publications, including submitted and accepted manuscripts Applications must be received by: 2026-09-07 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41235": {
      "title": "Postdoctor in Botany (Plant Science)",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctor in Botany (Plant Science) For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctor in Botany (Plant Science) Reference number PAR 2026/629 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. With 58 000 students and 6800 employees, the university is a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programs draw researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. At the Department of Biological and Environmental Sciences (BioEnv) we have teaching and research activities that stretch from the alpine ecosystem, through forests, cultivated land and streams, all the way to the marine environment. In these environments we study different levels of biological organization from genes, individuals and populations to communities and ecosystems. We work within ecology, evolution, physiology, systematics, and combinations of these fields to understand the impact of natural and anthropogenic changes of the environment. The department is based at two different localities: at Natrium on Medicinareberget in Gothenburg and at the Kristineberg Center in Fiskebäckskil. The current position is placed at Natrium. This position represents a collaboration between BioEnv and the Gothenburg Botanic Garden (GBG), which is one of the largest botanical gardens in Europe, being ca. 75 hectares in extent and holding roughly 20,000 species from >130 countries in its collections. GBG’s mission is to research and conserve plant diversity, to educate and raise awareness of plants, and to provide a unique visitor experience. The garden hosts important collections of many groups, with research at the garden straddling taxonomy, systematics, evolutionary biology, conservation, genetics, horticulture, and global change and restoration ecology. The GBG staff are diverse, including educators, horticulturists, managers, and scientists. We offer The University of Gothenburg is a state authority, which means special benefits, more holidays and a great pension. You can read more about our employment benefits here . Subject area Botany (Plant Science) Subject area description The postdoctor will work on a Swedish Science Council-funded project using a comprehensive phylogenetic tree of southern African (SA) Asteraceae to evaluate the roles of long-distance dispersal, biome shifts, and environmental change as stimuli of taxonomic and phenotypic diversification. Having undergone a major evolutionary radiation in SA to yield a regional flora comprising ca. 2500 species which has occupied all available environments, from deserts to the alpine zone, Asteraceae offers an ideal system for studying the dynamics of diversification in a continental context. In addition, the physiographic and vegetation diversity of contemporary SA, and a Miocene-Pliocene history involving tectonism and climate change, provide the opportunity to test the agency of contemporary and historical factors as drivers of evolutionary diversification. In addition to the above, the postdoctor will be required to develop an independent research project that makes use of GBG’s living collections and/or contributes to GBG’s conservation agenda. This project may be systematic, address questions relating to trait evolution and/or geographical distribution, or have an explicit conservation focus (e.g., relating to threat assessment and ex situ conservation practices). There is a possibility to participate in international plant collecting trips. The research will be supervised by Prof G Anthony Verboom (University of Gothenburg and GBG), Dr Nicola Bergh (GBG), and Dr Félix Forest (Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew). The postdoctor will also work closely with a PhD student whose research examines the diversification consequences of intercontinental long-distance dispersal. Duties Conduct primary research leading to the publication of at least three papers, two relating to the diversification of SA Asteraceae and one relating to the GBG-based project (see above). Analytical methodologies potentially include bioinformatic processing of sequence data, phylogenomic analysis, GIS-based spatial analysis, and comparative macroevolutionary analysis. Attend local and/or international conferences for the purpose of presenting research findings and contribute to fundraising to support such travels. Deliver seminars and participate in journal clubs and other academic fora at BioEnv and GBG. Potentially contribute to teaching in BioEnv, up to 10% of full-time employment. Organize and run a limited number of training events for GBG scientific and curatorial staff, such as a series of short introductory R workshops (including Rstudio, ggplot, and tidyverse). Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. Assessment criteria The suitability of applicants will be assessed based on (i) documentary evidence submitted at the time of application, (ii) a short oral presentation given at the time of interview, and (iii) response to interview questions. A subset of applicants will be invited for interview. Required qualifications for employment: PhD in evolutionary biology or systematics. Experience of data management and statistical analysis in R. Working knowledge of phylogenetic inference and comparative macroevolutionary analysis Ability to work well both independently and as part of a group. Organizational skills and ability to co-lead and coordinate a large project which involves processing and analysis of large amounts of data. Interest in field work. Excellent English communication skills, both written and spoken. Desirable qualifications for employment: Advanced knowledge of plant taxonomy, systematics, and evolution. Experience of processing and analyzing high-throughput genomic data, spatial data (e.g., SDM), and/or big data (i.e., on an HPC cluster). Experience of working in a team. Willingness to mentor undergraduate students. Driver’s license. Personal qualifications: Given the expectation of independent research development, we require someone who is independent and self-motivated. Since the postdoctor will be expected to collaborate closely with a PhD student and colleagues at both BioEnv and GBG, we are also looking for someone who is collegial, a good communicator, and capable of working as part of a team. The most qualified candidates, in relation to the assessment criteria in the advertisement, are invited for an interview. Regulations for the evaluation of qualifications for academic positions are given in Chapter 4, Section 3 - 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance. Employment The employment is temporary, lasting two years, 100% full-time, placed at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science. Starting date 11 January 2027 or, by agreement, as soon as possible thereafter. Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact: Professor G Anthony Verboom Phone: +46 76 6185923 Email: tony.verboom@bioenv.gu.se Webpage: https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=QT3_z5IAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao Åsa Arrhenius, Head of Department tel. +46 31 786 26 25 Email: asa.arrhenius@bioenv.gu.se If you have any questions about the employment process, please contact HR at the Department of Biological and Environmental Science, hr@bioenv.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. Publications that cannot be attached to the application in digital format should be mailed in single copy to: Department of Biological and Environmental Science Att: G Anthony Verboom University of Gothenburg Box 463 S-405 30 Gothenburg, Sweden Label the envelope \"Postdoctoral Fellowship in Botany (Plant Science), PAR 2026/629\". The publications must be received no later than the application deadline. The application should preferably be written in English and must include: A cover letter describing the applicant’s motivation for applying and explaining how the applicant meets the selection criteria. A list of qualifications (CV) Certificate of PhD exam Complete list of publications, including submitted and accepted manuscripts Applications must be received by: 2026-09-14 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41162": {
      "title": "Postdoktor för att studera lipid- och membranhomeostas hos C. elegans",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoktor för att studera lipid- och membranhomeostas hos C. elegans For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoktor för att studera lipid- och membranhomeostas hos C. elegans Reference number PAR 2026/462 Göteborgs universitet möter samhällets utmaningar med mångsidig kunskap. 58 000 studenter och 6800  medarbetare gör universitetet till en stor och inspirerande arbetsplats. Stark forskning och attraktiva utbildningar lockar forskare och studenter från hela världen. Med ny kunskap och nya perspektiv bidrar Göteborgs universitet till en bättre framtid. Institutionen för kemi och molekylärbiologi bedriver högkvalitativ forskning och utbildning inom kemiska vetenskaper och molekylära livsvetenskaper. Vår forskning och utbildning fokuserar på en djup förståelse av kemiska och biologiska processer i celler och i miljön. Vi ansvarar för forskarutbildning inom ämnesområdet Naturvetenskap med inriktning mot kemi, biofysik, biologi och utbildningsvetenskap. Därtill är institutionen värd för utbildningsprogram i molekylärbiologi, genomik och systembiologi, kemi, organisk kemi och läkemedelskemi samt receptarieutbildningen.  Institutionen är en internationell miljö med tvärvetenskapliga samarbeten inom både forskning och utbildning, och bidrar starkt till Göteborgs universitets topprankning inom life science. Institutionens verksamhet bedrivs i Natrium, Medicinareberget i Göteborg. Professor Marc Pilons grupp utför grundforskning om molekylära vägar som reglerar lipidmetabolism och membranhomeostas med mestadels nematoden C. elegans som modellorganism. Arbetsuppgifter Postdoktorn förväntas utföra oberoende/semioberoende forskning som utnyttjar insamlingen av mutanta C. elegans -alleler och/eller Pilon-gruppens expertis för att ytterligare främja vår förståelse av lipid/membranhomeostas. Uppgiften kommer sannolikt att inkludera hypotesformulering, att ställa in och utföra fram genetikscreeningar med C. elegans och karakterisering av vägar med hjälp av kombinationer av genetiska interaktionsstudier, mikroskopi, CRISPR/Cas9-genomredigering bland andra metoder. Kvalifikationer Behörig att anställas som postdoktor är den som avlagt doktorsexamen, eller utländsk examen som bedöms motsvara doktorsexamen. Detta behörighetskrav ska vara uppfyllt senast vid tidpunkten då anställningsbeslutet fattas. Främst bör den komma i fråga som har avlagt doktorsexamen högst tre år före ansökningstidens utgång. Om det finns särskilda skäl kan doktorsexamen ha avlagts tidigare. Övriga krav: Mycket goda kunskaper i engelska, i tal och skrift. Doktorsexamen, eller internationell examen som bedöms likvärdig med doktorsexamen, inom det allmänna området cell- och molekylärbiologi, avlagd högst tre år före sista ansökningsdag. Minst en förstaförfattarpublikation inom det allmänna området cell- och molekylärbiologi. Meriterande: Tidigare erfarenhet av att arbeta med C. elegans. Tidigare erfarenhet av arbete med lipid/membranhomeostas. Anställning Anställningen är tidsbegränsad på 2 år (en förlängning med ytterligare ett år kan vara möjlig) på heltid (100%) med placering på Institutionen för kemi och molekylärbiologi vid Göteborgs universitet. Startar i hösten 2026. Kontaktuppgifter för anställningen Har du frågor om anställningen är du välkommen att kontakta Marc Pilon, marc.pilon@cmb.gu.se. Fackliga organisationer Fackliga företrädare vid Göteborgs universitet hittar du här: https://www.gu.se/jobba-hos-oss/sa-blir-du-en-av-oss#kollektivavtal-och-fackliga-foretradare Ansökan Du söker anställningen via Göteborgs universitets rekryteringsportal genom att klicka på knappen \"Ansök\". Du som sökande ansvarar för att ansökan är komplett i enlighet med annonsen och att den är universitetet tillhanda senast sista ansökningsdag. Ansökan ska skrivas på engelska och innehålla: Meritförteckning/CV inklusive publikationslista. En generell beskrivning av tidigare forskningsarbete och framtida forskningsintressen (max tre sidor). Kontaktuppgifter till minst två referenser. Kopia av examensbevis för doktorsexamen samt ev. andra intyg/betyg som du önskar åberopa. De åberopade meriterna ska vara dokumenterade, bestyrkta och översatta till engelska. Samtliga bilagor ska vara namngivna på ett sätt som tydligt visar dess innehåll. Ansökan ska vara inkommen senast : 2026-09-11 Universitetet arbetar aktivt för en arbetsmiljö med jämställda förhållanden och sätter värde på de kvalitéer mångfald tillför verksamheten. Universitetet tillämpar individuell lönesättning. Enligt Riksarkivets föreskrifter är universitetet skyldigt att förvara ansökningshandlingar i två år efter tillsättningsbeslutet. Om du som sökande till en anställning särskilt begär tillbaka dina handlingar återsänds de när de två åren har förflutit, i annat fall kommer de att gallras ut. Till bemannings- och rekryteringsföretag och till dig som är försäljare: Göteborgs universitet anlitar upphandlad annonsbyrå i samband med rekrytering av personal. Vi undanber oss vänligen men bestämt direktkontakt med bemannings- och rekryteringsföretag samt försäljare av jobbannonser. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41214": {
      "title": "Assistant Researcher in Natural Sciences, specializing in Environmental Science",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Assistant Researcher in Natural Sciences, specializing in Environmental Science For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Assistant Researcher in Natural Sciences, specializing in Environmental Science Reference number PAR 2026/616 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Earth Sciences has approximately 75 employees working in geology, physical geography and ecosystem science. Our main research themes are “Climate and Climate Change”, “Earth Surface Processes” and “The Geosphere” and the research strategy of the department emphasizes on further development of Earth System Science. The department’s mission is to develop and conduct research, education and community engagement that deepen the understanding of processes in the Earth system and the effects of human impact. We strive to provide an inclusive and diverse working environment and a family-friendly workplace. More information is available on our website: Department of Earth Sciences, University of Gothenburg . The Hydrogeology research group ( https://www.gu.se/en/research/hydrogeology-group ) at the Department of Earth Sciences belongs to the “Geosphere” theme and is involved in fundamental research, teaching, and stakeholder activities in the field of hydrogeology, contaminant hydrogeology and hydrogeochemistry. The Hydrogeology Group focuses on two main research topics including a) fresh water resources under changing environmental and climatic conditions and b) the behavior of contaminants in the subsurface. The hydrogeology group has strong relations with international research groups and private companies in Germany, Switzerland, Canada, USA and France. The group also host a large pool of state-of-the-art equipment for stable isotope and elemental analyses. The Hydrogeology research group is recruiting an Assistant Researcher to contribute to the project: “Artificial groundwater recharge – a shortcut for harmful poly- and perfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) into groundwater systems and drinking water supplies – can we avoid this?” Job assignments The successful applicant will investigate the infiltration of PFASs into groundwater systems at artificial groundwater recharge plants and will develop methods to avoid this. The project will be conducted in collaboration with Professor Lutz Ahrens (Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences (SLU), Philip McLeaf (Uppsala Vatten and Avfall) and Britt-Marie Pott (Envidan). A major part of the project will include field work at two artificial groundwater recharge plants in Uppsala and Skåne (Sweden) combined with laboratory investigations at the Earth Science Department of the University of Gothenburg. Qualifications The applicant must, at the time of appointment, hold a doctoral degree in environmental geology, contaminant hydrogeology, environmental chemistry, or a closely related field. The successful candidate should have demonstrated experience in PFAS analysis using LC–MS/MS, including solid-phase extraction pre-concentration techniques. Experience conducting column experiments and field sampling of surface water and groundwater, particularly at managed aquifer recharge sites, is also required. Experience with numerical modelling or simulation is considered an asset and excellent written and spoken English is essential. Employment Employment type: Temporary full-time employment for 7 months, from 1 November 2026 to 30 June 2027. Location: Department of Earth Sciences, University of Gothenburg. Information regarding the position For further information regarding the position, please contact Dr. Philipp Wanner, philipp.wanner@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ How to apply In order to apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to register an account in our online recruitment system. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure that the application is complete in accordance with the instructions in the job advertisement, and that it is submitted before the deadline. The application should include: A cover letter with a motivation that describes the applicant’s suitability for the position (maximum one A4 page) A Curriculum vitae including education, qualifications, and previous experience A copy of examination certificates and a transcript of courses with grades A copy of the doctoral thesis and/or master’s thesis (or equivalent) Other documents deemed important by the applicant Contact information for at least two references that are familiar with the applicant’s qualifications. Closing date: 2026-09-06 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41198": {
      "title": "Doctoral student in Medical Science",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Doctoral student in Medical Science - Role of the immune system in ulcerative colitis For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Doctoral student in Medical Science - Role of the immune system in ulcerative colitis Reference number PAR 2026/612 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. Doctoral position in Medical Science Project title: Immunological mechanisms for predicting disease course in ulcerative colitis The Department of microbiology and immunology is located at the Institute of Biomedicine  at the Faculty of Medicine at Sahlgrenska Academy, University of Gothenburg. General information about being a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg can be found on the university's doctoral student pages. https://www.gu.se/en/doctoral-studies Duties The PhD education in Medical Science comprises carrying out a scientific project and completing at least 30 credits of courses at third-cycle level. The doctoral student must also write a scientific compilation thesis or monograph corresponding to at least 120 credits. For more information about third-cycle studies at Sahlgrenska Academy, see https://www.gu.se/en/sahlgrenska-akademin/doctoral-studies Doctoral studies comprises four years of full-time study, and leads to a doctoral degree. As part of your employment as a doctoral student, you may have departmental duties corresponding to up to 20 % of full-time employment, distributed throughout your study period, and result in a corresponding extension of the studies. Departmental duties usually consist of teaching at first- and second-cycle levels, but may also include research and administration. Project description: Ulcerative colitis is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease characterised by periods of remission and relapse. Although modern treatment often leads to clinical, endoscopic and histological healing, many patients relapse. Why an apparently healed intestinal mucosa remains biologically vulnerable is a central unanswered question. This PhD project aims to identify the molecular mechanisms that determine whether remission remains stable or the disease relapses. The project is based on the hypothesis that the intestinal microenvironment, shaped by interactions between the microbiota, microbial metabolites and host regulatory signals, is not fully restored during remission, which may increase susceptibility to renewed inflammatory stimuli. The PhD student will be part of BioLabX, Sweden's national research school in Biomedical Laboratory Science. The project is particularly relevant for applicants with a background in Biomedical Laboratory Science. The PhD student will combine patient-oriented research with experimental models to investigate how the intestinal microenvironment influences immune function during remission. The work includes handling and analysis of human patient samples, functional cell models using human macrophages, advanced cell culture, flow cytometry and molecular biology, as well as metabolomics and lipidomics. Bioinformatic and statistical methods will be used to integrate molecular data with clinical follow-up and identify biomarkers that may predict relapse. The research environment is translational and interdisciplinary, with close collaboration between preclinical researchers, clinical gastroenterologists and national research networks. Through BioLabX, the PhD student will have access to a national network, specialised doctoral courses and pedagogical training. The PhD student will also participate in teaching Biomedical Laboratory Science in Swedish. Eligibility Doctoral education requires general eligibility and, where appropriate, specific eligibility as set out in the general syllabus for the subject. The general eligibility requirements for doctoral studies are: having completed a degree at second-cycle level, or the fulfilment of course requirements totalling at least 240 credits, of which at least 60 credits must be at second-cycle level, or the acquisition of equivalent knowledge in some other way, either in Sweden or abroad. Specific entry requirements for this subject, according to the general syllabus, are: having completed the English B/6 course or is considered to have acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies Assessment criteria The selection of applicants who meet the basic and specific eligibility requirements will be based on the ability to assimilate the doctoral studies. The assessment shall attach particular importance to documented qualifications for: - specific knowledge and skills within the subject area and related research fields - experience of scientific theory and relevant research methodology - scientific analysis and presentation verified through an academic paper, degree project, scientific journals or the like. Admission and employment Once you have been admitted to doctoral studies, you will be employed as a doctoral student at the University of Gothenburg. The provisions for employment as a doctoral student can be found in ordinance SFS 1993:100. Initial employment as a doctoral student may apply for a maximum of one year, and may be renewed by a maximum of two years at a time. A doctoral student may be employed as a doctoral student for a maximum of eight years, but the total period of employment may not be longer than the equivalent of full-time education at doctoral level for four years. Location: Sahlgrenska Academy, Institute of Biomedicine, Department of microbiology and immunology. Extent: 100%. First day of employment: as agreed. The University applies a local agreement on salaries for doctoral students. Please note, doctoral studies at the institute of Biomedicine require physical presence to conduct the studies. If the admitted applicant needs a residence permit for higher education to pursue studies in Sweden, the institute has the right to revoke the admission decision if the applicant cannot present a valid residence permit no later than at the start of the studies. Contact information For more information about the project please contact the project supervisor, Maria Magnusson, Institute of Biomedicine, e-mail: maria.magnusson@microbio.gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application You can apply to be admitted to doctoral education via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per this notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. You must include the following, in pdf format Personal letter CV Diploma and transcripts showing that you meet with the general entry requirement Proof of completion of English 6 course or the acquired equivalent knowledge through previous studies If your degree is from a university outside of Sweden, make sure that you attach a diploma and full transcripts that makes assessing your degree easier . For information on what you need to submit in order to fulfill the English language requirement see https://www.universityadmissions.se/en/entry-requirements/english-language-requirements/ Applications must be received by: 2026-09-06 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=40996": {
      "title": "International student ambassadors",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | International student ambassadors For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. International student ambassadors Reference number PAR 2026/559 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The University of Gothenburg aims to be an international institution that attracts international students to its programmes and courses. Today we have around 80 Master programmes and an additional ten Bachelor programmes in English that are open to international applicants. To communicate with future students and to help international students get a good start to their studies, we have a team of student ambassadors. Our student ambassadors are enrolled students who represent the University towards prospective students, share their experience of what it is like to be a student here, and assist the University’s Welcome Programme and various events throughout the academic year. We will now hire around 18 international student ambassadors. The role is based at the Communication Unit, which is part of the University's Central Administration. We work with communication in many different areas: press, research communication, web and digital channels, student recruitment, visual identity, and more. Work assignments will also be carried out through the University’s International Centre. Job assignments As an international student ambassador, you are part of a team of 15-20 ambassadors from all over the world with a variety of educational backgrounds and study areas. The main tasks of our student ambassadors are: Communicating with prospective international students with a focus on providing information about studying at the University of Gothenburg. Supporting our Welcome Programme for new international students, as well as events throughout the academic year. Creating and managing social media content: A few student ambassadors will have a particular focus on working with the University’s student Instagram account. Student ambassador tasks are diverse and flexible, and there will be opportunities to get involved in different activities throughout the academic year. Specific examples include: Taking part in and hosting webinars and digital sessions Attending virtual study fairs Taking part in content creation for social media Planning and running events for new students (venue setup and cleanup, taking care of activities during an event, and acting as guides for visitors) Organising social activities for new and current international students Participation in information sessions on different subjects (e.g. life in Sweden, exchange and internship opportunities) For student ambassadors working with content creation: Developing ideas, producing and publishing posts, stories, and reels, and managing the University’s student Instagram account The employment is an intermittent/on-demand employment that is paid on an hourly basis. There are no fixed working hours, and throughout the academic year there will be more or less busy periods. As an international student ambassador, your assignments are flexible, and you can contribute with your own experiences and interests. You can both plan much of your work independently or work together with other ambassadors – of course, always in accordance with your studies. You will be able to conduct your work from home but are also invited to join us for meetings or work sessions at the Communication Unit’s or International Centre’s offices. In your application, please state which programme you are studying, which semester you are in, and which tasks you would most likely be interested in as a student ambassador. If you are interested in working with content creation for social media, please also submit samples of content that you have created or worked on before. Qualifications You are a student at the University of Gothenburg You are studying an international Bachelor or Master programme You have very good proficiency of written and spoken English You are excited to inspire international (prospective) students and to share your own experiences as an international student at the University, in Gothenburg, and in Sweden You are outgoing and comfortable with speaking in front of many people in English, either virtually or in-person, to individuals and in front of a group or on stage You are structured and able to convey information in a clear and precise manner Meritorious: experience with the tasks specified above or similar If you are interested in working with social media content creation: You have experience in creating content for social media, especially Instagram To be suitable for this assignment, you need to have a service-oriented mindset and enjoy working with people. We place great importance on personal qualities such as collaboration skills, motivation, and responsibility. For this role, particular emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is an intermittent employment from 1 October 2026 until 30 September 2027 with placement at the Communication Unit in Gothenburg. Hourly wage: 160 SEK Your job title will be Assistent. The university promotes equality, equity, and diversity in recruitment. Contacts Maria Eriksson, Head of Section, +46 (0)31-786 57 07, maria.eriksson@gu.se Matthias Teich, Communications Officer, +46 (0)31-786 67 19, matthias.teich@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. In your application, please state which programme you are studying, which semester you are in, and which tasks you would most likely be interested in as a student ambassador. If you are interested in working with content creation for social media, please also submit samples of content that you have created or worked on before. Applications must be received by: 6 September 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=40993": {
      "title": "International student ambassadors with focus on Chinese social media",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | International student ambassadors with focus on Chinese social media - 2 vacancies For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. International student ambassadors with focus on Chinese social media - 2 vacancies Reference number PAR 2026/558 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The University of Gothenburg aims to be an international institution that attracts international students to its programmes and courses. Today we have around 80 Master programmes and an additional ten Bachelor programmes in English that are open to international applicants. To communicate with future students and to help international students get a good start to their studies, we have a team of student ambassadors. Our student ambassadors are enrolled students who represent the University towards prospective students, share their experience of what it is like to be a student here, and assist the University’s Welcome Programme and various events throughout the academic year. As part of our international outreach, we are active on a variety of Chinese social media platforms, on which our student ambassadors play a key role in representing the University to prospective students from China. The role is based at the Communication Unit, which is part of the University's Central Administration. We work with communication in many different areas: press, research communication, web and digital channels, student recruitment, visual identity, and more. Work assignments will also be carried out through the University’s International Centre. Job assignments As an international student ambassador, you are part of a team of 15-20 ambassadors from all over the world with a variety of educational backgrounds and study areas. Two of our ambassadors will have a particular focus on the University’s presence on Chinese social media platforms. The main tasks of our student ambassadors are: Creating social media content: You will help represent the University of Gothenburg on platforms such as Weibo, WeChat, and Xiaohongshu, providing information and inspiration through content to prospective students from China Communicating with prospective international students with a focus on providing information about studying at the University of Gothenburg. Supporting our Welcome Programme for new international students, as well as events throughout the academic year. Student ambassador tasks are diverse and flexible, and there will be opportunities to get involved in different activities throughout the academic year. Specific examples include: Taking part in and hosting webinars and digital sessions Attending virtual study fairs Taking part in content creation for social media Planning and running events for new students (venue setup and cleanup, taking care of activities during an event, and acting as guides for visitors) Organising social activities for new and current international students Participation in information sessions on different subjects (e.g. life in Sweden, exchange and internship opportunities) The employment is an intermittent/on-demand employment that is paid on an hourly basis. There are no fixed working hours, and throughout the academic year there will be more or less busy periods. As an international student ambassador, your assignments are flexible, and you can contribute with your own experiences and interests. You can both plan much of your work independently or work together with other ambassadors – of course, always in accordance with your studies. You will be able to conduct your work from home but are also very welcome to join us for meetings or work sessions at the Communication Unit’s or International Centre’s offices. Qualifications You are a student at the University of Gothenburg You are studying an international Bachelor’s or Master’s programme You have very good proficiency of written and spoken Chinese You have very good proficiency of written and spoken English You are excited to inspire international (prospective) students and to share your own experiences as an international student at the University, in Gothenburg, and in Sweden You have experience in creating content for social media, especially the above-mentioned social media platforms You are structured and able to convey information in a clear and precise manner You are comfortable with speaking (virtually) in front of people Meritorious: experience with the tasks specified above or similar To be suitable for this assignment, you need to have a service-oriented mindset and enjoy working with people. We place great importance on personal qualities such as collaboration skills, motivation, and responsibility. For this role, particular emphasis will be placed on personal suitability. Employment The employment is an intermittent employment from 1 October 2026 until 30 September 2027 with placement at the Communication Unit in Gothenburg. Hourly wage: 160 SEK Your job title will be Assistent. The university promotes equality, equity, and diversity in recruitment. Contacts Maria Eriksson, Head of Section, +46 (0)31-786 57 07, maria.eriksson@gu.se Matthias Teich, Communications Officer, +46 (0)31-786 67 19, matthias.teich@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/om-universitetet/jobba-hos-oss/hjalp-for-sokande Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. In your application, please state which programme you are studying, which semester you are in. Please also submit samples of content that you have created or worked on before. Applications must be received by: 6 September 2026. The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=41163": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position to study lipid/membrane homeostasis in C. elegans",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral position to study lipid/membrane homeostasis in C. elegans For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral position to study lipid/membrane homeostasis in C. elegans Reference number PAR 2026/462 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology conducts high-quality research and education in chemical sciences and molecular life sciences. Our research and education focuses on a deep understanding of chemical and biological processes in cells and in the environment. We are responsible for postgraduate education in the subject area Natural Sciences with a focus on chemistry, biophysics, biology and educational science. In addition, the department hosts educational programs in molecular biology, genomics and systems biology, chemistry, organic chemistry and pharmaceutical chemistry, as well as prescription education. The department is an international environment with interdisciplinary collaborations in both research and education and contributes strongly to the University of Gothenburg's top ranking in life science. We are located in central premises at Natrium, Medicinareberget, Gothenburg. The group of Professor Marc Pilon performs basic research on molecular pathways that regulate lipid metabolism and membrane homeostasis using mostly the nematode C. elegans as model organism. Duties The postdoctoral fellow would be expected to carry out independent/semi-independent research leveraging the collection of C. elegans mutant alleles and/or the expertise of the Pilon group to further advance our understanding of lipid/membrane homeostasis. Task will likely include hypothesis formulation, setting and performing forward genetics screens using C. elegans and characterization of pathways using combinations of genetic interactions studies, microscopy, CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing among other methods. Qualifications To be eligible for appointment as a postdoctoral researcher, an applicant must have obtained a doctoral degree, or a foreign qualification deemed equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met by the time the appointment decision is made. Preference should be given to applicants who obtained their doctoral degree no more than three years prior to the closing date for applications. In exceptional circumstances, the doctoral degree may have been obtained earlier. Additional requirements: Very good oral and written proficiency in English. PhD, or an international degree deemed equivalent to a PhD, in the general field of cell and molecular biology, completed no more than three years before the last date for applications. At least one first-author publication in the general field of cell and molecular biology. Meritorious: Previous experience working with C. elegans. Previous experience working with lipid/membrane homeostasis. Employment The position is fixed-term for 2 years (an extension of one additional year may be possible) on a full-time basis (100%) with placement at the Department of Chemistry and Molecular Biology at the University of Gothenburg. Start in Fall 2026. Contact information for the post If you have any questions about the position, please contact Marc Pilon, marc.pilon@cmb.gu.se. Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application To apply for a position at the University of Gothenburg, you have to create an account in our recruitment system. Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. The application must be written in English and include: Résumé/CV, including a list of publications, A general description of past research and future research interests (no more than three pages), Contact information of at least two references, Copy of the doctoral degree certificate, and other certificates/grades that you wish to be considered. The qualifications claimed must be documented, certified and translated into English. All attachments must be labelled in a way that clearly shows their content. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-11 The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    },
    "https://web103.reachmee.com/ext/I005/1035/main?site=7&validator=9b89bead79bb7258ad55c8d75228e5b7&lang=UK&rmpage=job&rmjob=40871": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral fellow in Political Science",
      "description": "Göteborgs universitet | Postdoctoral fellow in Political Science For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. Postdoctoral fellow in Political Science Reference number PAR 2026/497 The University of Gothenburg tackles society’s challenges with diverse knowledge. 58 000 students and 6800 employees make the university a large and inspiring place to work and study. Strong research and attractive study programmes attract researchers and students from around the world. With new knowledge and new perspectives, the University contributes to a better future. The Department of Political Science is an international academic environment that welcomes students, researchers, and staff from all over the world. Our research covers topics such as political institutions, elections, public opinion and democracy, international and European politics, environmental politics, corruption, and political ideas. The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg is ranked as the best in Sweden and one of the top 10–15 in Europe in its subject area, according to the global Shanghai ranking of academic subjects for 2025. We organize a large number of research seminars and workshops each year in the various research areas of the department. We offer education at all levels in both Swedish and English. In total, around 1,400 people study with us, and we have around 170 employees. For more information about the Department, visit https://www.pol.gu.se . The Department of Political Science at the University of Gothenburg invites applications for a postdoctoral fellow in political science, 100% of full-time, for a two-year appointment with the possibility of extension. The position focuses on research in political behavior and comparative politics, with a particular focus on parenthood, health, pregnancy, and social outcomes. Research project and research environment The Gothenburg Research Program on Pregnancy and Politics (PregDem) is a cross-disciplinary research program bringing together political scientists, midwives, obstetricians, and other researchers at the University of Gothenburg, Sahlgrenska University Hospital, Princeton University, and McGill University. Its overarching aim is to deepen understanding of how pregnancy and becoming a parent affect people’s relationship to society, work, and politics – and how political and institutional conditions, in turn, shape experiences of pregnancy, care, and early parenthood. The program also includes research on whether and how the policy preferences of pregnant people and their partners are taken up by political parties, parliaments, and public policy. PregDem is led by Professor Elin Naurin and funded by the Swedish Research Council, the Knut and Alice Wallenberg Foundation, and the University of Gothenburg. More information is available at https://www.gu.se/en/pregdem . We are looking for a postdoctoral fellow who wants to build a strong research profile in an interdisciplinary environment where questions about democracy, health, gender, parenthood, and inequality meet. The postdoctoral fellow will be affiliated with two ongoing research projects within PregDem, with established frameworks, data sources, and research questions. For researchers interested in empirical social science, the projects offer strong opportunities to work with rich data, robust research designs, and questions that reach beyond the specific substantive area. At the same time, the position offers room to shape the projects’ continued direction, develop analyses, publications, and possible sub-studies, and broaden their expertise. The two projects are briefly described below. Project 1: Having a child, political participation, and representation . In this project, we investigate how having a child affects women’s political participation and opportunities to participate in representative politics. The study uses a research design based on IVF treatment, comparing women whose IVF treatment leads to a child with otherwise similar women whose treatment does not lead to a child. Through linked Swedish register data, the project can follow outcomes such as voter turnout, candidacy and elected office, as well as broader labor market and career outcomes over time. The postdoctoral fellow will contribute to developing theory and the empirical strategy, analyzing data, and writing scientific articles on how having a child may shape political participation, representation, and career development. Project 2: Perinatal health, measurement strategies, and inequality in social outcomes . In this project, we examine differences in perinatal health and care, as well as possible long-term consequences of health complications during pregnancy, childbirth, and the early period with a young child. One part of the project uses data from the Swedish Pregnancy Register and the Swedish Neonatal Care Register to develop comparable measures of health need, care burden, and complications. Another part links these measures to sources such as LISA and electoral registers to analyze inequalities in care relative to need and possible consequences for political participation and labor market outcomes. The project thus offers scope for both methodological development and substantive analyses of health, inequality, and civic participation, while also contributing to theoretical development in the field. Research group and collaborations . The postdoctoral fellow will be part of the PregDem research environment and work closely with researchers in political science, obstetrics, reproductive health, and related fields. The projects involve, among others, researchers from the University of Gothenburg, Princeton University, and McGill University. These include Elin Naurin, Elias Markstedt, Moa Frödin Gruneau, Verena Sengpiel, Maria Forslund, Karolina Linden, and Helen Elden at the University of Gothenburg, as well as Tanushree Goyal at Princeton University and Dietlind Stolle at McGill University. Subject area Political science Subject area description Political science is the study of politics. Duties Employment as a postdoctoral fellow gives the employee an early opportunity to develop their independence as a researcher after completing their doctoral degree and to create the conditions for further qualifications. The person employed as a postdoctoral fellow will mainly conduct research. Teaching may be included in the duties, but for no more than one-fifth of the working time. The work will be carried out in close collaboration with the research group and may include responsibility for sub-studies. The postdoctoral fellow is expected to independently develop theoretical and empirical ideas at the intersection of political science, health research, economics, register-based methods, and research on inequality. Duties include leading and contributing to scientific publications, developing and conducting quantitative analyses, and preparing, linking, and analyzing register and survey data. This may include data from the Swedish Pregnancy Register, the Swedish Neonatal Care Register, LISA, electoral registers, other Swedish administrative registers, and previous survey studies within PregDem. The work may also include ethics applications, documentation, measurement strategies, data preparation, project meetings, workshops, and international collaborations. The position offers good opportunities for supervision and mentoring, conference participation, methodological training and further skills development, and, where relevant, additional data collection within the framework of the projects. Eligibility The eligibility criteria for employing teaching staff are set out in Chapter 4 of the Higher Education Ordinance and in the Appointment Procedure for Teaching Posts at the University of Gothenburg. To be eligible for appointment as a postdoc, the applicant is required to have a doctoral degree, a doctoral degree in art or a foreign degree that is deemed to be equivalent to a doctoral degree. This eligibility requirement must be met before the employment decision is made. In the first instance, those who have completed their degree no more than three years prior to the end of the application period shall be considered. Those who have completed their degree more than three years prior to the end of the application period may also be considered in the first instance if special grounds exist. Special grounds relate to leave of absence due to illness, parental leave, commissions of trust within union organisations, service within the defence services or other similar circumstances, as well as clinical service or service/assignment relevant to the subject area. For more information regarding eligibility, please see Appointment Procedure Teaching Posts GU 2024-3252 Assessment criteria The assessment will primarily be based on scientific merit and the applicant’s ability to conduct and develop research within the area of the position. Particular weight will be given to the quality of the applicant’s research, the ability to work independently, and the potential to contribute to the projects. The following qualifications are required: A doctoral degree in political science or a relevant related subject no later than the time at which the employment decision is made. Documented research competence relevant to one or more of the research areas of the position. Good ability to independently produce high-quality scientific writing. Excellent written and oral proficiency in English. Meritorious qualifications include experience with quantitative methods, especially causal or quasi-experimental research designs, as well as work with register data, health quality registers, longitudinal survey data, or other large-scale data sources. Experience with quantitative analysis in, for example, Stata, R, or Python is also meritorious. We welcome applicants with research experience in political participation, representation, gender and politics, health inequalities, labor market outcomes, or social inequality. Scientific publications, high-quality manuscripts, interdisciplinary collaboration, project coordination, or responsibility for sub-studies are also meritorious. The work involves engaging with Swedish registers, documentation, and administrative systems. Knowledge of Swedish is not required, but the person employed should be willing to learn about the Swedish context and, where needed, develop relevant language understanding during the employment. To be successful in this position, you should be able to work carefully and independently, communicate well with researchers from different disciplines, and contribute constructively to a research group spanning political science, obstetrics, epidemiology, and related fields. Great emphasis is placed on personal suitability for the position, especially collaborative ability, analytical independence, and the capacity to take responsibility for research tasks within joint projects. We will make an overall assessment, and the applicant who is considered best qualified to carry out and develop the tasks described above will be appointed to the position. Employment The employment is a full-time position limited to two years, with the possibility of a one-year extension, and is currently placed at the Department of Political Science, University of Gothenburg. Starting date: 1 January 2027, or as agreed. For more information, please see Appointment Procedure Teaching Posts GU 2024-3252 Contact information If you have any questions about the position, please contact Elias Markstedt, elias.markstedt@pol.gu.se If you have any questions about the recruitment process, please contact Helena Jensen, helena.jensen@gu.se Unions Union representatives at the University of Gothenburg can be found here: https://www.gu.se/en/work-at-the-university-of-gothenburg/how-to-apply-for-a-position#collective-agreement-and-union-representatives Application Submit your application via the University of Gothenburg’s recruitment portal by clicking the “Apply” button. It is your responsibility to ensure that the application is complete as per the vacancy notice, and that the University receives it by the final application deadline. We kindly ask you to write your application in English, as the application may be reviewed by English-speaking staff. The application should include the following: A letter of application (max. 1 page), outlining how your research background has prepared you to contribute to the projects. It should clearly show the ways in which you meet the requirements specified above and why you would like to work with us. A short outline of possible research ideas relevant to the projects (max. 2 pages, excluding references). A CV (including a list of publications). One writing sample (article or article manuscript). A list of at least three references (with contact information) who may be contacted for letters of recommendation. Applications must be received by: 2026-09-07 Information for International Applicants Choosing a career in a foreign country is a big step. Thus, to give you a general idea of what we and Gothenburg have to offer in terms of benefits and life in general for you and your family/spouse/partner please visit: https://www.gu.se/en/about-the-university/welcome-services https://www.movetogothenburg.com/ The University works actively to achieve a working environment with equal conditions, and values the qualities that diversity brings to its operations. Salaries are set individually at the University. In accordance with the National Archives of Sweden’s regulations, the University must archive application documents for two years after the appointment is filled. If you request that your documents are returned, they will be returned to you once the two years have passed. Otherwise, they will be destroyed. In connection to this recruitment, we have already decided which recruitment channels we should use. We therefore decline further contact with vendors, recruitment and staffing companies. Apply"
    }
  },
